{"type":"tool_execution_end","toolCallId":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367","toolName":"codemode","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 12027)\nTotal output lines: 16\n\n==> text 1/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\",\"content\":\"# Working on Tinkery\\n\\n**Read [docs/yohaku.md](docs/yohaku.md) first:** the constitution wins over guides,\\ndesigns and briefs. Only the operator amends it; report discrepancies, never edit it.\\n[README.md](README.md) describes shipped behavior.\\n\\n## Guardrails\\n\\n- Subtract first; say each thing once, plainly.\\n- Fewer, deeper modules, smaller interfaces. Check data once where it comes in, keep\\n  that guarantee in types, then just use it. Write one-off logic right where it's used\\n  instead of splitting it into a function ahead of time. Skip try/catch wherever you can.\\n- Check changeability: name the [architecture diagnostics](docs/design/architecture.md#design-score)\\n  moved. Module and `pub` comments state promises and refusals, not implementation;\\n  the build requires them.\\n- Preserve operator originals verbatim; readings are guesses.\\n  Keep the displayed, confirmed goal unchanged through saving and handoff.\\n- Navigation and copying never launch agents. Goal confirmation is neither seed\\n  confirmation nor implementation permission.\\n- Work from an issue on a branch and open a PR. Merge it yourself once the issue is approved\\n  (or the operator asked for the change), CI is green, there are no conflicts and a reviewer\\n  who did not write the change has checked it against the issue. The one exception is the\\n  [tink-sdlc bump](.github/workflows/tink-sdlc-bump.yml): it merges itself when CI passes and it\\n  is exactly tink-sdlc's own upgrade.\\n  If the operator asks for drafts only, create and merge nothing until they say so.\\n- Model and provider calls require an explicit budget. The default is zero.\\n  Eval commands are outside zero-call verification.\\n- Agent operators (Pi via herdr) always launch Tinkery with `--operator agent`.\\n- Never record real audio; voice tests use fake engines and recorders, never native addons.\\n- Use temporary TEST roots. Never write to `~/.local/share/seed-me` or\\n  `~/.local/share/tinkery`, or change operator sessions, installs, launcher, Pi,\\n  Pi Voice or the operator's Seed Me checkout.\\n- Fix a bug from its observed cause. After two failed fixes, add logging and collect\\n  observations; after three, stop and hand over a fresh brief: problem, goal, failed attempts.\\n- Read relevant tests and design before implementation; update changed decisions.\\n  Add regressions, prove important protections fail when reverted, then restore them.\\n  Tests assert what users observe through public interfaces; never add test-only access\\n  to private state. Drive Think through its input, not `Conversation`'s capped test accessors.\\n  Unowned ideas confer neither shipped behavior nor build permission.\\n- For agent-facing docs, use `writing-for-agents`: one rule per authoritative home,\\n  task detail behind pointers.\\n  Preserve safeguards and runnable commands; brevity is not permission to weaken them.\\n  `tests/test_documentation.py` checks links, headings, paths, options and reachability.\\n- Create each task's worktree at `~/dev/worktrees/<repo>/<branch>`, never inside a checkout\\n  or beside one.\\n- At rest, keep a clean `main` matching `origin/main` after a fetch, with no stashes,\\n  extra worktrees or leftover branches. Report open PRs instead of cleaning them up. Delete only\\n  merged branches (`git branch -d`); never force-delete branches, drop stashes or\\n  remove worktrees for cleanliness.\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nEach reply spends the operator's attention, so it must earn its place ([Yohaku](docs/yohaku.md)).\\nWrite in plain technical English, modelled on\\n[simple-english](https://github.com/arekhalpern/simple-english) (ASD-STE100):\\n\\n- Lead with what the operator must decide or do, then the result. Ask at most one question.\\n- Cut each line that does not change what the operator does next.\\n- Name the state of each claim: proposed, attempted, observed, checked or accepted.\\n  Say unknowns and failures plainly, with the next step.\\n- Quote the operator's words exactly, and mark your readings as guesses.\\n- Give one fact per sentence: at most 20 words for an instruction, 25 for a description.\\n  Use active voice and simple tenses.\\n- Write \\\"must\\\" for a requirement and \\\"can\\\" for a possibility, never \\\"should\\\", \\\"may\\\" or \\\"might\\\".\\n  Put a condition before its instruction: \\\"If CI fails, read the log.\\\"\\n- Keep one name for one thing. Write complete sentences with articles. No contractions,\\n  semicolons, arrow chains or filler that states no fact.\\n- Never change code, commands, identifiers or quoted errors.\\n\\n## Layout and design entry points\\n\\nRead only the relevant task contract from the\\n[documentation index](docs/README.md). The [architecture map](docs/design/architecture.md#modules-today)\\nshows module responsibilities.\\n\\n## Cold setup\\n\\nFresh Debian or Ubuntu clone with Bash and Git:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/cloud-setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe script installs missing prerequisites, stable Rust with rustfmt and Clippy, and Node.\\nIt prints the verification exports and can be rerun safely. It downloads only\\nprerequisite packages, not providers, microphones, native voice engines or speech models.\\nApt may need root or sudo.\\n\\nCI uses stable Rust: Ubuntu on pull requests, Ubuntu and macOS on `main`; use that updated stable for Clippy.\\nNever alter operator toolchains without approval. On macOS, provision Rust, Python 3\\nand Node separately (bootstrap refuses macOS). No external skill checkout or other\\ndeveloper's files are needed.\\n\\n## Full zero-call verification\\n\\n[The Rust test wrapper](scripts/rust-tests.py) runs the workspace in a fresh TEST HOME\\nand refuses default application storage.\\n\\nRun `python3 scripts/full-check.py` from the repository root after setup. Its shared\\nlaunch guard gives the exact block below a temporary TEST HOME; do not run binary\\nverification against the operator's HOME. Cold-clone CI runs the block when setup may change. [CI routing](scripts/ci-scope.py) and its\\n[regressions](tests/test_ci_scope.py) define prose-only exceptions and fail-closed gates;\\ncode changes require the full block. [The Python runner](scripts/python-tests.py) runs files\\nin parallel; with `TINKERY_TEST_TIER=pr`, as on pull requests, it skips only the slow journeys it\\nlists, which still run on every push to `main`.\\n\\nNode is only for `voice/*.test.mjs` and `pi/*.test.mjs`. Keep the invalid `TINKERY_NODE` for voice PTYs;\\nPython journeys use the real binary, stub providers and temporary roots.\\n\\n<!-- full-check:start -->\\n```bash\\nexport PATH=\\\"${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}/bin:$PWD/.cloud-tools/node/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nexport TINKERY_NODE=/tinkery-TEST-no-node\\nrustc +stable --version\\nnode --version\\npython3 --version\\ncargo +stable build --locked\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --home --snapshot\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --snapshot --no-color\\npython3 scripts/rust-tests.py\\nnode --test voice/*.test.mjs pi/*.test.mjs\\npython3 scripts/python-tests.py\\ncargo +stable fmt --all -- --check\\ncargo +stable clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\\n```\\n<!-- full-check:end -->\\n\\n[Try it](README.md#try-it) without the installed launcher. `--snapshot` prints\\n100×30 without terminal mode or provider calls; `NO_COLOR=1` or `--no-color` retain non-color\\ncues. [Terminal setup](docs/terminal-setup.md#launch) has isolated real-drafting instructions.\\n\\n## Work intake\\n\\nThe board is [tinkery](https://github.com/users/jon-devlapaz/projects/2); its automations add\\ntinkery issues and set Done on close. Cloud sessions can't reach it; use `gh` on the operator's Mac.\\n\\nFile an issue only for work that serves Tinkery, after searching for an open duplicate, using the\\n[task template](.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/task.yml) with one `area:` and one kind label. Show the draft\\nand publish only on the operator's word; publication is not approval. Parallel work needs approved,\\nnon-blocking items with separate `area:` labels.\\n\\nUnapproved work stays `needs-approval`. `approved` records scoped operator permission,\\nnever grants it; material scope changes return to `needs-approval`. Publication, design\\nacceptance, implementation, data use and budget are separate decisions.\\n\\nUse one branch and PR per approved slice; link the issue, stay within its scope and fill in the\\n[PR template](.github/pull_request_template.md). Close with\\ncompletion evidence or an explicit reason. Report Rust, Node and Python counts, PTY\\njourneys and sizes, failures and reversion probes separately from operator dogfood:\\nstubs prove mechanics, not real-dump goal quality.\\n\\n\\n<!-- AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n## SDLC Workspace\\n- Read `_system/SDLC.md` for setup, evidence boundaries, and recovery.\\n- Inspect `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status` before creating a run.\\n- Read `stages/<stage-name>/CONTEXT.md` before processing a stage.\\n- Keep factory references in `_shared/` unchanged during feature runs.\\n- Use separate worktrees or clones for code-writing runs.\\n- Stage skills: see the Skills section of the current stage's CONTEXT.md.\\n- Need a specialised skill mid-task? `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"` prints it on stdout; exit 1 means none fits, so continue without one.\\n<!-- End AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nOnly respond to the user using ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English and the Bottom Line Up Front (BLUF) approach. Use SBAR for handovers and status updates. These do not apply to code or when the user asks otherwise\\n\\n<!-- tink:rules begin skillset=build-skillset digest=1f3d3f07b904318d51e96afd8174591e82cabe8ec2da6668bf084aa4d683d4c1 -->\\nDiscipline rules for this phase (compiled by tink; do not edit by hand):\\n- principle-build-the-lever: Apply to any non-trivial work, not just bulk work: edits, migrations, analyses, checks. Build the tool that does it or proves it (codemod, script, generator, or a skill your subagents follow) instead of working by hand. The tool is the artifact a reviewer can rerun. (read: tink mount principle-build-the-lever --json --payload)\\n- unslop: Cut AI tells from any writing. Must always apply. (read: tink mount unslop --json --payload)\\n<!-- tink:rules end -->\\n\"}\n==> text 2/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\",\"content\":\"# SDLC workspace\\n\\nA small filesystem workflow: define a change, implement and verify it in an isolated checkout, then obtain independent release review. Requires Python 3.9+, Bash, and Git on a POSIX system. Native Windows is not supported. Set `require_tink: true` when project policy requires Tink integrity checks.\\n\\n**Boundaries, said once.** Receipts, digests, locks and reviewer names are local workflow records: they detect mistakes and changes, not tampering, and authenticate no one. Checks run real commands, never in a sandbox; review them before approval. Nothing here authorizes a release: trusted CI, protected branches and independent forge review do. Never invent a reviewer or approval, and never hand-edit generated records.\\n\\n## Start a run\\n\\nRun scripts from any working directory; paths resolve from the script location.\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --kind bug\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status fix-example\\n```\\n\\nThe default `light` profile creates `brief.md` (problem, acceptance criteria, approach, risks, verification) and `checklist.json`. A human picks `--profile full` for consequential architecture or policy changes; it creates intent, spec and plan. Only runs created by `new` are supported, and text approval tags are not evidence.\\n\\nTo build an approved GitHub issue, let the issue be the definition:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --issue https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/issues/NUMBER\\n```\\n\\nIt reads the issue once with `gh` and calls no model. `brief.md` holds the issue's title, address, body hash and body unchanged; `checklist.json` holds one attested item for each line under the issue's `Done when` heading, unchanged. The `Done when` section must hold only top-level `-` or `*` lines, one line each, so no approved word is cut or dropped. It refuses a closed issue, an issue without the `approved` label, a Done-when section with no lines or any other content, an unreadable issue and `--profile full`, and then creates no run. `run.json` keeps the issue's address and body hash as a record. It records no decision: stage 3 still needs the human decision.\\n\\nWithout a confirmed seed, review the checklist commands and offer a dry run before definition approval (seed-bound runs use the [seed definition comparison](#seed-definition-comparison)):\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py dry-run fix-example\\n```\\n\\nIt runs every checklist `check` in the repository root with its timeout, replacing `runs/<slug>/checklist-dry-run.log` with launch errors, timeouts and nonzero exits per item. No check process survives its check: each starts in a new session, and on normal exit or timeout the rest of its process group is killed and reaped. Tell an expected reproduction failure from a broken command or environment (such as an unsuitable `TMPDIR`), fix the latter and rerun. The dry run records no decision, mark or receipt, does not enforce a new approval prerequisite, and does not replace `verify`, which still requires current approval and passing checks.\\n\\nAfter the human accepts the brief, record the actual review reference:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py decide fix-example 3 approved \\\\\\n  --reviewer 'Reviewer name' --source 'Actual review URL or decision reference' \\\\\\n  --reason 'Accepted scope, approach, and acceptance criteria'\\n```\\n\\nLight runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3: `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. Full runs record stages 1, 2 and 3 in order. `changes-requested` records rejection with the same command. Decisions bind their artifacts and contract inputs; edits stale them.\\n\\n`decide` and seed-judge `mark` are human-only: they refuse a reviewer starting with `Agent`, containing `not human`, or an agent actor (`--actor`, or run metadata). Older agent records stay readable, labelled `agent-recorded`, and never count as human acceptance.\\n\\n## Checklist\\n\\n`runs/<slug>/checklist.json` holds item definitions only: `{\\\"schema\\\":1,\\\"items\\\":[{\\\"id\\\",\\\"description\\\",\\\"verify\\\",\\\"check\\\"?}]}`. They are approved scope: stage 3 cannot be approved with zero or invalid items, and changing them stales that approval. Never add status fields. If the file is deleted, status reads MISSING and verification refuses until it is restored from Git.\\n\\n- **Checked items** carry `check`: `{\\\"argv\\\":[...],\\\"timeout_seconds\\\":N}`. `verify` runs them in the repository root after the configured checks, logging to `test-log.md`; a failure fails verification (`Checklist check failed: <id>`). A pass proves the item, so `mark` is refused for it.\\n- **Attested items** have no `check`: `sdlc.py mark <run> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>` appends a receipt under `marks/` (needs current stage 3 approval; latest wins; `verify` requires `passed`). Verification binds each latest mark, so mark first, then verify. Attestations are self-reported claims for review to challenge. Status flags a mark made on an older candidate; re-check it if the change affects it.\\n\\nWhen every command passes but attestations are missing, `verify` exits nonzero with `Checklist incomplete`, the mark commands to run, and `result: awaiting-attestation` with `pending_attestations`. That is machine proof only, not a pass. It stays current while its proof inputs and log match (evidence-only commits keep it); a mark or changed input retires it. An item a person marked `failed` makes the result `failed`. Record the actual observations, then rerun `verify`.\\n\\n### Locked seed acceptance checks\\n\\nAt stage-1 intake, use the explicitly supplied session and a trusted Tinkery executable:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 1 \\\\\\n  --seed-contract <session>/seed-contract.md \\\\\\n  --tinkery <path>/tinkery\\n```\\n\\nTinkery is the single seed verifier. Intake runs exactly `PATH seed status --json DIR` and `PATH seed checks DIR` (DIR is the seed's session directory) and reads status again to detect changes. Tinkery verifies human v2 and agent v3 confirmations against the seed; intake accepts v3 only with `operator: agent` and an agent `actor`, which records agent confirmation, never human confirmation. Missing executables, failed commands, malformed output, simulated sessions, missing confirmation and changed bindings refuse before any write. Supply trusted toolchain code, never a seed-provided script. Verification proves content binding, not operator identity or implementation approval.\\n\\nIntake copies the confirmed seed and its records into locked `runs/<slug>/seed-checks.json` items with reserved `seed-N` IDs; run metadata keeps the confirmation receipt and the original definitions' digest. Seed Markdown is never read as checks. Imported items are **LOCKED, seed-confirmed** definitions, distinct from the worker's `checklist.json`. Never silently weaken or remove acceptance: such edits are departures. Deleting the file or changing the run-local seed refuses verification. Later edits to the original session do not reach the run-local snapshot; to rebind, run verified intake again; rebinding replaces activations, and prior evidence stays in Git.\\n\\n#### Seed definition comparison\\n\\nStage 3 compares effective checks with the intake baseline. With no added, removed or changed checks, and no activation other than the confirmed proposal, it passes without another human question and records `matches confirmed seed (sha256: …)` as evidence, not a human decision. This is structural, not a claim about the brief or permission to implement. An explicit human `changes-requested` stays blocking until a later human decision supersedes it.\\n\\nAny departure blocks automatic passage. Status lists each changed field, removed or added check, added worker item and non-proposal activation, and asks only `Accept these departures from the confirmed seed?`. `decide … 3` records the human answer bound to those exact inputs. Full-profile stages 1 and 2 keep their own reviews. Legacy bindings without a stored baseline compare against the intake digest; if their definitions changed, rebind rather than invent a baseline.\\n\\nSeed record kinds:\\n\\n- **`cmd`** runs verbatim through `bash -c` with a 120-second timeout, or `seed_timeout_seconds` from `_system/verification.json`; `cwd` is relative to the checkout (absolute paths, traversal, backslashes, drive letters, NUL and CR/LF refused). Failures and timeouts fail `verify` even when all worker checks pass. **Passing output proves a `cmd` or activated proposal entirely; no `seed-N-expect` mark is owed.** `expect` is descriptive context, not a matcher.\\n- **`judge`** (prose or taste) needs a manual human attestation, judged by `how_to_judge` or else the statement, never an automated pass. Record only the actual operator's observation:\\n\\n  ```sh\\n  python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py mark <slug> seed-1 passed \\\\\\n    --reviewer 'Actual operator' --source 'Actual observation reference' \\\\\\n    --evidence 'Observed output and why it satisfies the seed expectation'\\n  ```\\n\\n  Evidence already used for another item is refused unless `--covers-all` says the operator meant it for each. Mark `unknown` when the observation does not address the expectation; `unknown` blocks verification. Marks need current definition approval and bind the exact item.\\n- **`provisional`** names its prerequisite in `missing`; `cmd` is an optional proposal. It stays pending and nonblocking, never proven, while the prerequisite is absent. At implementation time, inspect the prerequisite. **Once it exists**, add only an `activation` object to that item in `seed-checks.json`, keeping the proposal intact:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\\"activation\\\": {\\\"cmd\\\":…2027 tokens truncated…ython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py verify`.\\n- The reviewer works in a separate checkout, writes `runs/<slug>/05-deploy/output/REVIEW-findings.md`,\\n  and changes no code or evidence. The candidate's owner brings the findings to the PR branch.\\n\\n## Pinned build steps\\n\\n`stage` prepares a checkout and prints a prompt; it never starts an agent. For an explicitly\\nauthorized build agent, open stage 3, then, from that checkout, take these deliberate steps one at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build prepare \\\\\\n  --report /absolute/run-notes/build-report.md \\\\\\n  --model provider/exact-model-id --launcher /absolute/trusted-adapter [--max-responses N]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build show\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build launch\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build resume\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete --stopped-by operator  # or cap\\n```\\n\\n- `prepare` writes an immutable `runs/<run>/steps/<name>.json` binding the checkout, the exact model,\\n  the launcher's path and SHA-256, and a fresh absolute report path whose parent exists. It starts nothing.\\n  It refuses existing names or reports, relative paths, model aliases, and a run branch checked out\\n  elsewhere. Contracts are not portable: prepare a new step in a new checkout.\\n- `--max-responses N` caps responses; an adapter that cannot enforce it refuses, so a cap is never ignored.\\n- `launch` and `resume` recheck every definition gate and resolve the exact model offline first. The\\n  prompt names the approved inputs, `stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md` and the report path; limits writes to\\n  the checkout and report; tells the worker to commit before ending each turn; and forbids `sdlc.py step`, `stage` and `decide`. Inside the session\\n  `TINK_SDLC_STEP_SESSION=1` makes `step` refuse everything but `show`. Resume keeps the same session.\\n- `complete` needs a nonempty report at the exact path and the adapter's offline confirmation that the\\n  agent's latest turn ended on the pin. A paused, failed or running agent leaves the step `active`.\\n  `--stopped-by operator|cap` ends the step's process group and records it `stopped`, never as an ended turn.\\n- `show` adds `observed_turn` (`ended`, `not ended` or `unknown`) from an offline probe.\\n- A crash or failed admission leaves the step `uncertain`, never retried. Inspect the real session and\\n  reconcile it before preparing another step; never hand-edit the contract.\\n\\nA report is not proof of passing checks, authorship or approval; `verify` and forge gates stay independent.\\n\\nBundled adapters (their module comments state what each promises and refuses):\\n\\n| Adapter | Pin | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `_system/scripts/pi-launcher` | `provider/model-id:thinking`, e.g. `openai-codex/gpt-5.6-luna:low` | The model must be in Pi's local catalog (run `pi update --models` yourself) with a stored credential. Enforces `--max-responses`. |\\n\\nResume pinned steps only through `step … resume`: pi-herdr's resume drops the pin. Another launcher\\nmust implement the [launcher protocol](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/launcher-protocol.md)\\nand pass offline-resolution tests before it gets a live-call budget.\\n\\n## Stage skills\\n\\nEach stage has a skillset pin committed at `.tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json`: 01-plan `planning-skillset`, 02-design `design-skillset`, 03-build `build-skillset`, 04-test `testing-skillset`, 05-deploy `deployment-skillset`, 06-maintain `maintenance-skillset`. A pin selects exact upstream code, so review a pin change like a lockfile change.\\n\\n```sh\\ntink library fetch .tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json    # once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin\\ntink use <name>-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/<stage-dir>  # at stage open, then start a NEW session\\ntink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`required` lists the disciplines `tink use` compiles into `AGENTS.md` at stage open. Capability skills come only through `tink-route` for genuine gaps. It searches the whole library (`--anywhere`, the default; `--skillset NAME` restricts it, and exit 1 may then add `Hint: <skill> fits but is on another shelf`). Exit 1 or 2 means continue without a skill. Keep its receipt with the run and read the delivered skill before relying on it. `tink-route` mounts into the git-ignored `.tink/.active` and changes no project skills.\\n\\nCommit `.tink/skills.toml` and `.tink/skills.lock` when Tink creates them through an authorized operation; restore them into each worktree through Tink, never by symlink. Run `tink` mutations through the wrapper, which serializes them on this host and rejects symlinked skill state:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py skills tink -- skill check\\n```\\n\\nTinkery's verified confirmed seed contract is the input to 01-plan (bind it as in [Locked seed acceptance checks](#locked-seed-acceptance-checks)). `tink use` and `tink-route` are optional; runs work without them.\\n\\n## Recovery and release\\n\\nRuns have one writer at a time; a busy or interrupted operation fails with its lock path. After a crash, confirm no process owns the operation, remove the lock directory and rerun.\\n\\nOpen the PR once the candidate is committed and verification is current (end of stage 4; `stage <slug> 5` enforces it). Delivery and merge authority belong to `stages/05-deploy/CONTEXT.md`. Keep deployed revision, result and rollback references in the deployment system.\\n\\nRecord the run's ending:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py pr <run> <PR URL>   # needs current verification; one PR per run\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py label <run> --kind correction|none|accepted|rejected --words TEXT \\\\\\n  --reviewer NAME --source REF [--about FILE|outside] [--consent local|shareable]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py close <run> --outcome merged --merge <full commit>   # or --outcome abandoned\\n```\\n\\nPut `Tink-Run: <run_id>` in the PR description. Labels are human-only (refused inside a build step session), keep the words verbatim, and chain to each other by digest; `none` records no corrections and `outside` a correction outside the run's records. `close` needs a label (and, when merged, the recorded PR); a run closes once. While the run builds in another checkout, these refuse and name it. Closing in a linked worktree copies the run's records into the main checkout's `runs/<run>/` (refusing if that folder has uncommitted changes or another run); commit them there.\\n\\nMaintenance is optional intake. Enable it only with metric thresholds, deduplication, cooldowns and run limits; alerts create drafts for triage, never approvals.\\n\\n## Machine interface (API 1)\\n\\nClients read `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py capabilities --json` and `status [<run>] --json` from the selected checkout's runtime, check `protocol`, `api_version` and capabilities first, and fail closed on unknown states, never falling back to the human CLI output. The field contract is in [references/api-1.md](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/api-1.md).\\n\\n## Maintaining the scaffold\\n\\n`python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py walk [--json]` is a read-only structural check that a memoryless agent can orient from the files: router size, resolving pointers, stage contracts naming Inputs, Output and a human Gate, per-stage context size, skillset pins, `status` for every run, and current `tink:rules` blocks. A real walk by a fresh agent stays the test.\\n\\nPreview, then apply an upgrade (`_system/scaffold.json` is the trust baseline):\\n\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade --check\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade\\n\\nUnmodified managed files are updated, restored or removed; project-owned files (`_system/verification.json`, `.tink/skillsets/*.json`) are never overwritten. A customized managed file blocks the upgrade and is listed with a `diff`: merge by hand, or commit and rerun with `--overwrite-customized`. Only the `AGENTS.md` router block is replaced; `runs/` is never modified, but changed stage 1-3 contracts stale existing approvals. Downgrades are refused, and an interrupted upgrade is safe to rerun. A killed install can leave a partial one: inspect it and its lock, confirm no writer remains, and recover through a reviewed migration, never a forced overwrite. Never update factory files during an active feature run. Afterward run `walk`.\\n\\nTemplates live in `_shared/` (`brief-template.md` is the light-profile edit surface); run outputs go under `runs/`. Read the active contract and its immediate inputs, not the whole factory.\\n\"}\n==> text 3/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\",\"content\":\"# Stage 03: Implement\\n\\nInputs: current approved spec for full runs, or combined brief for light runs. A seed-bound run's\\nconfirmed `seed-contract.md` and locked checks define it; its `brief.md` may stay the template.\\nOutput: `runs/<slug>/03-build/output/plan.md` for full runs, the approved checklist definitions\\nin `runs/<slug>/checklist.json` for both profiles, and code in an isolated worktree/clone.\\nItems should carry a `check` whenever an automated proof exists; `verify` runs it and no mark is needed.\\nWhen a pending provisional seed check's prerequisite exists, follow the guide's [provisional activation step](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison) and report the activation; a command other than the confirmed proposal needs operator stage-3 approval.\\n\\nGate: inspect code and follow the stage 3 gate before implementation; for seed-bound\\nruns use the guide's [seed definition comparison](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison).\\nFor full runs, write the plan first. Light runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3 (`sdlc.py decide <run> 3 ...`): the approved `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. An explicit user instruction to execute a reviewed proposal is\\nimplementation authority; never fabricate separate role approvals.\\nEach concurrent writer needs its own checkout. Use the serialized skill wrapper\\nin `_system/SDLC.md`; no shared writable skill symlinks or automatic lockfile rewrites.\\n\\nFor bug fixes, reproduce the expected failure first, obtain independent acceptance,\\nand record protected test inputs with `sdlc.py lock-tests`. Implement and verify in\\na loop with stage 04. Update the plan when scope changes and renew stale decisions.\\nMark items only with `sdlc.py mark <slug> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>`; never hand-edit receipts.\\nFor launcher-managed build agents, use the guide's [pinned build steps](../../_system/SDLC.md#pinned-build-steps).\\nRead the supplied step contract, write the handoff at its exact absolute report path,\\nand retain that contract and model pin on resume. A missing report refuses step completion.\\n\\n## Skills\\n\\nSkillset: `build-skillset` (pin: `.tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`).\\n- Once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin (it selects exact upstream code):\\n  `tink library fetch .tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`\\n- At stage open, compile the required disciplines, then start a NEW session so\\n  `AGENTS.md` is re-read: `tink use build-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/03-build`\\n  The launcher does this for you: `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 3`.\\n- For a capability gap: `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"`\\n  (searches the whole library; prints the skill on stdout. Exit 1 means nothing fits; exit 1 or 2 means\\n  continue without a skill).\\n\\nStage skills (always for this stage; `tink use` compiles the same set):\\n- `principle-build-the-lever`: Non-trivial work: build the script or tool that does or proves it, so a reviewer can rerun it, instead of doing it by hand.\\n- `unslop`: Any prose you write (docs, commit messages, replies): cut AI tells and filler before you send it.\\nWhen one of these applies, read that skill in full before acting on it. In the handoff note, name each skill that changed a decision and the decision it changed.\\n\"}\n==> text 4/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\",\"content\":\"# Change brief\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/864\\nIssue body sha256: 29f447833355967617805c1fc76789f53c35598279aa8b5a4f7ba921cf6aa3b1\\nTitle: Tend: Keep a raw project thought before any goal is confirmed\\n\\nThe approved issue below is this run's brief, unchanged. Each Done-when line is one item in `checklist.json`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Primary area\\n\\ntend\\n\\n### Kind\\n\\nenhancement\\n\\n### Goal\\n\\nThe operator needs one place per project to keep ideas, goals and thoughts, including thoughts that are not yet goals.\\nSaving a thought must not claim that the operator confirmed a goal.\\n\\n### Evidence\\n\\nOperator words, 2026-11-15, exact:\\n\\n> i just want a place that i can saturate my ideas/goals and thoughts in that are project specific and I can allow LLM to find surfaced connections, refactoring and determination if a goal(s) are ready for shaping. thats my main goal. I think idempotency is an idea that is relevant\\n\\n> think of an entry to tend be a neuron, and with enough neurons a action potential activates over those neuron connections, and then its refined and insulated better through shape like myelination and then then the action is tink-sdlc which is like the behavior..\\n\\nAsked whether Tend must keep a raw thought before any goal is confirmed from it, the operator answered: \\\"Yes, raw thoughts\\\".\\n\\nObserved in code at `04c2bc4`:\\n\\n- Tend lists saved sessions only. Think exits without confirmation leave no record (`docs/design/think.md`, Think record).\\n- `tinkery seed new` saves any text without Think or a model call, but records it as a confirmed goal (`src/shape/command.rs`, `new_goal`).\\n- That command writes no repository attribution. One active goal with unknown attribution makes Find connections refuse\\n  for every repository (`src/tend/connection.rs`, `snapshot_with`).\\n\\nProposal, not observation: a thought is a separate kind of entry, not a goal with a weaker label.\\nNot established: that more entries make connection discovery useful. The one real trial gave a false alarm.\\n\\n### Scope and boundaries\\n\\nOne outcome: the operator saves exact words as a thought in the current repository's garden, sees it there after restart,\\nand later turns it into a goal only through the existing goal confirmation.\\n\\nChanges:\\n\\n- A key in Tend's repository garden opens one exact-word editor. No Think conversation is needed.\\n- One explicit save of exact words, with the repository shown before saving. No model call.\\n- Thoughts appear in the repository garden, visibly distinct from goals with and without colour.\\n- A thought records where it was saved. It never has unknown attribution.\\n\\nStays unchanged:\\n\\n- Goal confirmation, seed confirmation and implementation permission. A thought is none of them.\\n- Shape accepts confirmed goals only. A thought cannot open Shape.\\n- Find connections input, its 20-goal and 32 KiB bounds, the saved correction and all saved-goal formats.\\n  Thoughts do not enter a provider request in this slice.\\n- No automatic interpretation, grouping, merging, readiness judgment, wiki or background work.\\n- No editing or deletion of a saved thought in this slice.\\n\\nSubtract first: reuse the exact-word editor, the repository boundary, the private atomic write and the garden view.\\nIf the build needs a new storage service, a session-format migration or a change to the shared goal saver, stop and report.\\n\\n### Done when\\n\\n- [ ] From the repository garden, the operator saves a thought with one disclosed action, and the saved words equal the typed words byte for byte.\\n- [ ] After restart, the thought is listed in that repository's garden, labelled as a thought and not as a goal.\\n- [ ] A saved thought has no goal confirmation record, cannot open Shape, and is absent from the Find connections request.\\n- [ ] Find connections still runs in a repository that holds thoughts. A thought never causes incomplete discovery.\\n- [ ] Saving the same exact words twice in one repository results in one entry, and the screen says so.\\n- [ ] Turning a thought into a goal uses the existing goal confirmation, keeps the thought's words as the original, and leaves the thought on record.\\n- [ ] Terminal journeys at 80×24 and 100×30 with colour off cover save, restart, duplicate save, cancel and an unwritable store, with temporary TEST roots and no provider.\\n- [ ] Reversion probes fail when a thought is recorded as a confirmed goal, when saved words differ from typed words, and when a thought reaches a provider request.\\n\\n### Context and dependencies\\n\\n- [Yohaku](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/yohaku.md), Articles 1 and 2.\\n- [Tend and handoff](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/shape-and-handoff.md#tend-garden-and-detail) and [Think](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/think.md).\\n- #860 and #862 are merged. This slice does not change them.\\n- #852 covers pruning and restoring goals. It is related and not a blocker.\\n- Later, separate slices: thoughts as Find connections input, volume above 20 entries, refactoring proposals,\\n  readiness judgments and dismissal that persists. None is part of this issue.\\n\\n### Open decisions and permissions\\n\\nDecided by the operator on 2026-11-15: a thought enters through a key in Tend's garden. Operator words: \\\"Key in Tend garden\\\".\\nThe key name, its label and its place in the key bar remain design details for review.\\n\\nDefaults proposed by the agent and accepted by the operator for this slice on 2026-11-15:\\n\\n1. A saved thought cannot be edited or removed in this slice. Pruning is separate work (#852).\\n2. \\\"Project\\\" means the launch repository, as goals use today.\\n3. A duplicate means the same exact bytes in the same repository. Whitespace and case differences make a different thought.\\n\\nEach default can change in a later slice. No other decision is open for this slice.\\n\\nThis issue records a design request. Publication, implementation, provider budget and real-data trials each need separate operator approval.\\n\\n\"}\n==> text 5/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"d50e7a87-1232-43e5-a29a-0cbf2512c1ab\\\",\\n  \\\"checkout\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought\\\",\\n  \\\"report\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/output/handoff.md\\\",\\n  \\\"model\\\": \\\"openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol:medium\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/_system/scripts/pi-launcher\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher_sha256\\\": \\\"d0c05547c00cf7882c0fef3d8e89d786fdd2a488b76413aa9159cd61183a6763\\\",\\n  \\\"state\\\": \\\"active\\\",\\n  \\\"session\\\": \\\"tsdlc-4e1822e7fd164f8b825f6555621346b6\\\",\\n  \\\"prepared_ns\\\": 1794731465469500000,\\n  \\\"max_responses\\\": 250,\\n  \\\"process_group\\\": 32154\\n}\\n\"}\n==> text 6/7 <==\n{\"output\":\" M AGENTS.md\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n7c134de stage 3 open: issue-864-tend-thought (approved artifacts and receipts)\\n04c2bc4 Merge pull request #863 from jon-devlapaz/codex/issue-862-tend-correction\\nba6606f Record independent review of Linux test repair\\ndade851 docs: record verified Linux correction test fix\\nc1728c6 test(tend): observe clear frame before large correction paste\\nissue-860-tend-connection\\nissue-862-tend-correction\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit_code\":0,\"wall_time_seconds\":0.1}\n==> text 7/7 <==\nCreated #1: Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation (pending)\nCreated #2: Implement the approved slice and regression checks (pending)\nCreated #3: Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes (pending)\n\n[Full output: /var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt (read with offset/limit)]"}],"details":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.3395000000000437},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.0922499999996944},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.6723750000001019},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.624958999998853},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.575790999999299},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","args":"{\"command\":\"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status\",\"timeout\":30}","status":"ok","durationMs":117.500082999999},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/7","name":"todo","args":"{\"action\":\"create\",\"tasks\":[{\"subject\":\"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation\"},{\"subject\":\"Implement the approved slice and regression checks\"},{\"subject\":\"Run checks, write han...","status":"ok","durationMs":1.6686250000002474}],"fullOutputPath":"/var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt"}},"isError":false,"durationMs":190}
{"type":"message_start","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367","toolName":"codemode","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 12027)\nTotal output lines: 16\n\n==> text 1/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\",\"content\":\"# Working on Tinkery\\n\\n**Read [docs/yohaku.md](docs/yohaku.md) first:** the constitution wins over guides,\\ndesigns and briefs. Only the operator amends it; report discrepancies, never edit it.\\n[README.md](README.md) describes shipped behavior.\\n\\n## Guardrails\\n\\n- Subtract first; say each thing once, plainly.\\n- Fewer, deeper modules, smaller interfaces. Check data once where it comes in, keep\\n  that guarantee in types, then just use it. Write one-off logic right where it's used\\n  instead of splitting it into a function ahead of time. Skip try/catch wherever you can.\\n- Check changeability: name the [architecture diagnostics](docs/design/architecture.md#design-score)\\n  moved. Module and `pub` comments state promises and refusals, not implementation;\\n  the build requires them.\\n- Preserve operator originals verbatim; readings are guesses.\\n  Keep the displayed, confirmed goal unchanged through saving and handoff.\\n- Navigation and copying never launch agents. Goal confirmation is neither seed\\n  confirmation nor implementation permission.\\n- Work from an issue on a branch and open a PR. Merge it yourself once the issue is approved\\n  (or the operator asked for the change), CI is green, there are no conflicts and a reviewer\\n  who did not write the change has checked it against the issue. The one exception is the\\n  [tink-sdlc bump](.github/workflows/tink-sdlc-bump.yml): it merges itself when CI passes and it\\n  is exactly tink-sdlc's own upgrade.\\n  If the operator asks for drafts only, create and merge nothing until they say so.\\n- Model and provider calls require an explicit budget. The default is zero.\\n  Eval commands are outside zero-call verification.\\n- Agent operators (Pi via herdr) always launch Tinkery with `--operator agent`.\\n- Never record real audio; voice tests use fake engines and recorders, never native addons.\\n- Use temporary TEST roots. Never write to `~/.local/share/seed-me` or\\n  `~/.local/share/tinkery`, or change operator sessions, installs, launcher, Pi,\\n  Pi Voice or the operator's Seed Me checkout.\\n- Fix a bug from its observed cause. After two failed fixes, add logging and collect\\n  observations; after three, stop and hand over a fresh brief: problem, goal, failed attempts.\\n- Read relevant tests and design before implementation; update changed decisions.\\n  Add regressions, prove important protections fail when reverted, then restore them.\\n  Tests assert what users observe through public interfaces; never add test-only access\\n  to private state. Drive Think through its input, not `Conversation`'s capped test accessors.\\n  Unowned ideas confer neither shipped behavior nor build permission.\\n- For agent-facing docs, use `writing-for-agents`: one rule per authoritative home,\\n  task detail behind pointers.\\n  Preserve safeguards and runnable commands; brevity is not permission to weaken them.\\n  `tests/test_documentation.py` checks links, headings, paths, options and reachability.\\n- Create each task's worktree at `~/dev/worktrees/<repo>/<branch>`, never inside a checkout\\n  or beside one.\\n- At rest, keep a clean `main` matching `origin/main` after a fetch, with no stashes,\\n  extra worktrees or leftover branches. Report open PRs instead of cleaning them up. Delete only\\n  merged branches (`git branch -d`); never force-delete branches, drop stashes or\\n  remove worktrees for cleanliness.\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nEach reply spends the operator's attention, so it must earn its place ([Yohaku](docs/yohaku.md)).\\nWrite in plain technical English, modelled on\\n[simple-english](https://github.com/arekhalpern/simple-english) (ASD-STE100):\\n\\n- Lead with what the operator must decide or do, then the result. Ask at most one question.\\n- Cut each line that does not change what the operator does next.\\n- Name the state of each claim: proposed, attempted, observed, checked or accepted.\\n  Say unknowns and failures plainly, with the next step.\\n- Quote the operator's words exactly, and mark your readings as guesses.\\n- Give one fact per sentence: at most 20 words for an instruction, 25 for a description.\\n  Use active voice and simple tenses.\\n- Write \\\"must\\\" for a requirement and \\\"can\\\" for a possibility, never \\\"should\\\", \\\"may\\\" or \\\"might\\\".\\n  Put a condition before its instruction: \\\"If CI fails, read the log.\\\"\\n- Keep one name for one thing. Write complete sentences with articles. No contractions,\\n  semicolons, arrow chains or filler that states no fact.\\n- Never change code, commands, identifiers or quoted errors.\\n\\n## Layout and design entry points\\n\\nRead only the relevant task contract from the\\n[documentation index](docs/README.md). The [architecture map](docs/design/architecture.md#modules-today)\\nshows module responsibilities.\\n\\n## Cold setup\\n\\nFresh Debian or Ubuntu clone with Bash and Git:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/cloud-setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe script installs missing prerequisites, stable Rust with rustfmt and Clippy, and Node.\\nIt prints the verification exports and can be rerun safely. It downloads only\\nprerequisite packages, not providers, microphones, native voice engines or speech models.\\nApt may need root or sudo.\\n\\nCI uses stable Rust: Ubuntu on pull requests, Ubuntu and macOS on `main`; use that updated stable for Clippy.\\nNever alter operator toolchains without approval. On macOS, provision Rust, Python 3\\nand Node separately (bootstrap refuses macOS). No external skill checkout or other\\ndeveloper's files are needed.\\n\\n## Full zero-call verification\\n\\n[The Rust test wrapper](scripts/rust-tests.py) runs the workspace in a fresh TEST HOME\\nand refuses default application storage.\\n\\nRun `python3 scripts/full-check.py` from the repository root after setup. Its shared\\nlaunch guard gives the exact block below a temporary TEST HOME; do not run binary\\nverification against the operator's HOME. Cold-clone CI runs the block when setup may change. [CI routing](scripts/ci-scope.py) and its\\n[regressions](tests/test_ci_scope.py) define prose-only exceptions and fail-closed gates;\\ncode changes require the full block. [The Python runner](scripts/python-tests.py) runs files\\nin parallel; with `TINKERY_TEST_TIER=pr`, as on pull requests, it skips only the slow journeys it\\nlists, which still run on every push to `main`.\\n\\nNode is only for `voice/*.test.mjs` and `pi/*.test.mjs`. Keep the invalid `TINKERY_NODE` for voice PTYs;\\nPython journeys use the real binary, stub providers and temporary roots.\\n\\n<!-- full-check:start -->\\n```bash\\nexport PATH=\\\"${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}/bin:$PWD/.cloud-tools/node/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nexport TINKERY_NODE=/tinkery-TEST-no-node\\nrustc +stable --version\\nnode --version\\npython3 --version\\ncargo +stable build --locked\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --home --snapshot\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --snapshot --no-color\\npython3 scripts/rust-tests.py\\nnode --test voice/*.test.mjs pi/*.test.mjs\\npython3 scripts/python-tests.py\\ncargo +stable fmt --all -- --check\\ncargo +stable clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\\n```\\n<!-- full-check:end -->\\n\\n[Try it](README.md#try-it) without the installed launcher. `--snapshot` prints\\n100×30 without terminal mode or provider calls; `NO_COLOR=1` or `--no-color` retain non-color\\ncues. [Terminal setup](docs/terminal-setup.md#launch) has isolated real-drafting instructions.\\n\\n## Work intake\\n\\nThe board is [tinkery](https://github.com/users/jon-devlapaz/projects/2); its automations add\\ntinkery issues and set Done on close. Cloud sessions can't reach it; use `gh` on the operator's Mac.\\n\\nFile an issue only for work that serves Tinkery, after searching for an open duplicate, using the\\n[task template](.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/task.yml) with one `area:` and one kind label. Show the draft\\nand publish only on the operator's word; publication is not approval. Parallel work needs approved,\\nnon-blocking items with separate `area:` labels.\\n\\nUnapproved work stays `needs-approval`. `approved` records scoped operator permission,\\nnever grants it; material scope changes return to `needs-approval`. Publication, design\\nacceptance, implementation, data use and budget are separate decisions.\\n\\nUse one branch and PR per approved slice; link the issue, stay within its scope and fill in the\\n[PR template](.github/pull_request_template.md). Close with\\ncompletion evidence or an explicit reason. Report Rust, Node and Python counts, PTY\\njourneys and sizes, failures and reversion probes separately from operator dogfood:\\nstubs prove mechanics, not real-dump goal quality.\\n\\n\\n<!-- AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n## SDLC Workspace\\n- Read `_system/SDLC.md` for setup, evidence boundaries, and recovery.\\n- Inspect `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status` before creating a run.\\n- Read `stages/<stage-name>/CONTEXT.md` before processing a stage.\\n- Keep factory references in `_shared/` unchanged during feature runs.\\n- Use separate worktrees or clones for code-writing runs.\\n- Stage skills: see the Skills section of the current stage's CONTEXT.md.\\n- Need a specialised skill mid-task? `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"` prints it on stdout; exit 1 means none fits, so continue without one.\\n<!-- End AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nOnly respond to the user using ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English and the Bottom Line Up Front (BLUF) approach. Use SBAR for handovers and status updates. These do not apply to code or when the user asks otherwise\\n\\n<!-- tink:rules begin skillset=build-skillset digest=1f3d3f07b904318d51e96afd8174591e82cabe8ec2da6668bf084aa4d683d4c1 -->\\nDiscipline rules for this phase (compiled by tink; do not edit by hand):\\n- principle-build-the-lever: Apply to any non-trivial work, not just bulk work: edits, migrations, analyses, checks. Build the tool that does it or proves it (codemod, script, generator, or a skill your subagents follow) instead of working by hand. The tool is the artifact a reviewer can rerun. (read: tink mount principle-build-the-lever --json --payload)\\n- unslop: Cut AI tells from any writing. Must always apply. (read: tink mount unslop --json --payload)\\n<!-- tink:rules end -->\\n\"}\n==> text 2/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\",\"content\":\"# SDLC workspace\\n\\nA small filesystem workflow: define a change, implement and verify it in an isolated checkout, then obtain independent release review. Requires Python 3.9+, Bash, and Git on a POSIX system. Native Windows is not supported. Set `require_tink: true` when project policy requires Tink integrity checks.\\n\\n**Boundaries, said once.** Receipts, digests, locks and reviewer names are local workflow records: they detect mistakes and changes, not tampering, and authenticate no one. Checks run real commands, never in a sandbox; review them before approval. Nothing here authorizes a release: trusted CI, protected branches and independent forge review do. Never invent a reviewer or approval, and never hand-edit generated records.\\n\\n## Start a run\\n\\nRun scripts from any working directory; paths resolve from the script location.\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --kind bug\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status fix-example\\n```\\n\\nThe default `light` profile creates `brief.md` (problem, acceptance criteria, approach, risks, verification) and `checklist.json`. A human picks `--profile full` for consequential architecture or policy changes; it creates intent, spec and plan. Only runs created by `new` are supported, and text approval tags are not evidence.\\n\\nTo build an approved GitHub issue, let the issue be the definition:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --issue https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/issues/NUMBER\\n```\\n\\nIt reads the issue once with `gh` and calls no model. `brief.md` holds the issue's title, address, body hash and body unchanged; `checklist.json` holds one attested item for each line under the issue's `Done when` heading, unchanged. The `Done when` section must hold only top-level `-` or `*` lines, one line each, so no approved word is cut or dropped. It refuses a closed issue, an issue without the `approved` label, a Done-when section with no lines or any other content, an unreadable issue and `--profile full`, and then creates no run. `run.json` keeps the issue's address and body hash as a record. It records no decision: stage 3 still needs the human decision.\\n\\nWithout a confirmed seed, review the checklist commands and offer a dry run before definition approval (seed-bound runs use the [seed definition comparison](#seed-definition-comparison)):\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py dry-run fix-example\\n```\\n\\nIt runs every checklist `check` in the repository root with its timeout, replacing `runs/<slug>/checklist-dry-run.log` with launch errors, timeouts and nonzero exits per item. No check process survives its check: each starts in a new session, and on normal exit or timeout the rest of its process group is killed and reaped. Tell an expected reproduction failure from a broken command or environment (such as an unsuitable `TMPDIR`), fix the latter and rerun. The dry run records no decision, mark or receipt, does not enforce a new approval prerequisite, and does not replace `verify`, which still requires current approval and passing checks.\\n\\nAfter the human accepts the brief, record the actual review reference:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py decide fix-example 3 approved \\\\\\n  --reviewer 'Reviewer name' --source 'Actual review URL or decision reference' \\\\\\n  --reason 'Accepted scope, approach, and acceptance criteria'\\n```\\n\\nLight runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3: `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. Full runs record stages 1, 2 and 3 in order. `changes-requested` records rejection with the same command. Decisions bind their artifacts and contract inputs; edits stale them.\\n\\n`decide` and seed-judge `mark` are human-only: they refuse a reviewer starting with `Agent`, containing `not human`, or an agent actor (`--actor`, or run metadata). Older agent records stay readable, labelled `agent-recorded`, and never count as human acceptance.\\n\\n## Checklist\\n\\n`runs/<slug>/checklist.json` holds item definitions only: `{\\\"schema\\\":1,\\\"items\\\":[{\\\"id\\\",\\\"description\\\",\\\"verify\\\",\\\"check\\\"?}]}`. They are approved scope: stage 3 cannot be approved with zero or invalid items, and changing them stales that approval. Never add status fields. If the file is deleted, status reads MISSING and verification refuses until it is restored from Git.\\n\\n- **Checked items** carry `check`: `{\\\"argv\\\":[...],\\\"timeout_seconds\\\":N}`. `verify` runs them in the repository root after the configured checks, logging to `test-log.md`; a failure fails verification (`Checklist check failed: <id>`). A pass proves the item, so `mark` is refused for it.\\n- **Attested items** have no `check`: `sdlc.py mark <run> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>` appends a receipt under `marks/` (needs current stage 3 approval; latest wins; `verify` requires `passed`). Verification binds each latest mark, so mark first, then verify. Attestations are self-reported claims for review to challenge. Status flags a mark made on an older candidate; re-check it if the change affects it.\\n\\nWhen every command passes but attestations are missing, `verify` exits nonzero with `Checklist incomplete`, the mark commands to run, and `result: awaiting-attestation` with `pending_attestations`. That is machine proof only, not a pass. It stays current while its proof inputs and log match (evidence-only commits keep it); a mark or changed input retires it. An item a person marked `failed` makes the result `failed`. Record the actual observations, then rerun `verify`.\\n\\n### Locked seed acceptance checks\\n\\nAt stage-1 intake, use the explicitly supplied session and a trusted Tinkery executable:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 1 \\\\\\n  --seed-contract <session>/seed-contract.md \\\\\\n  --tinkery <path>/tinkery\\n```\\n\\nTinkery is the single seed verifier. Intake runs exactly `PATH seed status --json DIR` and `PATH seed checks DIR` (DIR is the seed's session directory) and reads status again to detect changes. Tinkery verifies human v2 and agent v3 confirmations against the seed; intake accepts v3 only with `operator: agent` and an agent `actor`, which records agent confirmation, never human confirmation. Missing executables, failed commands, malformed output, simulated sessions, missing confirmation and changed bindings refuse before any write. Supply trusted toolchain code, never a seed-provided script. Verification proves content binding, not operator identity or implementation approval.\\n\\nIntake copies the confirmed seed and its records into locked `runs/<slug>/seed-checks.json` items with reserved `seed-N` IDs; run metadata keeps the confirmation receipt and the original definitions' digest. Seed Markdown is never read as checks. Imported items are **LOCKED, seed-confirmed** definitions, distinct from the worker's `checklist.json`. Never silently weaken or remove acceptance: such edits are departures. Deleting the file or changing the run-local seed refuses verification. Later edits to the original session do not reach the run-local snapshot; to rebind, run verified intake again; rebinding replaces activations, and prior evidence stays in Git.\\n\\n#### Seed definition comparison\\n\\nStage 3 compares effective checks with the intake baseline. With no added, removed or changed checks, and no activation other than the confirmed proposal, it passes without another human question and records `matches confirmed seed (sha256: …)` as evidence, not a human decision. This is structural, not a claim about the brief or permission to implement. An explicit human `changes-requested` stays blocking until a later human decision supersedes it.\\n\\nAny departure blocks automatic passage. Status lists each changed field, removed or added check, added worker item and non-proposal activation, and asks only `Accept these departures from the confirmed seed?`. `decide … 3` records the human answer bound to those exact inputs. Full-profile stages 1 and 2 keep their own reviews. Legacy bindings without a stored baseline compare against the intake digest; if their definitions changed, rebind rather than invent a baseline.\\n\\nSeed record kinds:\\n\\n- **`cmd`** runs verbatim through `bash -c` with a 120-second timeout, or `seed_timeout_seconds` from `_system/verification.json`; `cwd` is relative to the checkout (absolute paths, traversal, backslashes, drive letters, NUL and CR/LF refused). Failures and timeouts fail `verify` even when all worker checks pass. **Passing output proves a `cmd` or activated proposal entirely; no `seed-N-expect` mark is owed.** `expect` is descriptive context, not a matcher.\\n- **`judge`** (prose or taste) needs a manual human attestation, judged by `how_to_judge` or else the statement, never an automated pass. Record only the actual operator's observation:\\n\\n  ```sh\\n  python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py mark <slug> seed-1 passed \\\\\\n    --reviewer 'Actual operator' --source 'Actual observation reference' \\\\\\n    --evidence 'Observed output and why it satisfies the seed expectation'\\n  ```\\n\\n  Evidence already used for another item is refused unless `--covers-all` says the operator meant it for each. Mark `unknown` when the observation does not address the expectation; `unknown` blocks verification. Marks need current definition approval and bind the exact item.\\n- **`provisional`** names its prerequisite in `missing`; `cmd` is an optional proposal. It stays pending and nonblocking, never proven, while the prerequisite is absent. At implementation time, inspect the prerequisite. **Once it exists**, add only an `activation` object to that item in `seed-checks.json`, keeping the proposal intact:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\\"activation\\\": {\\\"cmd\\\":…2027 tokens truncated…ython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py verify`.\\n- The reviewer works in a separate checkout, writes `runs/<slug>/05-deploy/output/REVIEW-findings.md`,\\n  and changes no code or evidence. The candidate's owner brings the findings to the PR branch.\\n\\n## Pinned build steps\\n\\n`stage` prepares a checkout and prints a prompt; it never starts an agent. For an explicitly\\nauthorized build agent, open stage 3, then, from that checkout, take these deliberate steps one at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build prepare \\\\\\n  --report /absolute/run-notes/build-report.md \\\\\\n  --model provider/exact-model-id --launcher /absolute/trusted-adapter [--max-responses N]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build show\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build launch\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build resume\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete --stopped-by operator  # or cap\\n```\\n\\n- `prepare` writes an immutable `runs/<run>/steps/<name>.json` binding the checkout, the exact model,\\n  the launcher's path and SHA-256, and a fresh absolute report path whose parent exists. It starts nothing.\\n  It refuses existing names or reports, relative paths, model aliases, and a run branch checked out\\n  elsewhere. Contracts are not portable: prepare a new step in a new checkout.\\n- `--max-responses N` caps responses; an adapter that cannot enforce it refuses, so a cap is never ignored.\\n- `launch` and `resume` recheck every definition gate and resolve the exact model offline first. The\\n  prompt names the approved inputs, `stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md` and the report path; limits writes to\\n  the checkout and report; tells the worker to commit before ending each turn; and forbids `sdlc.py step`, `stage` and `decide`. Inside the session\\n  `TINK_SDLC_STEP_SESSION=1` makes `step` refuse everything but `show`. Resume keeps the same session.\\n- `complete` needs a nonempty report at the exact path and the adapter's offline confirmation that the\\n  agent's latest turn ended on the pin. A paused, failed or running agent leaves the step `active`.\\n  `--stopped-by operator|cap` ends the step's process group and records it `stopped`, never as an ended turn.\\n- `show` adds `observed_turn` (`ended`, `not ended` or `unknown`) from an offline probe.\\n- A crash or failed admission leaves the step `uncertain`, never retried. Inspect the real session and\\n  reconcile it before preparing another step; never hand-edit the contract.\\n\\nA report is not proof of passing checks, authorship or approval; `verify` and forge gates stay independent.\\n\\nBundled adapters (their module comments state what each promises and refuses):\\n\\n| Adapter | Pin | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `_system/scripts/pi-launcher` | `provider/model-id:thinking`, e.g. `openai-codex/gpt-5.6-luna:low` | The model must be in Pi's local catalog (run `pi update --models` yourself) with a stored credential. Enforces `--max-responses`. |\\n\\nResume pinned steps only through `step … resume`: pi-herdr's resume drops the pin. Another launcher\\nmust implement the [launcher protocol](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/launcher-protocol.md)\\nand pass offline-resolution tests before it gets a live-call budget.\\n\\n## Stage skills\\n\\nEach stage has a skillset pin committed at `.tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json`: 01-plan `planning-skillset`, 02-design `design-skillset`, 03-build `build-skillset`, 04-test `testing-skillset`, 05-deploy `deployment-skillset`, 06-maintain `maintenance-skillset`. A pin selects exact upstream code, so review a pin change like a lockfile change.\\n\\n```sh\\ntink library fetch .tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json    # once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin\\ntink use <name>-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/<stage-dir>  # at stage open, then start a NEW session\\ntink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`required` lists the disciplines `tink use` compiles into `AGENTS.md` at stage open. Capability skills come only through `tink-route` for genuine gaps. It searches the whole library (`--anywhere`, the default; `--skillset NAME` restricts it, and exit 1 may then add `Hint: <skill> fits but is on another shelf`). Exit 1 or 2 means continue without a skill. Keep its receipt with the run and read the delivered skill before relying on it. `tink-route` mounts into the git-ignored `.tink/.active` and changes no project skills.\\n\\nCommit `.tink/skills.toml` and `.tink/skills.lock` when Tink creates them through an authorized operation; restore them into each worktree through Tink, never by symlink. Run `tink` mutations through the wrapper, which serializes them on this host and rejects symlinked skill state:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py skills tink -- skill check\\n```\\n\\nTinkery's verified confirmed seed contract is the input to 01-plan (bind it as in [Locked seed acceptance checks](#locked-seed-acceptance-checks)). `tink use` and `tink-route` are optional; runs work without them.\\n\\n## Recovery and release\\n\\nRuns have one writer at a time; a busy or interrupted operation fails with its lock path. After a crash, confirm no process owns the operation, remove the lock directory and rerun.\\n\\nOpen the PR once the candidate is committed and verification is current (end of stage 4; `stage <slug> 5` enforces it). Delivery and merge authority belong to `stages/05-deploy/CONTEXT.md`. Keep deployed revision, result and rollback references in the deployment system.\\n\\nRecord the run's ending:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py pr <run> <PR URL>   # needs current verification; one PR per run\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py label <run> --kind correction|none|accepted|rejected --words TEXT \\\\\\n  --reviewer NAME --source REF [--about FILE|outside] [--consent local|shareable]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py close <run> --outcome merged --merge <full commit>   # or --outcome abandoned\\n```\\n\\nPut `Tink-Run: <run_id>` in the PR description. Labels are human-only (refused inside a build step session), keep the words verbatim, and chain to each other by digest; `none` records no corrections and `outside` a correction outside the run's records. `close` needs a label (and, when merged, the recorded PR); a run closes once. While the run builds in another checkout, these refuse and name it. Closing in a linked worktree copies the run's records into the main checkout's `runs/<run>/` (refusing if that folder has uncommitted changes or another run); commit them there.\\n\\nMaintenance is optional intake. Enable it only with metric thresholds, deduplication, cooldowns and run limits; alerts create drafts for triage, never approvals.\\n\\n## Machine interface (API 1)\\n\\nClients read `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py capabilities --json` and `status [<run>] --json` from the selected checkout's runtime, check `protocol`, `api_version` and capabilities first, and fail closed on unknown states, never falling back to the human CLI output. The field contract is in [references/api-1.md](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/api-1.md).\\n\\n## Maintaining the scaffold\\n\\n`python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py walk [--json]` is a read-only structural check that a memoryless agent can orient from the files: router size, resolving pointers, stage contracts naming Inputs, Output and a human Gate, per-stage context size, skillset pins, `status` for every run, and current `tink:rules` blocks. A real walk by a fresh agent stays the test.\\n\\nPreview, then apply an upgrade (`_system/scaffold.json` is the trust baseline):\\n\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade --check\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade\\n\\nUnmodified managed files are updated, restored or removed; project-owned files (`_system/verification.json`, `.tink/skillsets/*.json`) are never overwritten. A customized managed file blocks the upgrade and is listed with a `diff`: merge by hand, or commit and rerun with `--overwrite-customized`. Only the `AGENTS.md` router block is replaced; `runs/` is never modified, but changed stage 1-3 contracts stale existing approvals. Downgrades are refused, and an interrupted upgrade is safe to rerun. A killed install can leave a partial one: inspect it and its lock, confirm no writer remains, and recover through a reviewed migration, never a forced overwrite. Never update factory files during an active feature run. Afterward run `walk`.\\n\\nTemplates live in `_shared/` (`brief-template.md` is the light-profile edit surface); run outputs go under `runs/`. Read the active contract and its immediate inputs, not the whole factory.\\n\"}\n==> text 3/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\",\"content\":\"# Stage 03: Implement\\n\\nInputs: current approved spec for full runs, or combined brief for light runs. A seed-bound run's\\nconfirmed `seed-contract.md` and locked checks define it; its `brief.md` may stay the template.\\nOutput: `runs/<slug>/03-build/output/plan.md` for full runs, the approved checklist definitions\\nin `runs/<slug>/checklist.json` for both profiles, and code in an isolated worktree/clone.\\nItems should carry a `check` whenever an automated proof exists; `verify` runs it and no mark is needed.\\nWhen a pending provisional seed check's prerequisite exists, follow the guide's [provisional activation step](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison) and report the activation; a command other than the confirmed proposal needs operator stage-3 approval.\\n\\nGate: inspect code and follow the stage 3 gate before implementation; for seed-bound\\nruns use the guide's [seed definition comparison](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison).\\nFor full runs, write the plan first. Light runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3 (`sdlc.py decide <run> 3 ...`): the approved `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. An explicit user instruction to execute a reviewed proposal is\\nimplementation authority; never fabricate separate role approvals.\\nEach concurrent writer needs its own checkout. Use the serialized skill wrapper\\nin `_system/SDLC.md`; no shared writable skill symlinks or automatic lockfile rewrites.\\n\\nFor bug fixes, reproduce the expected failure first, obtain independent acceptance,\\nand record protected test inputs with `sdlc.py lock-tests`. Implement and verify in\\na loop with stage 04. Update the plan when scope changes and renew stale decisions.\\nMark items only with `sdlc.py mark <slug> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>`; never hand-edit receipts.\\nFor launcher-managed build agents, use the guide's [pinned build steps](../../_system/SDLC.md#pinned-build-steps).\\nRead the supplied step contract, write the handoff at its exact absolute report path,\\nand retain that contract and model pin on resume. A missing report refuses step completion.\\n\\n## Skills\\n\\nSkillset: `build-skillset` (pin: `.tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`).\\n- Once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin (it selects exact upstream code):\\n  `tink library fetch .tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`\\n- At stage open, compile the required disciplines, then start a NEW session so\\n  `AGENTS.md` is re-read: `tink use build-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/03-build`\\n  The launcher does this for you: `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 3`.\\n- For a capability gap: `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"`\\n  (searches the whole library; prints the skill on stdout. Exit 1 means nothing fits; exit 1 or 2 means\\n  continue without a skill).\\n\\nStage skills (always for this stage; `tink use` compiles the same set):\\n- `principle-build-the-lever`: Non-trivial work: build the script or tool that does or proves it, so a reviewer can rerun it, instead of doing it by hand.\\n- `unslop`: Any prose you write (docs, commit messages, replies): cut AI tells and filler before you send it.\\nWhen one of these applies, read that skill in full before acting on it. In the handoff note, name each skill that changed a decision and the decision it changed.\\n\"}\n==> text 4/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\",\"content\":\"# Change brief\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/864\\nIssue body sha256: 29f447833355967617805c1fc76789f53c35598279aa8b5a4f7ba921cf6aa3b1\\nTitle: Tend: Keep a raw project thought before any goal is confirmed\\n\\nThe approved issue below is this run's brief, unchanged. Each Done-when line is one item in `checklist.json`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Primary area\\n\\ntend\\n\\n### Kind\\n\\nenhancement\\n\\n### Goal\\n\\nThe operator needs one place per project to keep ideas, goals and thoughts, including thoughts that are not yet goals.\\nSaving a thought must not claim that the operator confirmed a goal.\\n\\n### Evidence\\n\\nOperator words, 2026-11-15, exact:\\n\\n> i just want a place that i can saturate my ideas/goals and thoughts in that are project specific and I can allow LLM to find surfaced connections, refactoring and determination if a goal(s) are ready for shaping. thats my main goal. I think idempotency is an idea that is relevant\\n\\n> think of an entry to tend be a neuron, and with enough neurons a action potential activates over those neuron connections, and then its refined and insulated better through shape like myelination and then then the action is tink-sdlc which is like the behavior..\\n\\nAsked whether Tend must keep a raw thought before any goal is confirmed from it, the operator answered: \\\"Yes, raw thoughts\\\".\\n\\nObserved in code at `04c2bc4`:\\n\\n- Tend lists saved sessions only. Think exits without confirmation leave no record (`docs/design/think.md`, Think record).\\n- `tinkery seed new` saves any text without Think or a model call, but records it as a confirmed goal (`src/shape/command.rs`, `new_goal`).\\n- That command writes no repository attribution. One active goal with unknown attribution makes Find connections refuse\\n  for every repository (`src/tend/connection.rs`, `snapshot_with`).\\n\\nProposal, not observation: a thought is a separate kind of entry, not a goal with a weaker label.\\nNot established: that more entries make connection discovery useful. The one real trial gave a false alarm.\\n\\n### Scope and boundaries\\n\\nOne outcome: the operator saves exact words as a thought in the current repository's garden, sees it there after restart,\\nand later turns it into a goal only through the existing goal confirmation.\\n\\nChanges:\\n\\n- A key in Tend's repository garden opens one exact-word editor. No Think conversation is needed.\\n- One explicit save of exact words, with the repository shown before saving. No model call.\\n- Thoughts appear in the repository garden, visibly distinct from goals with and without colour.\\n- A thought records where it was saved. It never has unknown attribution.\\n\\nStays unchanged:\\n\\n- Goal confirmation, seed confirmation and implementation permission. A thought is none of them.\\n- Shape accepts confirmed goals only. A thought cannot open Shape.\\n- Find connections input, its 20-goal and 32 KiB bounds, the saved correction and all saved-goal formats.\\n  Thoughts do not enter a provider request in this slice.\\n- No automatic interpretation, grouping, merging, readiness judgment, wiki or background work.\\n- No editing or deletion of a saved thought in this slice.\\n\\nSubtract first: reuse the exact-word editor, the repository boundary, the private atomic write and the garden view.\\nIf the build needs a new storage service, a session-format migration or a change to the shared goal saver, stop and report.\\n\\n### Done when\\n\\n- [ ] From the repository garden, the operator saves a thought with one disclosed action, and the saved words equal the typed words byte for byte.\\n- [ ] After restart, the thought is listed in that repository's garden, labelled as a thought and not as a goal.\\n- [ ] A saved thought has no goal confirmation record, cannot open Shape, and is absent from the Find connections request.\\n- [ ] Find connections still runs in a repository that holds thoughts. A thought never causes incomplete discovery.\\n- [ ] Saving the same exact words twice in one repository results in one entry, and the screen says so.\\n- [ ] Turning a thought into a goal uses the existing goal confirmation, keeps the thought's words as the original, and leaves the thought on record.\\n- [ ] Terminal journeys at 80×24 and 100×30 with colour off cover save, restart, duplicate save, cancel and an unwritable store, with temporary TEST roots and no provider.\\n- [ ] Reversion probes fail when a thought is recorded as a confirmed goal, when saved words differ from typed words, and when a thought reaches a provider request.\\n\\n### Context and dependencies\\n\\n- [Yohaku](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/yohaku.md), Articles 1 and 2.\\n- [Tend and handoff](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/shape-and-handoff.md#tend-garden-and-detail) and [Think](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/think.md).\\n- #860 and #862 are merged. This slice does not change them.\\n- #852 covers pruning and restoring goals. It is related and not a blocker.\\n- Later, separate slices: thoughts as Find connections input, volume above 20 entries, refactoring proposals,\\n  readiness judgments and dismissal that persists. None is part of this issue.\\n\\n### Open decisions and permissions\\n\\nDecided by the operator on 2026-11-15: a thought enters through a key in Tend's garden. Operator words: \\\"Key in Tend garden\\\".\\nThe key name, its label and its place in the key bar remain design details for review.\\n\\nDefaults proposed by the agent and accepted by the operator for this slice on 2026-11-15:\\n\\n1. A saved thought cannot be edited or removed in this slice. Pruning is separate work (#852).\\n2. \\\"Project\\\" means the launch repository, as goals use today.\\n3. A duplicate means the same exact bytes in the same repository. Whitespace and case differences make a different thought.\\n\\nEach default can change in a later slice. No other decision is open for this slice.\\n\\nThis issue records a design request. Publication, implementation, provider budget and real-data trials each need separate operator approval.\\n\\n\"}\n==> text 5/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"d50e7a87-1232-43e5-a29a-0cbf2512c1ab\\\",\\n  \\\"checkout\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought\\\",\\n  \\\"report\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/output/handoff.md\\\",\\n  \\\"model\\\": \\\"openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol:medium\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/_system/scripts/pi-launcher\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher_sha256\\\": \\\"d0c05547c00cf7882c0fef3d8e89d786fdd2a488b76413aa9159cd61183a6763\\\",\\n  \\\"state\\\": \\\"active\\\",\\n  \\\"session\\\": \\\"tsdlc-4e1822e7fd164f8b825f6555621346b6\\\",\\n  \\\"prepared_ns\\\": 1794731465469500000,\\n  \\\"max_responses\\\": 250,\\n  \\\"process_group\\\": 32154\\n}\\n\"}\n==> text 6/7 <==\n{\"output\":\" M AGENTS.md\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n7c134de stage 3 open: issue-864-tend-thought (approved artifacts and receipts)\\n04c2bc4 Merge pull request #863 from jon-devlapaz/codex/issue-862-tend-correction\\nba6606f Record independent review of Linux test repair\\ndade851 docs: record verified Linux correction test fix\\nc1728c6 test(tend): observe clear frame before large correction paste\\nissue-860-tend-connection\\nissue-862-tend-correction\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit_code\":0,\"wall_time_seconds\":0.1}\n==> text 7/7 <==\nCreated #1: Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation (pending)\nCreated #2: Implement the approved slice and regression checks (pending)\nCreated #3: Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes (pending)\n\n[Full output: /var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt (read with offset/limit)]"}],"details":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.3395000000000437},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.0922499999996944},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.6723750000001019},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.624958999998853},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.575790999999299},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","args":"{\"command\":\"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status\",\"timeout\":30}","status":"ok","durationMs":117.500082999999},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/7","name":"todo","args":"{\"action\":\"create\",\"tasks\":[{\"subject\":\"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation\"},{\"subject\":\"Implement the approved slice and regression checks\"},{\"subject\":\"Run checks, write han...","status":"ok","durationMs":1.6686250000002474}],"fullOutputPath":"/var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt"},"isError":false,"durationMs":190,"timestamp":1794731482775,"nestedCalls":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"AGENTS.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"_system/SDLC.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","status":"ok","arguments":{"command":"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status","timeout":30},"durationMs":117},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/7","name":"todo","status":"ok","arguments":{"action":"create","tasks":[{"subject":"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation"},{"subject":"Implement the approved slice and regression checks"},{"subject":"Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes"}]},"durationMs":2}],"complete":true}}}
{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367","toolName":"codemode","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 12027)\nTotal output lines: 16\n\n==> text 1/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\",\"content\":\"# Working on Tinkery\\n\\n**Read [docs/yohaku.md](docs/yohaku.md) first:** the constitution wins over guides,\\ndesigns and briefs. Only the operator amends it; report discrepancies, never edit it.\\n[README.md](README.md) describes shipped behavior.\\n\\n## Guardrails\\n\\n- Subtract first; say each thing once, plainly.\\n- Fewer, deeper modules, smaller interfaces. Check data once where it comes in, keep\\n  that guarantee in types, then just use it. Write one-off logic right where it's used\\n  instead of splitting it into a function ahead of time. Skip try/catch wherever you can.\\n- Check changeability: name the [architecture diagnostics](docs/design/architecture.md#design-score)\\n  moved. Module and `pub` comments state promises and refusals, not implementation;\\n  the build requires them.\\n- Preserve operator originals verbatim; readings are guesses.\\n  Keep the displayed, confirmed goal unchanged through saving and handoff.\\n- Navigation and copying never launch agents. Goal confirmation is neither seed\\n  confirmation nor implementation permission.\\n- Work from an issue on a branch and open a PR. Merge it yourself once the issue is approved\\n  (or the operator asked for the change), CI is green, there are no conflicts and a reviewer\\n  who did not write the change has checked it against the issue. The one exception is the\\n  [tink-sdlc bump](.github/workflows/tink-sdlc-bump.yml): it merges itself when CI passes and it\\n  is exactly tink-sdlc's own upgrade.\\n  If the operator asks for drafts only, create and merge nothing until they say so.\\n- Model and provider calls require an explicit budget. The default is zero.\\n  Eval commands are outside zero-call verification.\\n- Agent operators (Pi via herdr) always launch Tinkery with `--operator agent`.\\n- Never record real audio; voice tests use fake engines and recorders, never native addons.\\n- Use temporary TEST roots. Never write to `~/.local/share/seed-me` or\\n  `~/.local/share/tinkery`, or change operator sessions, installs, launcher, Pi,\\n  Pi Voice or the operator's Seed Me checkout.\\n- Fix a bug from its observed cause. After two failed fixes, add logging and collect\\n  observations; after three, stop and hand over a fresh brief: problem, goal, failed attempts.\\n- Read relevant tests and design before implementation; update changed decisions.\\n  Add regressions, prove important protections fail when reverted, then restore them.\\n  Tests assert what users observe through public interfaces; never add test-only access\\n  to private state. Drive Think through its input, not `Conversation`'s capped test accessors.\\n  Unowned ideas confer neither shipped behavior nor build permission.\\n- For agent-facing docs, use `writing-for-agents`: one rule per authoritative home,\\n  task detail behind pointers.\\n  Preserve safeguards and runnable commands; brevity is not permission to weaken them.\\n  `tests/test_documentation.py` checks links, headings, paths, options and reachability.\\n- Create each task's worktree at `~/dev/worktrees/<repo>/<branch>`, never inside a checkout\\n  or beside one.\\n- At rest, keep a clean `main` matching `origin/main` after a fetch, with no stashes,\\n  extra worktrees or leftover branches. Report open PRs instead of cleaning them up. Delete only\\n  merged branches (`git branch -d`); never force-delete branches, drop stashes or\\n  remove worktrees for cleanliness.\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nEach reply spends the operator's attention, so it must earn its place ([Yohaku](docs/yohaku.md)).\\nWrite in plain technical English, modelled on\\n[simple-english](https://github.com/arekhalpern/simple-english) (ASD-STE100):\\n\\n- Lead with what the operator must decide or do, then the result. Ask at most one question.\\n- Cut each line that does not change what the operator does next.\\n- Name the state of each claim: proposed, attempted, observed, checked or accepted.\\n  Say unknowns and failures plainly, with the next step.\\n- Quote the operator's words exactly, and mark your readings as guesses.\\n- Give one fact per sentence: at most 20 words for an instruction, 25 for a description.\\n  Use active voice and simple tenses.\\n- Write \\\"must\\\" for a requirement and \\\"can\\\" for a possibility, never \\\"should\\\", \\\"may\\\" or \\\"might\\\".\\n  Put a condition before its instruction: \\\"If CI fails, read the log.\\\"\\n- Keep one name for one thing. Write complete sentences with articles. No contractions,\\n  semicolons, arrow chains or filler that states no fact.\\n- Never change code, commands, identifiers or quoted errors.\\n\\n## Layout and design entry points\\n\\nRead only the relevant task contract from the\\n[documentation index](docs/README.md). The [architecture map](docs/design/architecture.md#modules-today)\\nshows module responsibilities.\\n\\n## Cold setup\\n\\nFresh Debian or Ubuntu clone with Bash and Git:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/cloud-setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe script installs missing prerequisites, stable Rust with rustfmt and Clippy, and Node.\\nIt prints the verification exports and can be rerun safely. It downloads only\\nprerequisite packages, not providers, microphones, native voice engines or speech models.\\nApt may need root or sudo.\\n\\nCI uses stable Rust: Ubuntu on pull requests, Ubuntu and macOS on `main`; use that updated stable for Clippy.\\nNever alter operator toolchains without approval. On macOS, provision Rust, Python 3\\nand Node separately (bootstrap refuses macOS). No external skill checkout or other\\ndeveloper's files are needed.\\n\\n## Full zero-call verification\\n\\n[The Rust test wrapper](scripts/rust-tests.py) runs the workspace in a fresh TEST HOME\\nand refuses default application storage.\\n\\nRun `python3 scripts/full-check.py` from the repository root after setup. Its shared\\nlaunch guard gives the exact block below a temporary TEST HOME; do not run binary\\nverification against the operator's HOME. Cold-clone CI runs the block when setup may change. [CI routing](scripts/ci-scope.py) and its\\n[regressions](tests/test_ci_scope.py) define prose-only exceptions and fail-closed gates;\\ncode changes require the full block. [The Python runner](scripts/python-tests.py) runs files\\nin parallel; with `TINKERY_TEST_TIER=pr`, as on pull requests, it skips only the slow journeys it\\nlists, which still run on every push to `main`.\\n\\nNode is only for `voice/*.test.mjs` and `pi/*.test.mjs`. Keep the invalid `TINKERY_NODE` for voice PTYs;\\nPython journeys use the real binary, stub providers and temporary roots.\\n\\n<!-- full-check:start -->\\n```bash\\nexport PATH=\\\"${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}/bin:$PWD/.cloud-tools/node/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nexport TINKERY_NODE=/tinkery-TEST-no-node\\nrustc +stable --version\\nnode --version\\npython3 --version\\ncargo +stable build --locked\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --home --snapshot\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --snapshot --no-color\\npython3 scripts/rust-tests.py\\nnode --test voice/*.test.mjs pi/*.test.mjs\\npython3 scripts/python-tests.py\\ncargo +stable fmt --all -- --check\\ncargo +stable clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\\n```\\n<!-- full-check:end -->\\n\\n[Try it](README.md#try-it) without the installed launcher. `--snapshot` prints\\n100×30 without terminal mode or provider calls; `NO_COLOR=1` or `--no-color` retain non-color\\ncues. [Terminal setup](docs/terminal-setup.md#launch) has isolated real-drafting instructions.\\n\\n## Work intake\\n\\nThe board is [tinkery](https://github.com/users/jon-devlapaz/projects/2); its automations add\\ntinkery issues and set Done on close. Cloud sessions can't reach it; use `gh` on the operator's Mac.\\n\\nFile an issue only for work that serves Tinkery, after searching for an open duplicate, using the\\n[task template](.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/task.yml) with one `area:` and one kind label. Show the draft\\nand publish only on the operator's word; publication is not approval. Parallel work needs approved,\\nnon-blocking items with separate `area:` labels.\\n\\nUnapproved work stays `needs-approval`. `approved` records scoped operator permission,\\nnever grants it; material scope changes return to `needs-approval`. Publication, design\\nacceptance, implementation, data use and budget are separate decisions.\\n\\nUse one branch and PR per approved slice; link the issue, stay within its scope and fill in the\\n[PR template](.github/pull_request_template.md). Close with\\ncompletion evidence or an explicit reason. Report Rust, Node and Python counts, PTY\\njourneys and sizes, failures and reversion probes separately from operator dogfood:\\nstubs prove mechanics, not real-dump goal quality.\\n\\n\\n<!-- AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n## SDLC Workspace\\n- Read `_system/SDLC.md` for setup, evidence boundaries, and recovery.\\n- Inspect `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status` before creating a run.\\n- Read `stages/<stage-name>/CONTEXT.md` before processing a stage.\\n- Keep factory references in `_shared/` unchanged during feature runs.\\n- Use separate worktrees or clones for code-writing runs.\\n- Stage skills: see the Skills section of the current stage's CONTEXT.md.\\n- Need a specialised skill mid-task? `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"` prints it on stdout; exit 1 means none fits, so continue without one.\\n<!-- End AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nOnly respond to the user using ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English and the Bottom Line Up Front (BLUF) approach. Use SBAR for handovers and status updates. These do not apply to code or when the user asks otherwise\\n\\n<!-- tink:rules begin skillset=build-skillset digest=1f3d3f07b904318d51e96afd8174591e82cabe8ec2da6668bf084aa4d683d4c1 -->\\nDiscipline rules for this phase (compiled by tink; do not edit by hand):\\n- principle-build-the-lever: Apply to any non-trivial work, not just bulk work: edits, migrations, analyses, checks. Build the tool that does it or proves it (codemod, script, generator, or a skill your subagents follow) instead of working by hand. The tool is the artifact a reviewer can rerun. (read: tink mount principle-build-the-lever --json --payload)\\n- unslop: Cut AI tells from any writing. Must always apply. (read: tink mount unslop --json --payload)\\n<!-- tink:rules end -->\\n\"}\n==> text 2/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\",\"content\":\"# SDLC workspace\\n\\nA small filesystem workflow: define a change, implement and verify it in an isolated checkout, then obtain independent release review. Requires Python 3.9+, Bash, and Git on a POSIX system. Native Windows is not supported. Set `require_tink: true` when project policy requires Tink integrity checks.\\n\\n**Boundaries, said once.** Receipts, digests, locks and reviewer names are local workflow records: they detect mistakes and changes, not tampering, and authenticate no one. Checks run real commands, never in a sandbox; review them before approval. Nothing here authorizes a release: trusted CI, protected branches and independent forge review do. Never invent a reviewer or approval, and never hand-edit generated records.\\n\\n## Start a run\\n\\nRun scripts from any working directory; paths resolve from the script location.\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --kind bug\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status fix-example\\n```\\n\\nThe default `light` profile creates `brief.md` (problem, acceptance criteria, approach, risks, verification) and `checklist.json`. A human picks `--profile full` for consequential architecture or policy changes; it creates intent, spec and plan. Only runs created by `new` are supported, and text approval tags are not evidence.\\n\\nTo build an approved GitHub issue, let the issue be the definition:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --issue https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/issues/NUMBER\\n```\\n\\nIt reads the issue once with `gh` and calls no model. `brief.md` holds the issue's title, address, body hash and body unchanged; `checklist.json` holds one attested item for each line under the issue's `Done when` heading, unchanged. The `Done when` section must hold only top-level `-` or `*` lines, one line each, so no approved word is cut or dropped. It refuses a closed issue, an issue without the `approved` label, a Done-when section with no lines or any other content, an unreadable issue and `--profile full`, and then creates no run. `run.json` keeps the issue's address and body hash as a record. It records no decision: stage 3 still needs the human decision.\\n\\nWithout a confirmed seed, review the checklist commands and offer a dry run before definition approval (seed-bound runs use the [seed definition comparison](#seed-definition-comparison)):\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py dry-run fix-example\\n```\\n\\nIt runs every checklist `check` in the repository root with its timeout, replacing `runs/<slug>/checklist-dry-run.log` with launch errors, timeouts and nonzero exits per item. No check process survives its check: each starts in a new session, and on normal exit or timeout the rest of its process group is killed and reaped. Tell an expected reproduction failure from a broken command or environment (such as an unsuitable `TMPDIR`), fix the latter and rerun. The dry run records no decision, mark or receipt, does not enforce a new approval prerequisite, and does not replace `verify`, which still requires current approval and passing checks.\\n\\nAfter the human accepts the brief, record the actual review reference:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py decide fix-example 3 approved \\\\\\n  --reviewer 'Reviewer name' --source 'Actual review URL or decision reference' \\\\\\n  --reason 'Accepted scope, approach, and acceptance criteria'\\n```\\n\\nLight runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3: `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. Full runs record stages 1, 2 and 3 in order. `changes-requested` records rejection with the same command. Decisions bind their artifacts and contract inputs; edits stale them.\\n\\n`decide` and seed-judge `mark` are human-only: they refuse a reviewer starting with `Agent`, containing `not human`, or an agent actor (`--actor`, or run metadata). Older agent records stay readable, labelled `agent-recorded`, and never count as human acceptance.\\n\\n## Checklist\\n\\n`runs/<slug>/checklist.json` holds item definitions only: `{\\\"schema\\\":1,\\\"items\\\":[{\\\"id\\\",\\\"description\\\",\\\"verify\\\",\\\"check\\\"?}]}`. They are approved scope: stage 3 cannot be approved with zero or invalid items, and changing them stales that approval. Never add status fields. If the file is deleted, status reads MISSING and verification refuses until it is restored from Git.\\n\\n- **Checked items** carry `check`: `{\\\"argv\\\":[...],\\\"timeout_seconds\\\":N}`. `verify` runs them in the repository root after the configured checks, logging to `test-log.md`; a failure fails verification (`Checklist check failed: <id>`). A pass proves the item, so `mark` is refused for it.\\n- **Attested items** have no `check`: `sdlc.py mark <run> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>` appends a receipt under `marks/` (needs current stage 3 approval; latest wins; `verify` requires `passed`). Verification binds each latest mark, so mark first, then verify. Attestations are self-reported claims for review to challenge. Status flags a mark made on an older candidate; re-check it if the change affects it.\\n\\nWhen every command passes but attestations are missing, `verify` exits nonzero with `Checklist incomplete`, the mark commands to run, and `result: awaiting-attestation` with `pending_attestations`. That is machine proof only, not a pass. It stays current while its proof inputs and log match (evidence-only commits keep it); a mark or changed input retires it. An item a person marked `failed` makes the result `failed`. Record the actual observations, then rerun `verify`.\\n\\n### Locked seed acceptance checks\\n\\nAt stage-1 intake, use the explicitly supplied session and a trusted Tinkery executable:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 1 \\\\\\n  --seed-contract <session>/seed-contract.md \\\\\\n  --tinkery <path>/tinkery\\n```\\n\\nTinkery is the single seed verifier. Intake runs exactly `PATH seed status --json DIR` and `PATH seed checks DIR` (DIR is the seed's session directory) and reads status again to detect changes. Tinkery verifies human v2 and agent v3 confirmations against the seed; intake accepts v3 only with `operator: agent` and an agent `actor`, which records agent confirmation, never human confirmation. Missing executables, failed commands, malformed output, simulated sessions, missing confirmation and changed bindings refuse before any write. Supply trusted toolchain code, never a seed-provided script. Verification proves content binding, not operator identity or implementation approval.\\n\\nIntake copies the confirmed seed and its records into locked `runs/<slug>/seed-checks.json` items with reserved `seed-N` IDs; run metadata keeps the confirmation receipt and the original definitions' digest. Seed Markdown is never read as checks. Imported items are **LOCKED, seed-confirmed** definitions, distinct from the worker's `checklist.json`. Never silently weaken or remove acceptance: such edits are departures. Deleting the file or changing the run-local seed refuses verification. Later edits to the original session do not reach the run-local snapshot; to rebind, run verified intake again; rebinding replaces activations, and prior evidence stays in Git.\\n\\n#### Seed definition comparison\\n\\nStage 3 compares effective checks with the intake baseline. With no added, removed or changed checks, and no activation other than the confirmed proposal, it passes without another human question and records `matches confirmed seed (sha256: …)` as evidence, not a human decision. This is structural, not a claim about the brief or permission to implement. An explicit human `changes-requested` stays blocking until a later human decision supersedes it.\\n\\nAny departure blocks automatic passage. Status lists each changed field, removed or added check, added worker item and non-proposal activation, and asks only `Accept these departures from the confirmed seed?`. `decide … 3` records the human answer bound to those exact inputs. Full-profile stages 1 and 2 keep their own reviews. Legacy bindings without a stored baseline compare against the intake digest; if their definitions changed, rebind rather than invent a baseline.\\n\\nSeed record kinds:\\n\\n- **`cmd`** runs verbatim through `bash -c` with a 120-second timeout, or `seed_timeout_seconds` from `_system/verification.json`; `cwd` is relative to the checkout (absolute paths, traversal, backslashes, drive letters, NUL and CR/LF refused). Failures and timeouts fail `verify` even when all worker checks pass. **Passing output proves a `cmd` or activated proposal entirely; no `seed-N-expect` mark is owed.** `expect` is descriptive context, not a matcher.\\n- **`judge`** (prose or taste) needs a manual human attestation, judged by `how_to_judge` or else the statement, never an automated pass. Record only the actual operator's observation:\\n\\n  ```sh\\n  python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py mark <slug> seed-1 passed \\\\\\n    --reviewer 'Actual operator' --source 'Actual observation reference' \\\\\\n    --evidence 'Observed output and why it satisfies the seed expectation'\\n  ```\\n\\n  Evidence already used for another item is refused unless `--covers-all` says the operator meant it for each. Mark `unknown` when the observation does not address the expectation; `unknown` blocks verification. Marks need current definition approval and bind the exact item.\\n- **`provisional`** names its prerequisite in `missing`; `cmd` is an optional proposal. It stays pending and nonblocking, never proven, while the prerequisite is absent. At implementation time, inspect the prerequisite. **Once it exists**, add only an `activation` object to that item in `seed-checks.json`, keeping the proposal intact:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\\"activation\\\": {\\\"cmd\\\":…2027 tokens truncated…ython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py verify`.\\n- The reviewer works in a separate checkout, writes `runs/<slug>/05-deploy/output/REVIEW-findings.md`,\\n  and changes no code or evidence. The candidate's owner brings the findings to the PR branch.\\n\\n## Pinned build steps\\n\\n`stage` prepares a checkout and prints a prompt; it never starts an agent. For an explicitly\\nauthorized build agent, open stage 3, then, from that checkout, take these deliberate steps one at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build prepare \\\\\\n  --report /absolute/run-notes/build-report.md \\\\\\n  --model provider/exact-model-id --launcher /absolute/trusted-adapter [--max-responses N]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build show\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build launch\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build resume\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete --stopped-by operator  # or cap\\n```\\n\\n- `prepare` writes an immutable `runs/<run>/steps/<name>.json` binding the checkout, the exact model,\\n  the launcher's path and SHA-256, and a fresh absolute report path whose parent exists. It starts nothing.\\n  It refuses existing names or reports, relative paths, model aliases, and a run branch checked out\\n  elsewhere. Contracts are not portable: prepare a new step in a new checkout.\\n- `--max-responses N` caps responses; an adapter that cannot enforce it refuses, so a cap is never ignored.\\n- `launch` and `resume` recheck every definition gate and resolve the exact model offline first. The\\n  prompt names the approved inputs, `stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md` and the report path; limits writes to\\n  the checkout and report; tells the worker to commit before ending each turn; and forbids `sdlc.py step`, `stage` and `decide`. Inside the session\\n  `TINK_SDLC_STEP_SESSION=1` makes `step` refuse everything but `show`. Resume keeps the same session.\\n- `complete` needs a nonempty report at the exact path and the adapter's offline confirmation that the\\n  agent's latest turn ended on the pin. A paused, failed or running agent leaves the step `active`.\\n  `--stopped-by operator|cap` ends the step's process group and records it `stopped`, never as an ended turn.\\n- `show` adds `observed_turn` (`ended`, `not ended` or `unknown`) from an offline probe.\\n- A crash or failed admission leaves the step `uncertain`, never retried. Inspect the real session and\\n  reconcile it before preparing another step; never hand-edit the contract.\\n\\nA report is not proof of passing checks, authorship or approval; `verify` and forge gates stay independent.\\n\\nBundled adapters (their module comments state what each promises and refuses):\\n\\n| Adapter | Pin | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `_system/scripts/pi-launcher` | `provider/model-id:thinking`, e.g. `openai-codex/gpt-5.6-luna:low` | The model must be in Pi's local catalog (run `pi update --models` yourself) with a stored credential. Enforces `--max-responses`. |\\n\\nResume pinned steps only through `step … resume`: pi-herdr's resume drops the pin. Another launcher\\nmust implement the [launcher protocol](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/launcher-protocol.md)\\nand pass offline-resolution tests before it gets a live-call budget.\\n\\n## Stage skills\\n\\nEach stage has a skillset pin committed at `.tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json`: 01-plan `planning-skillset`, 02-design `design-skillset`, 03-build `build-skillset`, 04-test `testing-skillset`, 05-deploy `deployment-skillset`, 06-maintain `maintenance-skillset`. A pin selects exact upstream code, so review a pin change like a lockfile change.\\n\\n```sh\\ntink library fetch .tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json    # once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin\\ntink use <name>-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/<stage-dir>  # at stage open, then start a NEW session\\ntink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`required` lists the disciplines `tink use` compiles into `AGENTS.md` at stage open. Capability skills come only through `tink-route` for genuine gaps. It searches the whole library (`--anywhere`, the default; `--skillset NAME` restricts it, and exit 1 may then add `Hint: <skill> fits but is on another shelf`). Exit 1 or 2 means continue without a skill. Keep its receipt with the run and read the delivered skill before relying on it. `tink-route` mounts into the git-ignored `.tink/.active` and changes no project skills.\\n\\nCommit `.tink/skills.toml` and `.tink/skills.lock` when Tink creates them through an authorized operation; restore them into each worktree through Tink, never by symlink. Run `tink` mutations through the wrapper, which serializes them on this host and rejects symlinked skill state:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py skills tink -- skill check\\n```\\n\\nTinkery's verified confirmed seed contract is the input to 01-plan (bind it as in [Locked seed acceptance checks](#locked-seed-acceptance-checks)). `tink use` and `tink-route` are optional; runs work without them.\\n\\n## Recovery and release\\n\\nRuns have one writer at a time; a busy or interrupted operation fails with its lock path. After a crash, confirm no process owns the operation, remove the lock directory and rerun.\\n\\nOpen the PR once the candidate is committed and verification is current (end of stage 4; `stage <slug> 5` enforces it). Delivery and merge authority belong to `stages/05-deploy/CONTEXT.md`. Keep deployed revision, result and rollback references in the deployment system.\\n\\nRecord the run's ending:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py pr <run> <PR URL>   # needs current verification; one PR per run\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py label <run> --kind correction|none|accepted|rejected --words TEXT \\\\\\n  --reviewer NAME --source REF [--about FILE|outside] [--consent local|shareable]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py close <run> --outcome merged --merge <full commit>   # or --outcome abandoned\\n```\\n\\nPut `Tink-Run: <run_id>` in the PR description. Labels are human-only (refused inside a build step session), keep the words verbatim, and chain to each other by digest; `none` records no corrections and `outside` a correction outside the run's records. `close` needs a label (and, when merged, the recorded PR); a run closes once. While the run builds in another checkout, these refuse and name it. Closing in a linked worktree copies the run's records into the main checkout's `runs/<run>/` (refusing if that folder has uncommitted changes or another run); commit them there.\\n\\nMaintenance is optional intake. Enable it only with metric thresholds, deduplication, cooldowns and run limits; alerts create drafts for triage, never approvals.\\n\\n## Machine interface (API 1)\\n\\nClients read `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py capabilities --json` and `status [<run>] --json` from the selected checkout's runtime, check `protocol`, `api_version` and capabilities first, and fail closed on unknown states, never falling back to the human CLI output. The field contract is in [references/api-1.md](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/api-1.md).\\n\\n## Maintaining the scaffold\\n\\n`python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py walk [--json]` is a read-only structural check that a memoryless agent can orient from the files: router size, resolving pointers, stage contracts naming Inputs, Output and a human Gate, per-stage context size, skillset pins, `status` for every run, and current `tink:rules` blocks. A real walk by a fresh agent stays the test.\\n\\nPreview, then apply an upgrade (`_system/scaffold.json` is the trust baseline):\\n\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade --check\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade\\n\\nUnmodified managed files are updated, restored or removed; project-owned files (`_system/verification.json`, `.tink/skillsets/*.json`) are never overwritten. A customized managed file blocks the upgrade and is listed with a `diff`: merge by hand, or commit and rerun with `--overwrite-customized`. Only the `AGENTS.md` router block is replaced; `runs/` is never modified, but changed stage 1-3 contracts stale existing approvals. Downgrades are refused, and an interrupted upgrade is safe to rerun. A killed install can leave a partial one: inspect it and its lock, confirm no writer remains, and recover through a reviewed migration, never a forced overwrite. Never update factory files during an active feature run. Afterward run `walk`.\\n\\nTemplates live in `_shared/` (`brief-template.md` is the light-profile edit surface); run outputs go under `runs/`. Read the active contract and its immediate inputs, not the whole factory.\\n\"}\n==> text 3/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\",\"content\":\"# Stage 03: Implement\\n\\nInputs: current approved spec for full runs, or combined brief for light runs. A seed-bound run's\\nconfirmed `seed-contract.md` and locked checks define it; its `brief.md` may stay the template.\\nOutput: `runs/<slug>/03-build/output/plan.md` for full runs, the approved checklist definitions\\nin `runs/<slug>/checklist.json` for both profiles, and code in an isolated worktree/clone.\\nItems should carry a `check` whenever an automated proof exists; `verify` runs it and no mark is needed.\\nWhen a pending provisional seed check's prerequisite exists, follow the guide's [provisional activation step](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison) and report the activation; a command other than the confirmed proposal needs operator stage-3 approval.\\n\\nGate: inspect code and follow the stage 3 gate before implementation; for seed-bound\\nruns use the guide's [seed definition comparison](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison).\\nFor full runs, write the plan first. Light runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3 (`sdlc.py decide <run> 3 ...`): the approved `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. An explicit user instruction to execute a reviewed proposal is\\nimplementation authority; never fabricate separate role approvals.\\nEach concurrent writer needs its own checkout. Use the serialized skill wrapper\\nin `_system/SDLC.md`; no shared writable skill symlinks or automatic lockfile rewrites.\\n\\nFor bug fixes, reproduce the expected failure first, obtain independent acceptance,\\nand record protected test inputs with `sdlc.py lock-tests`. Implement and verify in\\na loop with stage 04. Update the plan when scope changes and renew stale decisions.\\nMark items only with `sdlc.py mark <slug> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>`; never hand-edit receipts.\\nFor launcher-managed build agents, use the guide's [pinned build steps](../../_system/SDLC.md#pinned-build-steps).\\nRead the supplied step contract, write the handoff at its exact absolute report path,\\nand retain that contract and model pin on resume. A missing report refuses step completion.\\n\\n## Skills\\n\\nSkillset: `build-skillset` (pin: `.tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`).\\n- Once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin (it selects exact upstream code):\\n  `tink library fetch .tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`\\n- At stage open, compile the required disciplines, then start a NEW session so\\n  `AGENTS.md` is re-read: `tink use build-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/03-build`\\n  The launcher does this for you: `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 3`.\\n- For a capability gap: `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"`\\n  (searches the whole library; prints the skill on stdout. Exit 1 means nothing fits; exit 1 or 2 means\\n  continue without a skill).\\n\\nStage skills (always for this stage; `tink use` compiles the same set):\\n- `principle-build-the-lever`: Non-trivial work: build the script or tool that does or proves it, so a reviewer can rerun it, instead of doing it by hand.\\n- `unslop`: Any prose you write (docs, commit messages, replies): cut AI tells and filler before you send it.\\nWhen one of these applies, read that skill in full before acting on it. In the handoff note, name each skill that changed a decision and the decision it changed.\\n\"}\n==> text 4/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\",\"content\":\"# Change brief\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/864\\nIssue body sha256: 29f447833355967617805c1fc76789f53c35598279aa8b5a4f7ba921cf6aa3b1\\nTitle: Tend: Keep a raw project thought before any goal is confirmed\\n\\nThe approved issue below is this run's brief, unchanged. Each Done-when line is one item in `checklist.json`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Primary area\\n\\ntend\\n\\n### Kind\\n\\nenhancement\\n\\n### Goal\\n\\nThe operator needs one place per project to keep ideas, goals and thoughts, including thoughts that are not yet goals.\\nSaving a thought must not claim that the operator confirmed a goal.\\n\\n### Evidence\\n\\nOperator words, 2026-11-15, exact:\\n\\n> i just want a place that i can saturate my ideas/goals and thoughts in that are project specific and I can allow LLM to find surfaced connections, refactoring and determination if a goal(s) are ready for shaping. thats my main goal. I think idempotency is an idea that is relevant\\n\\n> think of an entry to tend be a neuron, and with enough neurons a action potential activates over those neuron connections, and then its refined and insulated better through shape like myelination and then then the action is tink-sdlc which is like the behavior..\\n\\nAsked whether Tend must keep a raw thought before any goal is confirmed from it, the operator answered: \\\"Yes, raw thoughts\\\".\\n\\nObserved in code at `04c2bc4`:\\n\\n- Tend lists saved sessions only. Think exits without confirmation leave no record (`docs/design/think.md`, Think record).\\n- `tinkery seed new` saves any text without Think or a model call, but records it as a confirmed goal (`src/shape/command.rs`, `new_goal`).\\n- That command writes no repository attribution. One active goal with unknown attribution makes Find connections refuse\\n  for every repository (`src/tend/connection.rs`, `snapshot_with`).\\n\\nProposal, not observation: a thought is a separate kind of entry, not a goal with a weaker label.\\nNot established: that more entries make connection discovery useful. The one real trial gave a false alarm.\\n\\n### Scope and boundaries\\n\\nOne outcome: the operator saves exact words as a thought in the current repository's garden, sees it there after restart,\\nand later turns it into a goal only through the existing goal confirmation.\\n\\nChanges:\\n\\n- A key in Tend's repository garden opens one exact-word editor. No Think conversation is needed.\\n- One explicit save of exact words, with the repository shown before saving. No model call.\\n- Thoughts appear in the repository garden, visibly distinct from goals with and without colour.\\n- A thought records where it was saved. It never has unknown attribution.\\n\\nStays unchanged:\\n\\n- Goal confirmation, seed confirmation and implementation permission. A thought is none of them.\\n- Shape accepts confirmed goals only. A thought cannot open Shape.\\n- Find connections input, its 20-goal and 32 KiB bounds, the saved correction and all saved-goal formats.\\n  Thoughts do not enter a provider request in this slice.\\n- No automatic interpretation, grouping, merging, readiness judgment, wiki or background work.\\n- No editing or deletion of a saved thought in this slice.\\n\\nSubtract first: reuse the exact-word editor, the repository boundary, the private atomic write and the garden view.\\nIf the build needs a new storage service, a session-format migration or a change to the shared goal saver, stop and report.\\n\\n### Done when\\n\\n- [ ] From the repository garden, the operator saves a thought with one disclosed action, and the saved words equal the typed words byte for byte.\\n- [ ] After restart, the thought is listed in that repository's garden, labelled as a thought and not as a goal.\\n- [ ] A saved thought has no goal confirmation record, cannot open Shape, and is absent from the Find connections request.\\n- [ ] Find connections still runs in a repository that holds thoughts. A thought never causes incomplete discovery.\\n- [ ] Saving the same exact words twice in one repository results in one entry, and the screen says so.\\n- [ ] Turning a thought into a goal uses the existing goal confirmation, keeps the thought's words as the original, and leaves the thought on record.\\n- [ ] Terminal journeys at 80×24 and 100×30 with colour off cover save, restart, duplicate save, cancel and an unwritable store, with temporary TEST roots and no provider.\\n- [ ] Reversion probes fail when a thought is recorded as a confirmed goal, when saved words differ from typed words, and when a thought reaches a provider request.\\n\\n### Context and dependencies\\n\\n- [Yohaku](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/yohaku.md), Articles 1 and 2.\\n- [Tend and handoff](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/shape-and-handoff.md#tend-garden-and-detail) and [Think](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/think.md).\\n- #860 and #862 are merged. This slice does not change them.\\n- #852 covers pruning and restoring goals. It is related and not a blocker.\\n- Later, separate slices: thoughts as Find connections input, volume above 20 entries, refactoring proposals,\\n  readiness judgments and dismissal that persists. None is part of this issue.\\n\\n### Open decisions and permissions\\n\\nDecided by the operator on 2026-11-15: a thought enters through a key in Tend's garden. Operator words: \\\"Key in Tend garden\\\".\\nThe key name, its label and its place in the key bar remain design details for review.\\n\\nDefaults proposed by the agent and accepted by the operator for this slice on 2026-11-15:\\n\\n1. A saved thought cannot be edited or removed in this slice. Pruning is separate work (#852).\\n2. \\\"Project\\\" means the launch repository, as goals use today.\\n3. A duplicate means the same exact bytes in the same repository. Whitespace and case differences make a different thought.\\n\\nEach default can change in a later slice. No other decision is open for this slice.\\n\\nThis issue records a design request. Publication, implementation, provider budget and real-data trials each need separate operator approval.\\n\\n\"}\n==> text 5/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"d50e7a87-1232-43e5-a29a-0cbf2512c1ab\\\",\\n  \\\"checkout\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought\\\",\\n  \\\"report\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/output/handoff.md\\\",\\n  \\\"model\\\": \\\"openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol:medium\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/_system/scripts/pi-launcher\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher_sha256\\\": \\\"d0c05547c00cf7882c0fef3d8e89d786fdd2a488b76413aa9159cd61183a6763\\\",\\n  \\\"state\\\": \\\"active\\\",\\n  \\\"session\\\": \\\"tsdlc-4e1822e7fd164f8b825f6555621346b6\\\",\\n  \\\"prepared_ns\\\": 1794731465469500000,\\n  \\\"max_responses\\\": 250,\\n  \\\"process_group\\\": 32154\\n}\\n\"}\n==> text 6/7 <==\n{\"output\":\" M AGENTS.md\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n7c134de stage 3 open: issue-864-tend-thought (approved artifacts and receipts)\\n04c2bc4 Merge pull request #863 from jon-devlapaz/codex/issue-862-tend-correction\\nba6606f Record independent review of Linux test repair\\ndade851 docs: record verified Linux correction test fix\\nc1728c6 test(tend): observe clear frame before large correction paste\\nissue-860-tend-connection\\nissue-862-tend-correction\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit_code\":0,\"wall_time_seconds\":0.1}\n==> text 7/7 <==\nCreated #1: Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation (pending)\nCreated #2: Implement the approved slice and regression checks (pending)\nCreated #3: Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes (pending)\n\n[Full output: /var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt (read with offset/limit)]"}],"details":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.3395000000000437},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.0922499999996944},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.6723750000001019},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.624958999998853},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.575790999999299},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","args":"{\"command\":\"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status\",\"timeout\":30}","status":"ok","durationMs":117.500082999999},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/7","name":"todo","args":"{\"action\":\"create\",\"tasks\":[{\"subject\":\"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation\"},{\"subject\":\"Implement the approved slice and regression checks\"},{\"subject\":\"Run checks, write han...","status":"ok","durationMs":1.6686250000002474}],"fullOutputPath":"/var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt"},"isError":false,"durationMs":190,"timestamp":1794731482775,"nestedCalls":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"AGENTS.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"_system/SDLC.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","status":"ok","arguments":{"command":"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status","timeout":30},"durationMs":117},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/7","name":"todo","status":"ok","arguments":{"action":"create","tasks":[{"subject":"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation"},{"subject":"Implement the approved slice and regression checks"},{"subject":"Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes"}]},"durationMs":2}],"complete":true}}}
{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367","name":"codemode","arguments":{"code":"const paths=[\"AGENTS.md\",\"_system/SDLC.md\",\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\",\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\",\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\"];\nfor(const p of paths) text({path:p,content:await tools.read({path:p})});\ntext(await tools.bash({command:\"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status\",timeout:30}));\ntext(await tools.todo({action:\"create\",tasks:[{subject:\"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation\"},{subject:\"Implement the approved slice and regression checks\"},{subject:\"Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes\"}]}));\n"}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","usage":{"input":1511,"output":174,"cacheRead":10112,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":11797,"cost":{"input":0.003022,"output":0.0017400000000000002,"cacheRead":0.0010112,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.005773200000000001,"totalFromProvider":false}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794731476366,"responseId":"resp_0df587760909a640016af96dd4a04487d189a0a064f968f90e","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":6217,"thinkingLevel":"medium"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367","toolName":"codemode","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 12027)\nTotal output lines: 16\n\n==> text 1/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\",\"content\":\"# Working on Tinkery\\n\\n**Read [docs/yohaku.md](docs/yohaku.md) first:** the constitution wins over guides,\\ndesigns and briefs. Only the operator amends it; report discrepancies, never edit it.\\n[README.md](README.md) describes shipped behavior.\\n\\n## Guardrails\\n\\n- Subtract first; say each thing once, plainly.\\n- Fewer, deeper modules, smaller interfaces. Check data once where it comes in, keep\\n  that guarantee in types, then just use it. Write one-off logic right where it's used\\n  instead of splitting it into a function ahead of time. Skip try/catch wherever you can.\\n- Check changeability: name the [architecture diagnostics](docs/design/architecture.md#design-score)\\n  moved. Module and `pub` comments state promises and refusals, not implementation;\\n  the build requires them.\\n- Preserve operator originals verbatim; readings are guesses.\\n  Keep the displayed, confirmed goal unchanged through saving and handoff.\\n- Navigation and copying never launch agents. Goal confirmation is neither seed\\n  confirmation nor implementation permission.\\n- Work from an issue on a branch and open a PR. Merge it yourself once the issue is approved\\n  (or the operator asked for the change), CI is green, there are no conflicts and a reviewer\\n  who did not write the change has checked it against the issue. The one exception is the\\n  [tink-sdlc bump](.github/workflows/tink-sdlc-bump.yml): it merges itself when CI passes and it\\n  is exactly tink-sdlc's own upgrade.\\n  If the operator asks for drafts only, create and merge nothing until they say so.\\n- Model and provider calls require an explicit budget. The default is zero.\\n  Eval commands are outside zero-call verification.\\n- Agent operators (Pi via herdr) always launch Tinkery with `--operator agent`.\\n- Never record real audio; voice tests use fake engines and recorders, never native addons.\\n- Use temporary TEST roots. Never write to `~/.local/share/seed-me` or\\n  `~/.local/share/tinkery`, or change operator sessions, installs, launcher, Pi,\\n  Pi Voice or the operator's Seed Me checkout.\\n- Fix a bug from its observed cause. After two failed fixes, add logging and collect\\n  observations; after three, stop and hand over a fresh brief: problem, goal, failed attempts.\\n- Read relevant tests and design before implementation; update changed decisions.\\n  Add regressions, prove important protections fail when reverted, then restore them.\\n  Tests assert what users observe through public interfaces; never add test-only access\\n  to private state. Drive Think through its input, not `Conversation`'s capped test accessors.\\n  Unowned ideas confer neither shipped behavior nor build permission.\\n- For agent-facing docs, use `writing-for-agents`: one rule per authoritative home,\\n  task detail behind pointers.\\n  Preserve safeguards and runnable commands; brevity is not permission to weaken them.\\n  `tests/test_documentation.py` checks links, headings, paths, options and reachability.\\n- Create each task's worktree at `~/dev/worktrees/<repo>/<branch>`, never inside a checkout\\n  or beside one.\\n- At rest, keep a clean `main` matching `origin/main` after a fetch, with no stashes,\\n  extra worktrees or leftover branches. Report open PRs instead of cleaning them up. Delete only\\n  merged branches (`git branch -d`); never force-delete branches, drop stashes or\\n  remove worktrees for cleanliness.\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nEach reply spends the operator's attention, so it must earn its place ([Yohaku](docs/yohaku.md)).\\nWrite in plain technical English, modelled on\\n[simple-english](https://github.com/arekhalpern/simple-english) (ASD-STE100):\\n\\n- Lead with what the operator must decide or do, then the result. Ask at most one question.\\n- Cut each line that does not change what the operator does next.\\n- Name the state of each claim: proposed, attempted, observed, checked or accepted.\\n  Say unknowns and failures plainly, with the next step.\\n- Quote the operator's words exactly, and mark your readings as guesses.\\n- Give one fact per sentence: at most 20 words for an instruction, 25 for a description.\\n  Use active voice and simple tenses.\\n- Write \\\"must\\\" for a requirement and \\\"can\\\" for a possibility, never \\\"should\\\", \\\"may\\\" or \\\"might\\\".\\n  Put a condition before its instruction: \\\"If CI fails, read the log.\\\"\\n- Keep one name for one thing. Write complete sentences with articles. No contractions,\\n  semicolons, arrow chains or filler that states no fact.\\n- Never change code, commands, identifiers or quoted errors.\\n\\n## Layout and design entry points\\n\\nRead only the relevant task contract from the\\n[documentation index](docs/README.md). The [architecture map](docs/design/architecture.md#modules-today)\\nshows module responsibilities.\\n\\n## Cold setup\\n\\nFresh Debian or Ubuntu clone with Bash and Git:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/cloud-setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe script installs missing prerequisites, stable Rust with rustfmt and Clippy, and Node.\\nIt prints the verification exports and can be rerun safely. It downloads only\\nprerequisite packages, not providers, microphones, native voice engines or speech models.\\nApt may need root or sudo.\\n\\nCI uses stable Rust: Ubuntu on pull requests, Ubuntu and macOS on `main`; use that updated stable for Clippy.\\nNever alter operator toolchains without approval. On macOS, provision Rust, Python 3\\nand Node separately (bootstrap refuses macOS). No external skill checkout or other\\ndeveloper's files are needed.\\n\\n## Full zero-call verification\\n\\n[The Rust test wrapper](scripts/rust-tests.py) runs the workspace in a fresh TEST HOME\\nand refuses default application storage.\\n\\nRun `python3 scripts/full-check.py` from the repository root after setup. Its shared\\nlaunch guard gives the exact block below a temporary TEST HOME; do not run binary\\nverification against the operator's HOME. Cold-clone CI runs the block when setup may change. [CI routing](scripts/ci-scope.py) and its\\n[regressions](tests/test_ci_scope.py) define prose-only exceptions and fail-closed gates;\\ncode changes require the full block. [The Python runner](scripts/python-tests.py) runs files\\nin parallel; with `TINKERY_TEST_TIER=pr`, as on pull requests, it skips only the slow journeys it\\nlists, which still run on every push to `main`.\\n\\nNode is only for `voice/*.test.mjs` and `pi/*.test.mjs`. Keep the invalid `TINKERY_NODE` for voice PTYs;\\nPython journeys use the real binary, stub providers and temporary roots.\\n\\n<!-- full-check:start -->\\n```bash\\nexport PATH=\\\"${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}/bin:$PWD/.cloud-tools/node/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nexport TINKERY_NODE=/tinkery-TEST-no-node\\nrustc +stable --version\\nnode --version\\npython3 --version\\ncargo +stable build --locked\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --home --snapshot\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --snapshot --no-color\\npython3 scripts/rust-tests.py\\nnode --test voice/*.test.mjs pi/*.test.mjs\\npython3 scripts/python-tests.py\\ncargo +stable fmt --all -- --check\\ncargo +stable clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\\n```\\n<!-- full-check:end -->\\n\\n[Try it](README.md#try-it) without the installed launcher. `--snapshot` prints\\n100×30 without terminal mode or provider calls; `NO_COLOR=1` or `--no-color` retain non-color\\ncues. [Terminal setup](docs/terminal-setup.md#launch) has isolated real-drafting instructions.\\n\\n## Work intake\\n\\nThe board is [tinkery](https://github.com/users/jon-devlapaz/projects/2); its automations add\\ntinkery issues and set Done on close. Cloud sessions can't reach it; use `gh` on the operator's Mac.\\n\\nFile an issue only for work that serves Tinkery, after searching for an open duplicate, using the\\n[task template](.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/task.yml) with one `area:` and one kind label. Show the draft\\nand publish only on the operator's word; publication is not approval. Parallel work needs approved,\\nnon-blocking items with separate `area:` labels.\\n\\nUnapproved work stays `needs-approval`. `approved` records scoped operator permission,\\nnever grants it; material scope changes return to `needs-approval`. Publication, design\\nacceptance, implementation, data use and budget are separate decisions.\\n\\nUse one branch and PR per approved slice; link the issue, stay within its scope and fill in the\\n[PR template](.github/pull_request_template.md). Close with\\ncompletion evidence or an explicit reason. Report Rust, Node and Python counts, PTY\\njourneys and sizes, failures and reversion probes separately from operator dogfood:\\nstubs prove mechanics, not real-dump goal quality.\\n\\n\\n<!-- AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n## SDLC Workspace\\n- Read `_system/SDLC.md` for setup, evidence boundaries, and recovery.\\n- Inspect `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status` before creating a run.\\n- Read `stages/<stage-name>/CONTEXT.md` before processing a stage.\\n- Keep factory references in `_shared/` unchanged during feature runs.\\n- Use separate worktrees or clones for code-writing runs.\\n- Stage skills: see the Skills section of the current stage's CONTEXT.md.\\n- Need a specialised skill mid-task? `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"` prints it on stdout; exit 1 means none fits, so continue without one.\\n<!-- End AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nOnly respond to the user using ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English and the Bottom Line Up Front (BLUF) approach. Use SBAR for handovers and status updates. These do not apply to code or when the user asks otherwise\\n\\n<!-- tink:rules begin skillset=build-skillset digest=1f3d3f07b904318d51e96afd8174591e82cabe8ec2da6668bf084aa4d683d4c1 -->\\nDiscipline rules for this phase (compiled by tink; do not edit by hand):\\n- principle-build-the-lever: Apply to any non-trivial work, not just bulk work: edits, migrations, analyses, checks. Build the tool that does it or proves it (codemod, script, generator, or a skill your subagents follow) instead of working by hand. The tool is the artifact a reviewer can rerun. (read: tink mount principle-build-the-lever --json --payload)\\n- unslop: Cut AI tells from any writing. Must always apply. (read: tink mount unslop --json --payload)\\n<!-- tink:rules end -->\\n\"}\n==> text 2/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\",\"content\":\"# SDLC workspace\\n\\nA small filesystem workflow: define a change, implement and verify it in an isolated checkout, then obtain independent release review. Requires Python 3.9+, Bash, and Git on a POSIX system. Native Windows is not supported. Set `require_tink: true` when project policy requires Tink integrity checks.\\n\\n**Boundaries, said once.** Receipts, digests, locks and reviewer names are local workflow records: they detect mistakes and changes, not tampering, and authenticate no one. Checks run real commands, never in a sandbox; review them before approval. Nothing here authorizes a release: trusted CI, protected branches and independent forge review do. Never invent a reviewer or approval, and never hand-edit generated records.\\n\\n## Start a run\\n\\nRun scripts from any working directory; paths resolve from the script location.\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --kind bug\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status fix-example\\n```\\n\\nThe default `light` profile creates `brief.md` (problem, acceptance criteria, approach, risks, verification) and `checklist.json`. A human picks `--profile full` for consequential architecture or policy changes; it creates intent, spec and plan. Only runs created by `new` are supported, and text approval tags are not evidence.\\n\\nTo build an approved GitHub issue, let the issue be the definition:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --issue https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/issues/NUMBER\\n```\\n\\nIt reads the issue once with `gh` and calls no model. `brief.md` holds the issue's title, address, body hash and body unchanged; `checklist.json` holds one attested item for each line under the issue's `Done when` heading, unchanged. The `Done when` section must hold only top-level `-` or `*` lines, one line each, so no approved word is cut or dropped. It refuses a closed issue, an issue without the `approved` label, a Done-when section with no lines or any other content, an unreadable issue and `--profile full`, and then creates no run. `run.json` keeps the issue's address and body hash as a record. It records no decision: stage 3 still needs the human decision.\\n\\nWithout a confirmed seed, review the checklist commands and offer a dry run before definition approval (seed-bound runs use the [seed definition comparison](#seed-definition-comparison)):\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py dry-run fix-example\\n```\\n\\nIt runs every checklist `check` in the repository root with its timeout, replacing `runs/<slug>/checklist-dry-run.log` with launch errors, timeouts and nonzero exits per item. No check process survives its check: each starts in a new session, and on normal exit or timeout the rest of its process group is killed and reaped. Tell an expected reproduction failure from a broken command or environment (such as an unsuitable `TMPDIR`), fix the latter and rerun. The dry run records no decision, mark or receipt, does not enforce a new approval prerequisite, and does not replace `verify`, which still requires current approval and passing checks.\\n\\nAfter the human accepts the brief, record the actual review reference:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py decide fix-example 3 approved \\\\\\n  --reviewer 'Reviewer name' --source 'Actual review URL or decision reference' \\\\\\n  --reason 'Accepted scope, approach, and acceptance criteria'\\n```\\n\\nLight runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3: `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. Full runs record stages 1, 2 and 3 in order. `changes-requested` records rejection with the same command. Decisions bind their artifacts and contract inputs; edits stale them.\\n\\n`decide` and seed-judge `mark` are human-only: they refuse a reviewer starting with `Agent`, containing `not human`, or an agent actor (`--actor`, or run metadata). Older agent records stay readable, labelled `agent-recorded`, and never count as human acceptance.\\n\\n## Checklist\\n\\n`runs/<slug>/checklist.json` holds item definitions only: `{\\\"schema\\\":1,\\\"items\\\":[{\\\"id\\\",\\\"description\\\",\\\"verify\\\",\\\"check\\\"?}]}`. They are approved scope: stage 3 cannot be approved with zero or invalid items, and changing them stales that approval. Never add status fields. If the file is deleted, status reads MISSING and verification refuses until it is restored from Git.\\n\\n- **Checked items** carry `check`: `{\\\"argv\\\":[...],\\\"timeout_seconds\\\":N}`. `verify` runs them in the repository root after the configured checks, logging to `test-log.md`; a failure fails verification (`Checklist check failed: <id>`). A pass proves the item, so `mark` is refused for it.\\n- **Attested items** have no `check`: `sdlc.py mark <run> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>` appends a receipt under `marks/` (needs current stage 3 approval; latest wins; `verify` requires `passed`). Verification binds each latest mark, so mark first, then verify. Attestations are self-reported claims for review to challenge. Status flags a mark made on an older candidate; re-check it if the change affects it.\\n\\nWhen every command passes but attestations are missing, `verify` exits nonzero with `Checklist incomplete`, the mark commands to run, and `result: awaiting-attestation` with `pending_attestations`. That is machine proof only, not a pass. It stays current while its proof inputs and log match (evidence-only commits keep it); a mark or changed input retires it. An item a person marked `failed` makes the result `failed`. Record the actual observations, then rerun `verify`.\\n\\n### Locked seed acceptance checks\\n\\nAt stage-1 intake, use the explicitly supplied session and a trusted Tinkery executable:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 1 \\\\\\n  --seed-contract <session>/seed-contract.md \\\\\\n  --tinkery <path>/tinkery\\n```\\n\\nTinkery is the single seed verifier. Intake runs exactly `PATH seed status --json DIR` and `PATH seed checks DIR` (DIR is the seed's session directory) and reads status again to detect changes. Tinkery verifies human v2 and agent v3 confirmations against the seed; intake accepts v3 only with `operator: agent` and an agent `actor`, which records agent confirmation, never human confirmation. Missing executables, failed commands, malformed output, simulated sessions, missing confirmation and changed bindings refuse before any write. Supply trusted toolchain code, never a seed-provided script. Verification proves content binding, not operator identity or implementation approval.\\n\\nIntake copies the confirmed seed and its records into locked `runs/<slug>/seed-checks.json` items with reserved `seed-N` IDs; run metadata keeps the confirmation receipt and the original definitions' digest. Seed Markdown is never read as checks. Imported items are **LOCKED, seed-confirmed** definitions, distinct from the worker's `checklist.json`. Never silently weaken or remove acceptance: such edits are departures. Deleting the file or changing the run-local seed refuses verification. Later edits to the original session do not reach the run-local snapshot; to rebind, run verified intake again; rebinding replaces activations, and prior evidence stays in Git.\\n\\n#### Seed definition comparison\\n\\nStage 3 compares effective checks with the intake baseline. With no added, removed or changed checks, and no activation other than the confirmed proposal, it passes without another human question and records `matches confirmed seed (sha256: …)` as evidence, not a human decision. This is structural, not a claim about the brief or permission to implement. An explicit human `changes-requested` stays blocking until a later human decision supersedes it.\\n\\nAny departure blocks automatic passage. Status lists each changed field, removed or added check, added worker item and non-proposal activation, and asks only `Accept these departures from the confirmed seed?`. `decide … 3` records the human answer bound to those exact inputs. Full-profile stages 1 and 2 keep their own reviews. Legacy bindings without a stored baseline compare against the intake digest; if their definitions changed, rebind rather than invent a baseline.\\n\\nSeed record kinds:\\n\\n- **`cmd`** runs verbatim through `bash -c` with a 120-second timeout, or `seed_timeout_seconds` from `_system/verification.json`; `cwd` is relative to the checkout (absolute paths, traversal, backslashes, drive letters, NUL and CR/LF refused). Failures and timeouts fail `verify` even when all worker checks pass. **Passing output proves a `cmd` or activated proposal entirely; no `seed-N-expect` mark is owed.** `expect` is descriptive context, not a matcher.\\n- **`judge`** (prose or taste) needs a manual human attestation, judged by `how_to_judge` or else the statement, never an automated pass. Record only the actual operator's observation:\\n\\n  ```sh\\n  python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py mark <slug> seed-1 passed \\\\\\n    --reviewer 'Actual operator' --source 'Actual observation reference' \\\\\\n    --evidence 'Observed output and why it satisfies the seed expectation'\\n  ```\\n\\n  Evidence already used for another item is refused unless `--covers-all` says the operator meant it for each. Mark `unknown` when the observation does not address the expectation; `unknown` blocks verification. Marks need current definition approval and bind the exact item.\\n- **`provisional`** names its prerequisite in `missing`; `cmd` is an optional proposal. It stays pending and nonblocking, never proven, while the prerequisite is absent. At implementation time, inspect the prerequisite. **Once it exists**, add only an `activation` object to that item in `seed-checks.json`, keeping the proposal intact:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\\"activation\\\": {\\\"cmd\\\":…2027 tokens truncated…ython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py verify`.\\n- The reviewer works in a separate checkout, writes `runs/<slug>/05-deploy/output/REVIEW-findings.md`,\\n  and changes no code or evidence. The candidate's owner brings the findings to the PR branch.\\n\\n## Pinned build steps\\n\\n`stage` prepares a checkout and prints a prompt; it never starts an agent. For an explicitly\\nauthorized build agent, open stage 3, then, from that checkout, take these deliberate steps one at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build prepare \\\\\\n  --report /absolute/run-notes/build-report.md \\\\\\n  --model provider/exact-model-id --launcher /absolute/trusted-adapter [--max-responses N]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build show\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build launch\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build resume\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete --stopped-by operator  # or cap\\n```\\n\\n- `prepare` writes an immutable `runs/<run>/steps/<name>.json` binding the checkout, the exact model,\\n  the launcher's path and SHA-256, and a fresh absolute report path whose parent exists. It starts nothing.\\n  It refuses existing names or reports, relative paths, model aliases, and a run branch checked out\\n  elsewhere. Contracts are not portable: prepare a new step in a new checkout.\\n- `--max-responses N` caps responses; an adapter that cannot enforce it refuses, so a cap is never ignored.\\n- `launch` and `resume` recheck every definition gate and resolve the exact model offline first. The\\n  prompt names the approved inputs, `stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md` and the report path; limits writes to\\n  the checkout and report; tells the worker to commit before ending each turn; and forbids `sdlc.py step`, `stage` and `decide`. Inside the session\\n  `TINK_SDLC_STEP_SESSION=1` makes `step` refuse everything but `show`. Resume keeps the same session.\\n- `complete` needs a nonempty report at the exact path and the adapter's offline confirmation that the\\n  agent's latest turn ended on the pin. A paused, failed or running agent leaves the step `active`.\\n  `--stopped-by operator|cap` ends the step's process group and records it `stopped`, never as an ended turn.\\n- `show` adds `observed_turn` (`ended`, `not ended` or `unknown`) from an offline probe.\\n- A crash or failed admission leaves the step `uncertain`, never retried. Inspect the real session and\\n  reconcile it before preparing another step; never hand-edit the contract.\\n\\nA report is not proof of passing checks, authorship or approval; `verify` and forge gates stay independent.\\n\\nBundled adapters (their module comments state what each promises and refuses):\\n\\n| Adapter | Pin | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `_system/scripts/pi-launcher` | `provider/model-id:thinking`, e.g. `openai-codex/gpt-5.6-luna:low` | The model must be in Pi's local catalog (run `pi update --models` yourself) with a stored credential. Enforces `--max-responses`. |\\n\\nResume pinned steps only through `step … resume`: pi-herdr's resume drops the pin. Another launcher\\nmust implement the [launcher protocol](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/launcher-protocol.md)\\nand pass offline-resolution tests before it gets a live-call budget.\\n\\n## Stage skills\\n\\nEach stage has a skillset pin committed at `.tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json`: 01-plan `planning-skillset`, 02-design `design-skillset`, 03-build `build-skillset`, 04-test `testing-skillset`, 05-deploy `deployment-skillset`, 06-maintain `maintenance-skillset`. A pin selects exact upstream code, so review a pin change like a lockfile change.\\n\\n```sh\\ntink library fetch .tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json    # once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin\\ntink use <name>-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/<stage-dir>  # at stage open, then start a NEW session\\ntink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`required` lists the disciplines `tink use` compiles into `AGENTS.md` at stage open. Capability skills come only through `tink-route` for genuine gaps. It searches the whole library (`--anywhere`, the default; `--skillset NAME` restricts it, and exit 1 may then add `Hint: <skill> fits but is on another shelf`). Exit 1 or 2 means continue without a skill. Keep its receipt with the run and read the delivered skill before relying on it. `tink-route` mounts into the git-ignored `.tink/.active` and changes no project skills.\\n\\nCommit `.tink/skills.toml` and `.tink/skills.lock` when Tink creates them through an authorized operation; restore them into each worktree through Tink, never by symlink. Run `tink` mutations through the wrapper, which serializes them on this host and rejects symlinked skill state:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py skills tink -- skill check\\n```\\n\\nTinkery's verified confirmed seed contract is the input to 01-plan (bind it as in [Locked seed acceptance checks](#locked-seed-acceptance-checks)). `tink use` and `tink-route` are optional; runs work without them.\\n\\n## Recovery and release\\n\\nRuns have one writer at a time; a busy or interrupted operation fails with its lock path. After a crash, confirm no process owns the operation, remove the lock directory and rerun.\\n\\nOpen the PR once the candidate is committed and verification is current (end of stage 4; `stage <slug> 5` enforces it). Delivery and merge authority belong to `stages/05-deploy/CONTEXT.md`. Keep deployed revision, result and rollback references in the deployment system.\\n\\nRecord the run's ending:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py pr <run> <PR URL>   # needs current verification; one PR per run\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py label <run> --kind correction|none|accepted|rejected --words TEXT \\\\\\n  --reviewer NAME --source REF [--about FILE|outside] [--consent local|shareable]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py close <run> --outcome merged --merge <full commit>   # or --outcome abandoned\\n```\\n\\nPut `Tink-Run: <run_id>` in the PR description. Labels are human-only (refused inside a build step session), keep the words verbatim, and chain to each other by digest; `none` records no corrections and `outside` a correction outside the run's records. `close` needs a label (and, when merged, the recorded PR); a run closes once. While the run builds in another checkout, these refuse and name it. Closing in a linked worktree copies the run's records into the main checkout's `runs/<run>/` (refusing if that folder has uncommitted changes or another run); commit them there.\\n\\nMaintenance is optional intake. Enable it only with metric thresholds, deduplication, cooldowns and run limits; alerts create drafts for triage, never approvals.\\n\\n## Machine interface (API 1)\\n\\nClients read `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py capabilities --json` and `status [<run>] --json` from the selected checkout's runtime, check `protocol`, `api_version` and capabilities first, and fail closed on unknown states, never falling back to the human CLI output. The field contract is in [references/api-1.md](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/api-1.md).\\n\\n## Maintaining the scaffold\\n\\n`python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py walk [--json]` is a read-only structural check that a memoryless agent can orient from the files: router size, resolving pointers, stage contracts naming Inputs, Output and a human Gate, per-stage context size, skillset pins, `status` for every run, and current `tink:rules` blocks. A real walk by a fresh agent stays the test.\\n\\nPreview, then apply an upgrade (`_system/scaffold.json` is the trust baseline):\\n\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade --check\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade\\n\\nUnmodified managed files are updated, restored or removed; project-owned files (`_system/verification.json`, `.tink/skillsets/*.json`) are never overwritten. A customized managed file blocks the upgrade and is listed with a `diff`: merge by hand, or commit and rerun with `--overwrite-customized`. Only the `AGENTS.md` router block is replaced; `runs/` is never modified, but changed stage 1-3 contracts stale existing approvals. Downgrades are refused, and an interrupted upgrade is safe to rerun. A killed install can leave a partial one: inspect it and its lock, confirm no writer remains, and recover through a reviewed migration, never a forced overwrite. Never update factory files during an active feature run. Afterward run `walk`.\\n\\nTemplates live in `_shared/` (`brief-template.md` is the light-profile edit surface); run outputs go under `runs/`. Read the active contract and its immediate inputs, not the whole factory.\\n\"}\n==> text 3/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\",\"content\":\"# Stage 03: Implement\\n\\nInputs: current approved spec for full runs, or combined brief for light runs. A seed-bound run's\\nconfirmed `seed-contract.md` and locked checks define it; its `brief.md` may stay the template.\\nOutput: `runs/<slug>/03-build/output/plan.md` for full runs, the approved checklist definitions\\nin `runs/<slug>/checklist.json` for both profiles, and code in an isolated worktree/clone.\\nItems should carry a `check` whenever an automated proof exists; `verify` runs it and no mark is needed.\\nWhen a pending provisional seed check's prerequisite exists, follow the guide's [provisional activation step](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison) and report the activation; a command other than the confirmed proposal needs operator stage-3 approval.\\n\\nGate: inspect code and follow the stage 3 gate before implementation; for seed-bound\\nruns use the guide's [seed definition comparison](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison).\\nFor full runs, write the plan first. Light runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3 (`sdlc.py decide <run> 3 ...`): the approved `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. An explicit user instruction to execute a reviewed proposal is\\nimplementation authority; never fabricate separate role approvals.\\nEach concurrent writer needs its own checkout. Use the serialized skill wrapper\\nin `_system/SDLC.md`; no shared writable skill symlinks or automatic lockfile rewrites.\\n\\nFor bug fixes, reproduce the expected failure first, obtain independent acceptance,\\nand record protected test inputs with `sdlc.py lock-tests`. Implement and verify in\\na loop with stage 04. Update the plan when scope changes and renew stale decisions.\\nMark items only with `sdlc.py mark <slug> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>`; never hand-edit receipts.\\nFor launcher-managed build agents, use the guide's [pinned build steps](../../_system/SDLC.md#pinned-build-steps).\\nRead the supplied step contract, write the handoff at its exact absolute report path,\\nand retain that contract and model pin on resume. A missing report refuses step completion.\\n\\n## Skills\\n\\nSkillset: `build-skillset` (pin: `.tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`).\\n- Once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin (it selects exact upstream code):\\n  `tink library fetch .tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`\\n- At stage open, compile the required disciplines, then start a NEW session so\\n  `AGENTS.md` is re-read: `tink use build-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/03-build`\\n  The launcher does this for you: `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 3`.\\n- For a capability gap: `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"`\\n  (searches the whole library; prints the skill on stdout. Exit 1 means nothing fits; exit 1 or 2 means\\n  continue without a skill).\\n\\nStage skills (always for this stage; `tink use` compiles the same set):\\n- `principle-build-the-lever`: Non-trivial work: build the script or tool that does or proves it, so a reviewer can rerun it, instead of doing it by hand.\\n- `unslop`: Any prose you write (docs, commit messages, replies): cut AI tells and filler before you send it.\\nWhen one of these applies, read that skill in full before acting on it. In the handoff note, name each skill that changed a decision and the decision it changed.\\n\"}\n==> text 4/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\",\"content\":\"# Change brief\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/864\\nIssue body sha256: 29f447833355967617805c1fc76789f53c35598279aa8b5a4f7ba921cf6aa3b1\\nTitle: Tend: Keep a raw project thought before any goal is confirmed\\n\\nThe approved issue below is this run's brief, unchanged. Each Done-when line is one item in `checklist.json`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Primary area\\n\\ntend\\n\\n### Kind\\n\\nenhancement\\n\\n### Goal\\n\\nThe operator needs one place per project to keep ideas, goals and thoughts, including thoughts that are not yet goals.\\nSaving a thought must not claim that the operator confirmed a goal.\\n\\n### Evidence\\n\\nOperator words, 2026-11-15, exact:\\n\\n> i just want a place that i can saturate my ideas/goals and thoughts in that are project specific and I can allow LLM to find surfaced connections, refactoring and determination if a goal(s) are ready for shaping. thats my main goal. I think idempotency is an idea that is relevant\\n\\n> think of an entry to tend be a neuron, and with enough neurons a action potential activates over those neuron connections, and then its refined and insulated better through shape like myelination and then then the action is tink-sdlc which is like the behavior..\\n\\nAsked whether Tend must keep a raw thought before any goal is confirmed from it, the operator answered: \\\"Yes, raw thoughts\\\".\\n\\nObserved in code at `04c2bc4`:\\n\\n- Tend lists saved sessions only. Think exits without confirmation leave no record (`docs/design/think.md`, Think record).\\n- `tinkery seed new` saves any text without Think or a model call, but records it as a confirmed goal (`src/shape/command.rs`, `new_goal`).\\n- That command writes no repository attribution. One active goal with unknown attribution makes Find connections refuse\\n  for every repository (`src/tend/connection.rs`, `snapshot_with`).\\n\\nProposal, not observation: a thought is a separate kind of entry, not a goal with a weaker label.\\nNot established: that more entries make connection discovery useful. The one real trial gave a false alarm.\\n\\n### Scope and boundaries\\n\\nOne outcome: the operator saves exact words as a thought in the current repository's garden, sees it there after restart,\\nand later turns it into a goal only through the existing goal confirmation.\\n\\nChanges:\\n\\n- A key in Tend's repository garden opens one exact-word editor. No Think conversation is needed.\\n- One explicit save of exact words, with the repository shown before saving. No model call.\\n- Thoughts appear in the repository garden, visibly distinct from goals with and without colour.\\n- A thought records where it was saved. It never has unknown attribution.\\n\\nStays unchanged:\\n\\n- Goal confirmation, seed confirmation and implementation permission. A thought is none of them.\\n- Shape accepts confirmed goals only. A thought cannot open Shape.\\n- Find connections input, its 20-goal and 32 KiB bounds, the saved correction and all saved-goal formats.\\n  Thoughts do not enter a provider request in this slice.\\n- No automatic interpretation, grouping, merging, readiness judgment, wiki or background work.\\n- No editing or deletion of a saved thought in this slice.\\n\\nSubtract first: reuse the exact-word editor, the repository boundary, the private atomic write and the garden view.\\nIf the build needs a new storage service, a session-format migration or a change to the shared goal saver, stop and report.\\n\\n### Done when\\n\\n- [ ] From the repository garden, the operator saves a thought with one disclosed action, and the saved words equal the typed words byte for byte.\\n- [ ] After restart, the thought is listed in that repository's garden, labelled as a thought and not as a goal.\\n- [ ] A saved thought has no goal confirmation record, cannot open Shape, and is absent from the Find connections request.\\n- [ ] Find connections still runs in a repository that holds thoughts. A thought never causes incomplete discovery.\\n- [ ] Saving the same exact words twice in one repository results in one entry, and the screen says so.\\n- [ ] Turning a thought into a goal uses the existing goal confirmation, keeps the thought's words as the original, and leaves the thought on record.\\n- [ ] Terminal journeys at 80×24 and 100×30 with colour off cover save, restart, duplicate save, cancel and an unwritable store, with temporary TEST roots and no provider.\\n- [ ] Reversion probes fail when a thought is recorded as a confirmed goal, when saved words differ from typed words, and when a thought reaches a provider request.\\n\\n### Context and dependencies\\n\\n- [Yohaku](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/yohaku.md), Articles 1 and 2.\\n- [Tend and handoff](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/shape-and-handoff.md#tend-garden-and-detail) and [Think](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/think.md).\\n- #860 and #862 are merged. This slice does not change them.\\n- #852 covers pruning and restoring goals. It is related and not a blocker.\\n- Later, separate slices: thoughts as Find connections input, volume above 20 entries, refactoring proposals,\\n  readiness judgments and dismissal that persists. None is part of this issue.\\n\\n### Open decisions and permissions\\n\\nDecided by the operator on 2026-11-15: a thought enters through a key in Tend's garden. Operator words: \\\"Key in Tend garden\\\".\\nThe key name, its label and its place in the key bar remain design details for review.\\n\\nDefaults proposed by the agent and accepted by the operator for this slice on 2026-11-15:\\n\\n1. A saved thought cannot be edited or removed in this slice. Pruning is separate work (#852).\\n2. \\\"Project\\\" means the launch repository, as goals use today.\\n3. A duplicate means the same exact bytes in the same repository. Whitespace and case differences make a different thought.\\n\\nEach default can change in a later slice. No other decision is open for this slice.\\n\\nThis issue records a design request. Publication, implementation, provider budget and real-data trials each need separate operator approval.\\n\\n\"}\n==> text 5/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"d50e7a87-1232-43e5-a29a-0cbf2512c1ab\\\",\\n  \\\"checkout\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought\\\",\\n  \\\"report\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/output/handoff.md\\\",\\n  \\\"model\\\": \\\"openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol:medium\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/_system/scripts/pi-launcher\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher_sha256\\\": \\\"d0c05547c00cf7882c0fef3d8e89d786fdd2a488b76413aa9159cd61183a6763\\\",\\n  \\\"state\\\": \\\"active\\\",\\n  \\\"session\\\": \\\"tsdlc-4e1822e7fd164f8b825f6555621346b6\\\",\\n  \\\"prepared_ns\\\": 1794731465469500000,\\n  \\\"max_responses\\\": 250,\\n  \\\"process_group\\\": 32154\\n}\\n\"}\n==> text 6/7 <==\n{\"output\":\" M AGENTS.md\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n7c134de stage 3 open: issue-864-tend-thought (approved artifacts and receipts)\\n04c2bc4 Merge pull request #863 from jon-devlapaz/codex/issue-862-tend-correction\\nba6606f Record independent review of Linux test repair\\ndade851 docs: record verified Linux correction test fix\\nc1728c6 test(tend): observe clear frame before large correction paste\\nissue-860-tend-connection\\nissue-862-tend-correction\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit_code\":0,\"wall_time_seconds\":0.1}\n==> text 7/7 <==\nCreated #1: Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation (pending)\nCreated #2: Implement the approved slice and regression checks (pending)\nCreated #3: Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes (pending)\n\n[Full output: /var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt (read with offset/limit)]"}],"details":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.3395000000000437},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.0922499999996944},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.6723750000001019},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.624958999998853},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.575790999999299},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","args":"{\"command\":\"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status\",\"timeout\":30}","status":"ok","durationMs":117.500082999999},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/7","name":"todo","args":"{\"action\":\"create\",\"tasks\":[{\"subject\":\"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation\"},{\"subject\":\"Implement the approved slice and regression checks\"},{\"subject\":\"Run checks, write han...","status":"ok","durationMs":1.6686250000002474}],"fullOutputPath":"/var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt"},"isError":false,"durationMs":190,"timestamp":1794731482775,"nestedCalls":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"AGENTS.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"_system/SDLC.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","status":"ok","arguments":{"command":"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status","timeout":30},"durationMs":117},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efad