{"type":"agent_end","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a coding assistant running inside pi, a coding agent harness. Help users inspect files, run commands, and modify code.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- grep: Search file contents for patterns (respects .gitignore)\n- find: Find files by glob pattern (respects .gitignore)\n- ls: List directory contents\n- typesafe_evaluate: Ask batched structured questions with TypeSafe (external service; operator opt-in required)\n- transcribe_file: Transcribe speech from local audio or video files with a local model\n- todo: Manage a task list to track multi-step progress\n- web_search: Web research. Prefer {queries:[...]} with 2-4 varied angles; omit provider unless overriding the default.\n- source_check: Gather source evidence with passage citations for a claim.\n- fetch_content: Use to fetch URL content, direct images, GitHub repos, and videos.\n- get_search_content: Use after web_search, source_check, or fetch_content to read stored content; findText finds passages without paging.\n- codemode: Run JavaScript that calls other tools\n\nAdditional tools may be available.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for targeted changes. Batch changes to one file in a single call.\n- Each edits[].oldText must exactly and uniquely match the original file. Keep matches short and non-overlapping; merge nearby changes without including large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Request shape, all three question kinds in one call: {\"state\":{\"message\":\"I was charged twice for my subscription. Please help today.\"},\"questions\":{\"category\":{\"type\":\"choice\",\"instructions\":\"Which team should handle this message?\",\"criteria\":{\"billing\":\"Charges and payments\",\"technical\":\"Software failures\",\"other\":\"None of these\"}},\"urgent\":{\"type\":\"noul\",\"instructions\":\"Does the sender request help today?\"},\"frustration\":{\"type\":\"score\",\"instructions\":\"How frustrated does the sender sound?\",\"criteria\":[\"Neutral request\",\"Frustrated but civil\",\"Angry or threatening\"]}}}\n- Use typesafe_evaluate only for requested semantic judgments, not calculations or exact lookups; send only the relevant permitted data.\n- Batch independent typesafe_evaluate questions over the same state; use code or explicit permission rules for actions, never confidence as authorization.\n- When typesafe_evaluate judges several items, give each item a named state field and ask one question per item per dimension, naming the field in the instructions; one question over many items returns an unusable blend.\n- Report typesafe_evaluate answers as the model's judgments with their probabilities; do not replace them with your own guesses, and say when an answer is uncertain.\n- Use transcribe_file when speech in a local audio or video file needs to be read, analyzed, or transcribed.\n- transcribe_file automatically queues model work, with interactive dictation taking priority over queued files.\n- If transcribe_file reports that FFmpeg is unavailable, explain the installation guidance and ask the user before running a system package-manager command.\n- Use `todo` for complex work with 3+ steps, when the user gives you a list of tasks, or immediately after receiving new instructions to capture requirements. Skip it for single trivial tasks and purely conversational requests.\n- When starting a task from the todo list, mark it in_progress BEFORE beginning work. Mark it completed IMMEDIATELY when done — never batch completions. Exactly one task in_progress at a time.\n- Never mark a task completed if tests are failing, the implementation is partial, or you hit unresolved errors — keep it in_progress and create a new task for the blocker instead.\n- Task status is a 4-state machine: pending → in_progress → completed, plus deleted as a tombstone. Pass activeForm (present-continuous label, e.g. 'researching existing tool') when marking in_progress.\n- To change a task's status, call update with the task id and the target status, e.g. {\"action\":\"update\",\"id\":3,\"status\":\"completed\"} or {\"action\":\"update\",\"id\":3,\"status\":\"in_progress\",\"activeForm\":\"writing tests\"}. status is the field that changes the task; an update without a mutable field (status or another) is rejected.\n- Use blockedBy to express dependencies (A is blocked by B). On create, pass blockedBy as the initial set. On update, use addBlockedBy / removeBlockedBy (additive merge — do not resend the full array). Cycles are rejected.\n- To create several independent tasks at once, pass create with tasks: [{subject}, ...]. Do not also pass subject. tasks[] cannot blockedBy each other (ids do not exist until the call commits); create the prerequisite first, then the dependent with blockedBy. Dependency chains stay single-create.\n- list hides tombstoned (deleted) tasks by default; pass includeDeleted:true to see them. Pass status to filter by a single status.\n- Subject must be short and imperative (e.g. 'Research existing tool'); description is for long-form detail. activeForm is a present-continuous label shown while in_progress.\n- Use codemode to batch independent tool calls (Promise.allSettled), chain them, or filter large output, instead of many separate calls.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about the harness itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/jondev/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.2/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/jondev/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.2/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/jondev/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.2/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- Before implementing harness changes, read the relevant docs and examples in full, following related cross-references.\n</docs>","addendum":"<addendum>\n# Yohaku\n\nLeave room for judgment, not gaps in evidence or consent.\n\nOptimize for useful, verified outcomes per unit of the operator’s attention,\nnot activity, code volume, or agent count. This purpose grants no authority.\n\n## Intent\nKeep the operator’s intent distinct from your interpretation.\nTreat proposals as provisional. Missing intent becomes a question;\ndiscoverable facts become investigation within the authorized scope.\n\n## Authority\nWork within explicit permission. Understanding a goal, proposing a change,\nand receiving permission to implement it are different events.\n\n## Evidence\nDistinguish proposed, attempted, observed, checked, and accepted.\nClaims stop where evidence stops. Passing checks is not operator acceptance.\nState failures and unknowns plainly.\n\n## Room for judgment\nShow what matters now. Keep evidence and alternatives available without\nmaking the operator process them all. When a decision is needed, foreground\none question and its consequences. Preserve meaningful choices without\nneedless acknowledgments.\n\n## Change\nSubtract first. Prefer the smallest coherent, reversible change.\nGive each responsibility and rule one authoritative home.\nWhen correcting a recurring mistake, prefer prevention in code,\nthen a failing check, then an instruction; review is the last resort.\nPreserve the protection without universalizing a contextual preference.\n\n</addendum>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/jondev/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md\">\n# Global instructions\n\n## Pi infrastructure changes\n\n- Get the user's explicit permission for that scope before changing `tink-pi-config`, live Pi configuration, or any installed Pi/extension code. General project work is not permission.\n- Use unmodified upstream packages. Creating, patching, or introducing extensions, shims, wrappers, provider/workflow glue, or dependency overrides also requires explicit permission. Report upstream limitations and ask instead of adding workarounds.\n- Normal Pi preference/session writes and the approved nightly upstream update are expected runtime operations, not permission for agent-initiated infrastructure edits or changes to the approved package list.\n\n## Environment\n\n- macOS, Apple Silicon, zsh, Homebrew at /opt/homebrew.\n- Use Pi’s built-in `find`, `grep`, and `ls`; use system `fd`/`rg` through `bash` for advanced shell searches.\n- Keep shell commands non-interactive; never assume sudo.\n\n## Style\n\n- Be terse and code-first: diffs and commands first, prose only for what is non-obvious.\n- No preamble, no summaries of what was just done unless asked.\n- Ask instead of guessing when a consequential requirement is ambiguous.\n\n## Safety and Git\n\n- Never stage, commit, push, or amend unless explicitly asked in this session.\n- When asked to commit: inspect the diff first, stage only relevant files, match the repo's commit style.\n- In long sessions, fetch/pull before pushing; the remote may have moved under you.\n- Confirm before destructive operations such as recursive deletion, dropping data, or force-pushing.\n- Do not expose secrets in chat or copy them into agent sessions.\n\n## Engineering\n\n- Read relevant tests before changing behavior; treat them as contracts.\n- After code changes, detect and run the project's applicable tests, lint, and type checks (check package.json / Makefile / README). Report failures; don't claim completion with unresolved blockers.\n- If no check exists, say so in one line instead of inventing one.\n- At module boundaries, prefer explicit errors over silent coercion or fallback behavior.\n- Do not remove tests without explaining why in the diff. A regression test only counts if verified to fail with its guard reverted.\n- Mock-green suites hide real bugs; probe with stub-driven adversarial runs when it matters.\n- No code comments unless asked. Do not create README/docs/markdown files unless asked.\n\n## Herdr Desktop-only orchestration\n\n- Use `hc` (herdr-core) for Herdr agent/program orchestration and `herdr-app` for Desktop window control. Read `hc --skill` before orchestration and `herdr-app --skill` before window changes.\n- Herdr Desktop is the home for visible, persistent agents and programs. Route all authorized delegation through interactive `hc` terminals, using configured templates, authenticated Pi defaults, or the subagents skill's model routing rather than inventing model flags. Pi supplies coding tools and native status/title reporting, not internal subagent/workflow/side-agent orchestration.\n- The legacy `herdr` CLI, `herdr_*` tools, and native Herdr TUI inspector target a separate server, not Desktop. Do not use them for new work, including from cmux or an inherited TUI environment. Never substitute `hc` through `HERDR_BIN`; the command APIs differ.\n- Start interactive Pi inside Desktop for automatic OSC 7501 status reporting. Preserve existing TUI/cmux agents; do not migrate, reload, stop, or message unrelated sessions.\n- Only when the user types `/better`, stock pi-better may start its temporary prompt helper outside Herdr. The helper receives the saved chat and can run shell commands with your account's access. Its read-only instructions do not sandbox the shell. Leave the draft for the user to review and submit. Route all other delegated work through Herdr Desktop.\n- Keep long-lived programs in `hc` terminals. Preserve retained sessions and worktrees until cleanup is explicitly authorized.\n\n## Delegation\n\n- Delegate only when the current request or applicable instructions authorize it. Direct execution is the default; complexity and tool availability are not authorization.\n- Before authorized delegation, read [the subagents skill](skills/subagents/SKILL.md). It owns runner selection, model routing, lifecycle, recovery, and privacy rules.\n- Keep delegation bounded and non-recursive. Use one writer per working tree and isolated worktrees for concurrent writers.\n- Start independent reviewers with fresh context and a self-contained specification plus diff, not the implementation conversation. Fork only when inherited history is necessary and safe to share.\n- Require each child to report changes, validation results, and unresolved decisions; review delegated changes and validate them yourself before reporting completion.\n\n## Response style\n\nOnly respond to the user using ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English and the Bottom Line Up Front (BLUF) approach. Use SBAR for handovers and status updates. These do not apply to code or when the user asks otherwise\n\n</project_instructions>\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/AGENTS.md\">\n# Working on Tinkery\n\n**Read [docs/yohaku.md](docs/yohaku.md) first:** the constitution wins over guides,\ndesigns and briefs. Only the operator amends it; report discrepancies, never edit it.\n[README.md](README.md) describes shipped behavior.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Subtract first; say each thing once, plainly.\n- Fewer, deeper modules, smaller interfaces. Check data once where it comes in, keep\n  that guarantee in types, then just use it. Write one-off logic right where it's used\n  instead of splitting it into a function ahead of time. Skip try/catch wherever you can.\n- Check changeability: name the [architecture diagnostics](docs/design/architecture.md#design-score)\n  moved. Module and `pub` comments state promises and refusals, not implementation;\n  the build requires them.\n- Preserve operator originals verbatim; readings are guesses.\n  Keep the displayed, confirmed goal unchanged through saving and handoff.\n- Navigation and copying never launch agents. Goal confirmation is neither seed\n  confirmation nor implementation permission.\n- Work from an issue on a branch and open a PR. Merge it yourself once the issue is approved\n  (or the operator asked for the change), CI is green, there are no conflicts and a reviewer\n  who did not write the change has checked it against the issue. The one exception is the\n  [tink-sdlc bump](.github/workflows/tink-sdlc-bump.yml): it merges itself when CI passes and it\n  is exactly tink-sdlc's own upgrade.\n  If the operator asks for drafts only, create and merge nothing until they say so.\n- Model and provider calls require an explicit budget. The default is zero.\n  Eval commands are outside zero-call verification.\n- Agent operators (Pi via herdr) always launch Tinkery with `--operator agent`.\n- Never record real audio; voice tests use fake engines and recorders, never native addons.\n- Use temporary TEST roots. Never write to `~/.local/share/seed-me` or\n  `~/.local/share/tinkery`, or change operator sessions, installs, launcher, Pi,\n  Pi Voice or the operator's Seed Me checkout.\n- Fix a bug from its observed cause. After two failed fixes, add logging and collect\n  observations; after three, stop and hand over a fresh brief: problem, goal, failed attempts.\n- Read relevant tests and design before implementation; update changed decisions.\n  Add regressions, prove important protections fail when reverted, then restore them.\n  Tests assert what users observe through public interfaces; never add test-only access\n  to private state. Drive Think through its input, not `Conversation`'s capped test accessors.\n  Unowned ideas confer neither shipped behavior nor build permission.\n- For agent-facing docs, use `writing-for-agents`: one rule per authoritative home,\n  task detail behind pointers.\n  Preserve safeguards and runnable commands; brevity is not permission to weaken them.\n  `tests/test_documentation.py` checks links, headings, paths, options and reachability.\n- Create each task's worktree at `~/dev/worktrees/<repo>/<branch>`, never inside a checkout\n  or beside one.\n- At rest, keep a clean `main` matching `origin/main` after a fetch, with no stashes,\n  extra worktrees or leftover branches. Report open PRs instead of cleaning them up. Delete only\n  merged branches (`git branch -d`); never force-delete branches, drop stashes or\n  remove worktrees for cleanliness.\n\n## Response style\n\nEach reply spends the operator's attention, so it must earn its place ([Yohaku](docs/yohaku.md)).\nWrite in plain technical English, modelled on\n[simple-english](https://github.com/arekhalpern/simple-english) (ASD-STE100):\n\n- Lead with what the operator must decide or do, then the result. Ask at most one question.\n- Cut each line that does not change what the operator does next.\n- Name the state of each claim: proposed, attempted, observed, checked or accepted.\n  Say unknowns and failures plainly, with the next step.\n- Quote the operator's words exactly, and mark your readings as guesses.\n- Give one fact per sentence: at most 20 words for an instruction, 25 for a description.\n  Use active voice and simple tenses.\n- Write \"must\" for a requirement and \"can\" for a possibility, never \"should\", \"may\" or \"might\".\n  Put a condition before its instruction: \"If CI fails, read the log.\"\n- Keep one name for one thing. Write complete sentences with articles. No contractions,\n  semicolons, arrow chains or filler that states no fact.\n- Never change code, commands, identifiers or quoted errors.\n\n## Layout and design entry points\n\nRead only the relevant task contract from the\n[documentation index](docs/README.md). The [architecture map](docs/design/architecture.md#modules-today)\nshows module responsibilities.\n\n## Cold setup\n\nFresh Debian or Ubuntu clone with Bash and Git:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/cloud-setup.sh\n```\n\nThe script installs missing prerequisites, stable Rust with rustfmt and Clippy, and Node.\nIt prints the verification exports and can be rerun safely. It downloads only\nprerequisite packages, not providers, microphones, native voice engines or speech models.\nApt may need root or sudo.\n\nCI uses stable Rust: Ubuntu on pull requests, Ubuntu and macOS on `main`; use that updated stable for Clippy.\nNever alter operator toolchains without approval. On macOS, provision Rust, Python 3\nand Node separately (bootstrap refuses macOS). No external skill checkout or other\ndeveloper's files are needed.\n\n## Full zero-call verification\n\n[The Rust test wrapper](scripts/rust-tests.py) runs the workspace in a fresh TEST HOME\nand refuses default application storage.\n\nRun `python3 scripts/full-check.py` from the repository root after setup. Its shared\nlaunch guard gives the exact block below a temporary TEST HOME; do not run binary\nverification against the operator's HOME. Cold-clone CI runs the block when setup may change. [CI routing](scripts/ci-scope.py) and its\n[regressions](tests/test_ci_scope.py) define prose-only exceptions and fail-closed gates;\ncode changes require the full block. [The Python runner](scripts/python-tests.py) runs files\nin parallel; with `TINKERY_TEST_TIER=pr`, as on pull requests, it skips only the slow journeys it\nlists, which still run on every push to `main`.\n\nNode is only for `voice/*.test.mjs` and `pi/*.test.mjs`. Keep the invalid `TINKERY_NODE` for voice PTYs;\nPython journeys use the real binary, stub providers and temporary roots.\n\n<!-- full-check:start -->\n```bash\nexport PATH=\"${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}/bin:$PWD/.cloud-tools/node/bin:$PATH\"\nexport TINKERY_NODE=/tinkery-TEST-no-node\nrustc +stable --version\nnode --version\npython3 --version\ncargo +stable build --locked\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --home --snapshot\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --snapshot --no-color\npython3 scripts/rust-tests.py\nnode --test voice/*.test.mjs pi/*.test.mjs\npython3 scripts/python-tests.py\ncargo +stable fmt --all -- --check\ncargo +stable clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\n```\n<!-- full-check:end -->\n\n[Try it](README.md#try-it) without the installed launcher. `--snapshot` prints\n100×30 without terminal mode or provider calls; `NO_COLOR=1` or `--no-color` retain non-color\ncues. [Terminal setup](docs/terminal-setup.md#launch) has isolated real-drafting instructions.\n\n## Work intake\n\nThe board is [tinkery](https://github.com/users/jon-devlapaz/projects/2); its automations add\ntinkery issues and set Done on close. Cloud sessions can't reach it; use `gh` on the operator's Mac.\n\nFile an issue only for work that serves Tinkery, after searching for an open duplicate, using the\n[task template](.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/task.yml) with one `area:` and one kind label. Show the draft\nand publish only on the operator's word; publication is not approval. Parallel work needs approved,\nnon-blocking items with separate `area:` labels.\n\nUnapproved work stays `needs-approval`. `approved` records scoped operator permission,\nnever grants it; material scope changes return to `needs-approval`. Publication, design\nacceptance, implementation, data use and budget are separate decisions.\n\nUse one branch and PR per approved slice; link the issue, stay within its scope and fill in the\n[PR template](.github/pull_request_template.md). Close with\ncompletion evidence or an explicit reason. Report Rust, Node and Python counts, PTY\njourneys and sizes, failures and reversion probes separately from operator dogfood:\nstubs prove mechanics, not real-dump goal quality.\n\n\n<!-- AI-Native SDLC Router -->\n## SDLC Workspace\n- Read `_system/SDLC.md` for setup, evidence boundaries, and recovery.\n- Inspect `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status` before creating a run.\n- Read `stages/<stage-name>/CONTEXT.md` before processing a stage.\n- Keep factory references in `_shared/` unchanged during feature runs.\n- Use separate worktrees or clones for code-writing runs.\n- Stage skills: see the Skills section of the current stage's CONTEXT.md.\n- Need a specialised skill mid-task? `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \"<what you need>\"` prints it on stdout; exit 1 means none fits, so continue without one.\n<!-- End AI-Native SDLC Router -->\n\n## Response style\n\nOnly respond to the user using ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English and the Bottom Line Up Front (BLUF) approach. Use SBAR for handovers and status updates. These do not apply to code or when the user asks otherwise\n\n<!-- tink:rules begin skillset=build-skillset digest=1f3d3f07b904318d51e96afd8174591e82cabe8ec2da6668bf084aa4d683d4c1 -->\nDiscipline rules for this phase (compiled by tink; do not edit by hand):\n- principle-build-the-lever: Apply to any non-trivial work, not just bulk work: edits, migrations, analyses, checks. Build the tool that does it or proves it (codemod, script, generator, or a skill your subagents follow) instead of working by hand. The tool is the artifact a reviewer can rerun. (read: tink mount principle-build-the-lever --json --payload)\n- unslop: Cut AI tells from any writing. Must always apply. (read: tink mount unslop --json --payload)\n<!-- tink:rules end -->\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nResolve relative paths against the skill's directory (parent of SKILL.md). Use absolute paths in tool calls.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-for-agents</name>\n    <description>Writing documents for agents. Use when creating or editing skills, or modifying AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md.</description>\n    <location>/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/.agents/skills/writing-for-agents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>background-terminals</name>\n    <description>Manage persistent shell jobs with herdr-core. Use for dev servers, watchers, streaming builds, and commands that must keep running while work continues.</description>\n    <location>/Users/jondev/.pi/agent/skills/background-terminals/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>bro</name>\n    <description>Re-explain the previous assistant message, or any text typed after the command, in a much simpler way. For when a reply made you go &apos;bro what&apos;. Use /bro for a plain-language version of the last answer, or /bro &lt;text&gt; to simplify a pasted paragraph, error, email, or doc.</description>\n    <location>/Users/jondev/.pi/agent/skills/bro/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>subagents</name>\n    <description>Coordinate authorized visible, persistent agents in Herdr Desktop. Use when the user requests delegation, parallel agent work, or a persistent agent session.</description>\n    <location>/Users/jondev/.pi/agent/skills/subagents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>capture-idea</name>\n    <description>Save a hunch, idea, or discussion into the lightweight Obsidian Ideas layer when the user asks to capture it, save it for later, or add it to the idea vault. Preserve and link the original conversation.</description>\n    <location>/Users/jondev/.agents/skills/capture-idea/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>features-and-futures</name>\n    <description>Preserve changeability with Kent Beck&apos;s features-and-futures loop. Use alongside tink-sdlc / ai-native-sdlc when choosing a feature slice, pausing between verified slices, reassessing a specification after feedback, or facing AI-generated changes that cause cascading regressions. Decide whether to proceed, consolidate, or re-scope within the existing factory contracts. Not a replacement SDLC, generic style cleanup, or authority to run the factory.</description>\n    <location>/Users/jondev/.agents/skills/features-and-futures/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>lavish</name>\n    <description>Turn complex or visual agent responses into rich, reviewable HTML artifacts (HTML files) the user can annotate and send feedback on, using the lavish-axi CLI. Use when about to give a plan, comparison, diagram, table, code diff, report, or anything easier to grasp visually than as prose.</description>\n    <location>/Users/jondev/.agents/skills/lavish/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>merge-risk</name>\n    <description>Use when assessing the risk of merging a PR</description>\n    <location>/Users/jondev/.agents/skills/merge-risk/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought\n</cwd>","mcp_servers":"<mcp_servers>\nMCP servers whose tools are not declared to you. Call the tools of `codemode` servers from codemode scripts.\n- mcp__context7 (codemode)\n</mcp_servers>"},"timestamp":1794731473124,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.\n\nCodemode: `tools.read(args)` resolves to `string | { data: string; mimeType: string; note: string; type: \"image\"; }`.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.\n\nCodemode: `tools.bash(args)` resolves to `{ output, truncated, full_output_path?, exit_code, wall_time_seconds }`.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.\n\nCodemode: `tools.edit(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.\n\nCodemode: `tools.write(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"grep","description":"Search file contents for a pattern. Returns matching lines with file paths and line numbers. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 100 matches or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Long lines are truncated to 500 chars.\n\nCodemode: `tools.grep(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["pattern"],"properties":{"pattern":{"type":"string","description":"Search pattern (regex or literal string)"},"path":{"type":"string","description":"Directory or file to search (default: current directory)"},"glob":{"type":"string","description":"Filter files by glob pattern, relative to path, e.g. '*.ts', 'src/**/*.ts' or '**/*.spec.ts'. A pattern without '/' matches file names at any depth"},"ignoreCase":{"type":"boolean","description":"Case-insensitive search (default: false)"},"literal":{"type":"boolean","description":"Treat pattern as literal string instead of regex (default: false)"},"context":{"type":"number","description":"Number of lines to show before and after each match (default: 0)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of matches to return (default: 100)"}}}},{"name":"find","description":"Search for files by glob pattern. Returns matching file paths relative to the search directory. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 1000 results or 50KB (whichever is hit first).\n\nCodemode: `tools.find(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["pattern"],"properties":{"pattern":{"type":"string","description":"Glob pattern to match files, e.g. '*.ts', '**/*.json', or 'src/**/*.spec.ts'"},"path":{"type":"string","description":"Directory to search in (default: current directory)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of results (default: 1000)"}}}},{"name":"ls","description":"List directory contents. Returns entries sorted alphabetically, with '/' suffix for directories. Includes dotfiles. Output is truncated to 500 entries or 50KB (whichever is hit first).\n\nCodemode: `tools.ls(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Directory to list (default: current directory)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of entries to return (default: 500)"}}}},{"name":"typesafe_evaluate","description":"Evaluate supplied state with independent Choice, Score, and Noul questions in one TypeSafe request. Each question judges the whole state, so when several items are involved, put each item in a named state field (e.g. `reports.r1`) and ask one question per item per dimension (e.g. `r1_owner`, `r2_owner`), naming the field in the instructions; never aggregate several items into one question. Submitted state and questions will be sent to api.typesafe.ai and may incur charges. Do not include secrets. The extension does not collect files or conversation history. Results are model judgments, not proof or authorization. Requires operator opt-in via /typesafe enable or PI_TYPESAFE_ENABLED=1. Limit: 32 questions, 64 KiB JSON, 20 attempts per session; no retries.\n\nCodemode: `tools.typesafe_evaluate(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["state","questions"],"properties":{"state":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{}},{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}}}],"description":"What to judge: text, or an object whose fields the questions name. Not null."},"questions":{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","required":["type"],"properties":{"type":{"type":"string","const":"noul","description":"Yes or no: the probability the instructions hold."},"instructions":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{}},{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"One judgment about the whole state, phrased as a question or a statement."},"criteria":{"anyOf":[{"type":"null"},{"type":"object","properties":{"true":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{}},{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}}},{"type":"null"}]},"false":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{}},{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}}},{"type":"null"}]}},"additionalProperties":false}],"description":"Optional: what counts as yes and what counts as no, { true, false }."}},"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"object","required":["type","criteria"],"properties":{"type":{"type":"string","const":"choice","description":"Pick one criteria label."},"instructions":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{}},{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"One judgment about the whole state, phrased as a question or a statement."},"criteria":{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{}},{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}}},{"type":"null"}]}},"minProperties":1,"maxProperties":64,"description":"The options as a map from label to when it applies: { billing: \"Charges and payments\", other: null }. 1–64 entries."}},"additionalProperties":false},{"type":"object","required":["type","criteria"],"properties":{"type":{"type":"string","const":"score","description":"Rate against the ordered criteria levels."},"instructions":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{}},{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}}},{"type":"null"}],"description":"One judgment about the whole state, phrased as a question or a statement."},"criteria":{"type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"array","items":{}},{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}}},{"type":"null"}]},"minItems":2,"maxItems":32,"description":"Ordered rubric levels, lowest first: [\"neutral\", \"angry\"]. 2–32 levels."}},"additionalProperties":false}]}},"minProperties":1,"maxProperties":32,"description":"Questions keyed by a short id, as an object map, not an array: { \"urgent\": { type: \"noul\", instructions: ... } }."},"model":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":100,"description":"Jev model id, e.g. jev-latest. Omit for the default."}},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"transcribe_file","description":"Transcribe speech from a local audio or video file using Pi Voice's configured local model. Requires the ffmpeg executable on PATH (or PI_VOICE_FFMPEG_PATH). Output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50.0KB; a complete transcript is saved to a temporary file when needed.\n\nCodemode: `tools.transcribe_file(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Local media file path, absolute or relative to the current working directory"}}}},{"name":"todo","description":"Manage a task list for tracking multi-step progress. Actions: create (new task, or several independent tasks at once via tasks[]), update (change status/fields/dependencies), list (all tasks, optionally filtered by status), get (single task details), delete (tombstone), clear (reset all). Status: pending → in_progress → completed, plus deleted tombstone. Use this to plan and track multi-step work like research, design, and implementation.\n\nCodemode: `tools.todo(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["action"],"properties":{"action":{"type":"string","enum":["create","update","list","get","delete","clear"]},"subject":{"type":"string","description":"Task subject line (required for create unless tasks[] is set)"},"description":{"type":"string","description":"Long-form task description"},"activeForm":{"type":"string","description":"Present-continuous spinner label shown while status is in_progress (e.g. 'writing tests')"},"status":{"type":"string","enum":["pending","in_progress","completed","deleted"],"description":"Set this task's status (update): one of pending, in_progress, completed, deleted. When action is list, filters returned tasks by this status."},"blockedBy":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"number"},"description":"Initial blockedBy ids (create only)"},"addBlockedBy":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"number"},"description":"Task ids to add to blockedBy (update only, additive merge)"},"removeBlockedBy":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"number"},"description":"Task ids to remove from blockedBy (update only, additive merge)"},"owner":{"type":"string","description":"Agent/owner assigned to this task"},"metadata":{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}},"description":"Arbitrary metadata; pass null value for a key to delete that key on update"},"id":{"type":"number","description":"Task id (required for update, get, delete)"},"includeDeleted":{"type":"boolean","description":"If true, list action returns deleted (tombstoned) tasks as well. Default: false."},"tasks":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["subject"],"properties":{"subject":{"type":"string","description":"Task subject line"},"description":{"type":"string","description":"Long-form task description"},"activeForm":{"type":"string","description":"Present-continuous spinner label shown while status is in_progress (e.g. 'writing tests')"},"blockedBy":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"number"},"description":"Existing task ids this item waits on. Cannot name another item in this same batch; create the prerequisite first, then the dependent with blockedBy."},"owner":{"type":"string","description":"Agent/owner assigned to this task"},"metadata":{"type":"object","patternProperties":{"^.*$":{}},"description":"Arbitrary metadata"}}},"description":"Batch create: independent tasks in one call, one-of with subject. The whole list is validated then committed together. Items cannot blockedBy each other; create the prerequisite first, then the dependent with blockedBy."}}}},{"name":"web_search","description":"Search the web for sources or answers. Full results are stored for retrieval with get_search_content by responseId. Prefer 2-4 varied queries.\n\nCodemode: `tools.web_search(args)` resolves to `{ responseId, fetchId, queries }`.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"},"queries":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Run concurrently (up to three at a time); vary the angle"},"numResults":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":20,"description":"Default 5"},"includeContent":{"type":"boolean","description":"Also fetch full page content"},"recencyFilter":{"type":"string","enum":["day","week","month","year"]},"domainFilter":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Prefix - to exclude"},"category":{"type":"string","description":"Exa only, e.g. news"},"provider":{"type":"string","description":"auto, all, a name, or comma-separated names; omit for the configured default. Allowed: exa, duckduckgo."},"workflow":{"type":"string","enum":["none","summary-review","auto-summary"],"description":"Default none; summary-review opens the curator; auto-summary summarizes"},"proxy":{"type":"string","description":"Proxy URL; omit by default (env proxy vars ignored); \"\" forces direct"}}}},{"name":"source_check","description":"Gather cited web passages about a claim for manual review.\n\nCodemode: `tools.source_check(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["claim"],"properties":{"claim":{"type":"string","description":"Assertion to check"},"queries":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Default: the claim"},"numResults":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":20,"description":"Default 5"},"fetchContent":{"type":"boolean","description":"Fetch top 5 pages for exact passages"},"recencyFilter":{"type":"string","enum":["day","week","month","year"]},"domainFilter":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Prefix - to exclude"},"provider":{"type":"string","description":"auto, all, a name, or comma-separated names; omit for the configured default. Allowed: exa, duckduckgo."},"proxy":{"type":"string","description":"Proxy URL; omit by default; \"\" forces direct"}}}},{"name":"fetch_content","description":"Fetch web pages, PDFs, GitHub repos, images, YouTube and local video, as the mode allows. Full content is stored for retrieval with get_search_content.\n\nCodemode: `tools.fetch_content(args)` resolves to `{ responseId, urls }`.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"url":{"type":"string"},"urls":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"forceClone":{"type":"boolean","description":"Clone GitHub repos over the size limit"},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"Question for video analysis"},"mode":{"type":"string","enum":["readable","raw"],"description":"readable (default): extract readable content as markdown; raw: return the exact textual body using direct HTTP only."},"timestamp":{"type":"string","description":"Video frame time ('1:23:45') or range ('23:41-25:00'); needs ffmpeg"},"frames":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":12,"description":"Frames over a range, 5s apart from one time, or over the video"},"model":{"type":"string","description":"Gemini model for video analysis"},"auth":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"boolean"}],"description":"authFetch profile name, or true for the only one"},"proxy":{"type":"string","description":"Proxy URL; omit by default; \"\" forces direct"},"redirects":{"type":"string","enum":["same-origin"],"description":"Refuse cross-origin redirects"}}}},{"name":"get_search_content","description":"Page through or search stored content from web_search, source_check, or fetch_content by responseId.\n\nCodemode: `tools.get_search_content(args)` resolves to a string.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["responseId"],"properties":{"responseId":{"type":"string"},"query":{"type":"string"},"queryIndex":{"type":"integer","minimum":0},"url":{"type":"string"},"urlIndex":{"type":"integer","minimum":0},"offset":{"type":"integer","minimum":0},"limit":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":30000,"description":"Characters, default the maximum"},"findText":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":500},{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":500},"minItems":1,"maxItems":10}],"description":"Find passages instead of paging"},"findMode":{"type":"string","enum":["exact","case-insensitive","fuzzy"]}}}},{"name":"codemode","description":"Run JavaScript that calls other tools. The input is raw JavaScript (not JSON, no code fence), run as an async function body in a QuickJS sandbox: top-level `await` and `return` work. No Node, file system, network, or timers.\n- `await tools.<name>({ ...args })` resolves to a string, or an object if the tool's declaration says so, and rejects with an Error on failure. A tool whose declaration returns an object resolves on failure too when it returned that object; check its fields. Calls still running when the script ends are cancelled.\n- Optional first line: `// @options: {\"max_output_tokens\": 10000, \"timeout_ms\": 60000}`\n\nGlobals:\n- `text(value)`, `image(dataUrlOrImageBlock)`, `console.log(...)`, and top-level `return` add output; `exit()` ends the script. With several text items, each starts with a `==> text N/M <==` line, and `console` lines follow the other output in one `<console_output>` block. `image()` also saves the image to a temp file and the result names its path.\n- `store(key, value)` and `load(key)` keep JSON values across codemode calls.\n- `ALL_TOOLS`, `await searchTools(query, { limit?, namespace? })`, `await describeTool(name)`, `await describeNamespace(name)`: find unlisted tools, such as MCP tools.\n- `models`: classifiers and image generation. Read /Users/jondev/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.2/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs/codemode.md first.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["code"],"properties":{"code":{"type":"string","description":"Raw JavaScript source."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"grammar","variants":{"openai_lark":"\nstart: options_source | plain_source\noptions_source: OPTIONS_LINE NEWLINE SOURCE\nplain_source: SOURCE\n\nOPTIONS_LINE: /[ \\t]*\\/\\/ @options:[^\\r\\n]*/\nNEWLINE: /\\r?\\n/\nSOURCE: /[\\s\\S]+/\n"}}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are the build agent for this run. Implement the approved run work using /Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md; read those approved inputs, stage-3 CONTEXT.md at /Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md, the step contract at /Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json, and checkout instructions first. Write only in /Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought, plus your handoff report at exactly /Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/output/handoff.md (absolute path). Outside the checkout you may read, never change, what those approved inputs name; never read secrets or credentials. If a named fact cannot be read, say so in the report instead of building around it. Send no requests to production or live endpoints unless the approved inputs allow them; stop any local server or background process you start before ending your turn. Commit your work in /Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought before ending your turn, even when a check still fails, and name any failure in the report: verify runs only committed work. Never run sdlc.py step, stage or decide. Do not change the model or thinking level."}],"timestamp":1794731473123},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ea64dfb495d54540a1d4f341300bdad4|fc_0df587760909a640016af96dd4047887d187a54b8b26325fee","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"docs/yohaku.md","offset":null,"limit":null}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","usage":{"input":10224,"output":29,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":10253,"cost":{"input":0.020447999999999997,"output":0.00029,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.020737999999999996,"totalFromProvider":false}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794731473134,"responseId":"resp_0df587760909a640016af96dd263a887d1b1a217e449c71943","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":3221,"thinkingLevel":"medium"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ea64dfb495d54540a1d4f341300bdad4|fc_0df587760909a640016af96dd4047887d187a54b8b26325fee","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Yohaku: the constitution of Tinkery\n\nTinkery is the substrate for the operator’s agentic work: think, tend, shape, approve, watch, taste, measure.\nAgents do the repeatable work; the operator decides what runs and judges the result.\n\nThis constitution wins over guides, designs, briefs and instincts. Only the operator amends it.\n\n## Purpose\n\n**Useful, verified changes per operator hour.**\n\nGenerating code is cheap; the operator’s attention is not. Questions, explanations and reviews spend it.\nJudge changes by useful outcomes relative to that attention—not by activity, code volume or agent count.\nImprovement requires evidence; this purpose grants no permission to act.\n\n*Yohaku no bi* describes deliberately composed space; *ma* includes the intervals between things and actions.\nTheir value lies in the relationships they make possible, not in emptiness alone\n([Seattle Japanese Garden](https://www.seattlejapanesegarden.org/blog/2016/03/15/yohaku-no-bi-the-beauty-of-empty-space),\n[Deeper Japan](https://www.deeperjapan.com/deeper-views/ma-and-modern-minimalism)).\n\nFor Tinkery: **leave room for judgment, not gaps in evidence or consent.**\n\n## The articles\n\n### 1. Intent remains sovereign.\n\nBegin Think with the operator’s words, not suggestions. Keep what they type or say exactly.\nNever rewrite, negate or reinterpret their words as fact. Preserve the displayed, confirmed goal unchanged\nthrough saving and handoff.\n\nVisibly distinguish Tinkery’s interpretations as guesses and its proposals as provisional until explicitly\nconfirmed; distinguish both from operator words. Missing intent becomes a question, not an invention.\n\n**The test:** are originals intact, confirmed goals unchanged, and Tinkery-authored interpretations and\nproposals visibly distinguishable from operator words at a glance?\n\n### 2. Action stays within explicit authority.\n\nTinkery starts no agent, harness or command, and writes no durable record, without an explicit action in its\ninterface by the operator or an agent acting within the operator’s explicit authorization.\n\nCopying is not running. Readiness is not consent. Goal confirmation, seed confirmation and implementation\npermission are separate.\n\nAsk the operator only what only they can decide. Investigate discoverable facts within the work and budget\nthey authorized; ask before going beyond them.\n\nKeep work local and private by default. Voice stays on the operator’s machine. Nothing leaves without the\noperator knowing exactly what will leave and when.\n\n**The test:** could this action happen without the operator or an explicitly authorized agent pressing a key\nthat says what it does? Does the operator know exactly what leaves their machine, and when?\n\n### 3. Claims stop where evidence stops.\n\nDistinguish proposed, attempted, observed, checked and accepted. Interpretations are not observations.\nPresent evidence-backed status as status, not as a guess. Passing checks establishes neither operator\nacceptance nor improved outcomes.\n\nShow unknowns as unknown. Say failures plainly, with what to do next. Never invent information to make a\nresult look complete.\n\n**The test:** does each claim have matching evidence, with its limits and remaining unknowns visible?\n\n### 4. Make room for informed judgment.\n\nShow only what is needed now.\n\nFocus on one reading, at most one active question, and one clear next step. Keep the originals, alternatives\nand evidence needed to judge that step available.\n\nEvery screen says what comes next, in plain words, and how to leave. Preserve meaningful decision points\nwithout adding needless acknowledgments.\n\nIntent, authority and truthful evidence constrain simplification. Keep necessary consequences, uncertainty\nand exits legible, even when they require more space or attention.\n\nAmong choices that preserve these protections, favor the one that leaves more room for judgment, keeps\nthe operator more in control, and makes useful work easier to verify.\n\n**The test:** does the screen focus on one reading, ask at most one active question, provide the context\nneeded to judge the next step, and make that step and the way out clear without help?\n\n## For anyone changing Tinkery, human or agent\n\n- **Subtract first.** Before adding a field, key, check, structural mechanism or screen text, ask what can be\n  removed while preserving the protected behavior. Prefer the smallest coherent, reversible change;\n  compare added complexity with a simpler approach.\n- **Prove behaviour, not effort.** Test what the operator sees and does. Add a reversion probe for anything\n  protecting an article above. Preserve verification’s ability to reject faulty work; never weaken checks\n  to manufacture success.\n- **Move each correction left.** When the operator corrects an agent, make the mistake impossible in code,\n  or else a check that fails. Write a rule only when neither can hold it; review is the last resort.\n  Preserve the correction’s underlying protection without turning a contextual preference into a universal\n  prohibition.\n- **Keep the next change clear.** Give responsibilities clear boundaries and rules one authoritative home.\n  Hide implementation decisions behind interfaces so changing them need not spread through callers.\n- **Keep the design true.** For design updates, verification and approved-work issue, branch and PR rules,\n  follow the [working guide](../AGENTS.md#guardrails).\n\n## Colour has one job each\n\n| Colour | Job |\n|---|---|\n| Ink | the operator’s words, and the goal as it stands |\n| Muted grey | hints, the key bar, and lines that didn’t change |\n| Italic | Tinkery’s interpretation of intent—a guess |\n| Jade | where you are: the question, the current place |\n| Rust | consequence only, always with `!` and words saying what is lost |\n\nColour is never the only signal. With `NO_COLOR`, the same meanings carry through bold, dim and underline.\n\n## Design contracts\n\n- **Think interactions:** readings, questions, review and stopping → [Think](design/think.md).\n- **Goal structure:** composition and rendering → [goal block](design/goal-block.md).\n- **Persistence and handoff:** saving, receipts, Shape and continuation → [Tend and handoff](design/shape-and-handoff.md).\n- **Presentation:** visual conventions and controls → [principles](design/principles.md).\n\n*Amended by the operator on 2026-10-15: Tinkery is the substrate where agentic work happens, not only its front door.*\n\n*Amended by the operator on 2026-10-16: agents may merge approved work once its checks pass.*\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":3,"timestamp":1794731476363},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367","name":"codemode","arguments":{"code":"const paths=[\"AGENTS.md\",\"_system/SDLC.md\",\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\",\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\",\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\"];\nfor(const p of paths) text({path:p,content:await tools.read({path:p})});\ntext(await tools.bash({command:\"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status\",timeout:30}));\ntext(await tools.todo({action:\"create\",tasks:[{subject:\"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation\"},{subject:\"Implement the approved slice and regression checks\"},{subject:\"Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes\"}]}));\n"}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","usage":{"input":1511,"output":174,"cacheRead":10112,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":11797,"cost":{"input":0.003022,"output":0.0017400000000000002,"cacheRead":0.0010112,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.005773200000000001,"totalFromProvider":false}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794731476366,"responseId":"resp_0df587760909a640016af96dd4a04487d189a0a064f968f90e","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":6217,"thinkingLevel":"medium"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367","toolName":"codemode","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 12027)\nTotal output lines: 16\n\n==> text 1/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\",\"content\":\"# Working on Tinkery\\n\\n**Read [docs/yohaku.md](docs/yohaku.md) first:** the constitution wins over guides,\\ndesigns and briefs. Only the operator amends it; report discrepancies, never edit it.\\n[README.md](README.md) describes shipped behavior.\\n\\n## Guardrails\\n\\n- Subtract first; say each thing once, plainly.\\n- Fewer, deeper modules, smaller interfaces. Check data once where it comes in, keep\\n  that guarantee in types, then just use it. Write one-off logic right where it's used\\n  instead of splitting it into a function ahead of time. Skip try/catch wherever you can.\\n- Check changeability: name the [architecture diagnostics](docs/design/architecture.md#design-score)\\n  moved. Module and `pub` comments state promises and refusals, not implementation;\\n  the build requires them.\\n- Preserve operator originals verbatim; readings are guesses.\\n  Keep the displayed, confirmed goal unchanged through saving and handoff.\\n- Navigation and copying never launch agents. Goal confirmation is neither seed\\n  confirmation nor implementation permission.\\n- Work from an issue on a branch and open a PR. Merge it yourself once the issue is approved\\n  (or the operator asked for the change), CI is green, there are no conflicts and a reviewer\\n  who did not write the change has checked it against the issue. The one exception is the\\n  [tink-sdlc bump](.github/workflows/tink-sdlc-bump.yml): it merges itself when CI passes and it\\n  is exactly tink-sdlc's own upgrade.\\n  If the operator asks for drafts only, create and merge nothing until they say so.\\n- Model and provider calls require an explicit budget. The default is zero.\\n  Eval commands are outside zero-call verification.\\n- Agent operators (Pi via herdr) always launch Tinkery with `--operator agent`.\\n- Never record real audio; voice tests use fake engines and recorders, never native addons.\\n- Use temporary TEST roots. Never write to `~/.local/share/seed-me` or\\n  `~/.local/share/tinkery`, or change operator sessions, installs, launcher, Pi,\\n  Pi Voice or the operator's Seed Me checkout.\\n- Fix a bug from its observed cause. After two failed fixes, add logging and collect\\n  observations; after three, stop and hand over a fresh brief: problem, goal, failed attempts.\\n- Read relevant tests and design before implementation; update changed decisions.\\n  Add regressions, prove important protections fail when reverted, then restore them.\\n  Tests assert what users observe through public interfaces; never add test-only access\\n  to private state. Drive Think through its input, not `Conversation`'s capped test accessors.\\n  Unowned ideas confer neither shipped behavior nor build permission.\\n- For agent-facing docs, use `writing-for-agents`: one rule per authoritative home,\\n  task detail behind pointers.\\n  Preserve safeguards and runnable commands; brevity is not permission to weaken them.\\n  `tests/test_documentation.py` checks links, headings, paths, options and reachability.\\n- Create each task's worktree at `~/dev/worktrees/<repo>/<branch>`, never inside a checkout\\n  or beside one.\\n- At rest, keep a clean `main` matching `origin/main` after a fetch, with no stashes,\\n  extra worktrees or leftover branches. Report open PRs instead of cleaning them up. Delete only\\n  merged branches (`git branch -d`); never force-delete branches, drop stashes or\\n  remove worktrees for cleanliness.\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nEach reply spends the operator's attention, so it must earn its place ([Yohaku](docs/yohaku.md)).\\nWrite in plain technical English, modelled on\\n[simple-english](https://github.com/arekhalpern/simple-english) (ASD-STE100):\\n\\n- Lead with what the operator must decide or do, then the result. Ask at most one question.\\n- Cut each line that does not change what the operator does next.\\n- Name the state of each claim: proposed, attempted, observed, checked or accepted.\\n  Say unknowns and failures plainly, with the next step.\\n- Quote the operator's words exactly, and mark your readings as guesses.\\n- Give one fact per sentence: at most 20 words for an instruction, 25 for a description.\\n  Use active voice and simple tenses.\\n- Write \\\"must\\\" for a requirement and \\\"can\\\" for a possibility, never \\\"should\\\", \\\"may\\\" or \\\"might\\\".\\n  Put a condition before its instruction: \\\"If CI fails, read the log.\\\"\\n- Keep one name for one thing. Write complete sentences with articles. No contractions,\\n  semicolons, arrow chains or filler that states no fact.\\n- Never change code, commands, identifiers or quoted errors.\\n\\n## Layout and design entry points\\n\\nRead only the relevant task contract from the\\n[documentation index](docs/README.md). The [architecture map](docs/design/architecture.md#modules-today)\\nshows module responsibilities.\\n\\n## Cold setup\\n\\nFresh Debian or Ubuntu clone with Bash and Git:\\n\\n```bash\\n./scripts/cloud-setup.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe script installs missing prerequisites, stable Rust with rustfmt and Clippy, and Node.\\nIt prints the verification exports and can be rerun safely. It downloads only\\nprerequisite packages, not providers, microphones, native voice engines or speech models.\\nApt may need root or sudo.\\n\\nCI uses stable Rust: Ubuntu on pull requests, Ubuntu and macOS on `main`; use that updated stable for Clippy.\\nNever alter operator toolchains without approval. On macOS, provision Rust, Python 3\\nand Node separately (bootstrap refuses macOS). No external skill checkout or other\\ndeveloper's files are needed.\\n\\n## Full zero-call verification\\n\\n[The Rust test wrapper](scripts/rust-tests.py) runs the workspace in a fresh TEST HOME\\nand refuses default application storage.\\n\\nRun `python3 scripts/full-check.py` from the repository root after setup. Its shared\\nlaunch guard gives the exact block below a temporary TEST HOME; do not run binary\\nverification against the operator's HOME. Cold-clone CI runs the block when setup may change. [CI routing](scripts/ci-scope.py) and its\\n[regressions](tests/test_ci_scope.py) define prose-only exceptions and fail-closed gates;\\ncode changes require the full block. [The Python runner](scripts/python-tests.py) runs files\\nin parallel; with `TINKERY_TEST_TIER=pr`, as on pull requests, it skips only the slow journeys it\\nlists, which still run on every push to `main`.\\n\\nNode is only for `voice/*.test.mjs` and `pi/*.test.mjs`. Keep the invalid `TINKERY_NODE` for voice PTYs;\\nPython journeys use the real binary, stub providers and temporary roots.\\n\\n<!-- full-check:start -->\\n```bash\\nexport PATH=\\\"${CARGO_HOME:-$HOME/.cargo}/bin:$PWD/.cloud-tools/node/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nexport TINKERY_NODE=/tinkery-TEST-no-node\\nrustc +stable --version\\nnode --version\\npython3 --version\\ncargo +stable build --locked\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --home --snapshot\\ncargo +stable run --locked -- --operator agent --snapshot --no-color\\npython3 scripts/rust-tests.py\\nnode --test voice/*.test.mjs pi/*.test.mjs\\npython3 scripts/python-tests.py\\ncargo +stable fmt --all -- --check\\ncargo +stable clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings\\n```\\n<!-- full-check:end -->\\n\\n[Try it](README.md#try-it) without the installed launcher. `--snapshot` prints\\n100×30 without terminal mode or provider calls; `NO_COLOR=1` or `--no-color` retain non-color\\ncues. [Terminal setup](docs/terminal-setup.md#launch) has isolated real-drafting instructions.\\n\\n## Work intake\\n\\nThe board is [tinkery](https://github.com/users/jon-devlapaz/projects/2); its automations add\\ntinkery issues and set Done on close. Cloud sessions can't reach it; use `gh` on the operator's Mac.\\n\\nFile an issue only for work that serves Tinkery, after searching for an open duplicate, using the\\n[task template](.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/task.yml) with one `area:` and one kind label. Show the draft\\nand publish only on the operator's word; publication is not approval. Parallel work needs approved,\\nnon-blocking items with separate `area:` labels.\\n\\nUnapproved work stays `needs-approval`. `approved` records scoped operator permission,\\nnever grants it; material scope changes return to `needs-approval`. Publication, design\\nacceptance, implementation, data use and budget are separate decisions.\\n\\nUse one branch and PR per approved slice; link the issue, stay within its scope and fill in the\\n[PR template](.github/pull_request_template.md). Close with\\ncompletion evidence or an explicit reason. Report Rust, Node and Python counts, PTY\\njourneys and sizes, failures and reversion probes separately from operator dogfood:\\nstubs prove mechanics, not real-dump goal quality.\\n\\n\\n<!-- AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n## SDLC Workspace\\n- Read `_system/SDLC.md` for setup, evidence boundaries, and recovery.\\n- Inspect `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status` before creating a run.\\n- Read `stages/<stage-name>/CONTEXT.md` before processing a stage.\\n- Keep factory references in `_shared/` unchanged during feature runs.\\n- Use separate worktrees or clones for code-writing runs.\\n- Stage skills: see the Skills section of the current stage's CONTEXT.md.\\n- Need a specialised skill mid-task? `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"` prints it on stdout; exit 1 means none fits, so continue without one.\\n<!-- End AI-Native SDLC Router -->\\n\\n## Response style\\n\\nOnly respond to the user using ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English and the Bottom Line Up Front (BLUF) approach. Use SBAR for handovers and status updates. These do not apply to code or when the user asks otherwise\\n\\n<!-- tink:rules begin skillset=build-skillset digest=1f3d3f07b904318d51e96afd8174591e82cabe8ec2da6668bf084aa4d683d4c1 -->\\nDiscipline rules for this phase (compiled by tink; do not edit by hand):\\n- principle-build-the-lever: Apply to any non-trivial work, not just bulk work: edits, migrations, analyses, checks. Build the tool that does it or proves it (codemod, script, generator, or a skill your subagents follow) instead of working by hand. The tool is the artifact a reviewer can rerun. (read: tink mount principle-build-the-lever --json --payload)\\n- unslop: Cut AI tells from any writing. Must always apply. (read: tink mount unslop --json --payload)\\n<!-- tink:rules end -->\\n\"}\n==> text 2/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\",\"content\":\"# SDLC workspace\\n\\nA small filesystem workflow: define a change, implement and verify it in an isolated checkout, then obtain independent release review. Requires Python 3.9+, Bash, and Git on a POSIX system. Native Windows is not supported. Set `require_tink: true` when project policy requires Tink integrity checks.\\n\\n**Boundaries, said once.** Receipts, digests, locks and reviewer names are local workflow records: they detect mistakes and changes, not tampering, and authenticate no one. Checks run real commands, never in a sandbox; review them before approval. Nothing here authorizes a release: trusted CI, protected branches and independent forge review do. Never invent a reviewer or approval, and never hand-edit generated records.\\n\\n## Start a run\\n\\nRun scripts from any working directory; paths resolve from the script location.\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --kind bug\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status fix-example\\n```\\n\\nThe default `light` profile creates `brief.md` (problem, acceptance criteria, approach, risks, verification) and `checklist.json`. A human picks `--profile full` for consequential architecture or policy changes; it creates intent, spec and plan. Only runs created by `new` are supported, and text approval tags are not evidence.\\n\\nTo build an approved GitHub issue, let the issue be the definition:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py new fix-example --issue https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/issues/NUMBER\\n```\\n\\nIt reads the issue once with `gh` and calls no model. `brief.md` holds the issue's title, address, body hash and body unchanged; `checklist.json` holds one attested item for each line under the issue's `Done when` heading, unchanged. The `Done when` section must hold only top-level `-` or `*` lines, one line each, so no approved word is cut or dropped. It refuses a closed issue, an issue without the `approved` label, a Done-when section with no lines or any other content, an unreadable issue and `--profile full`, and then creates no run. `run.json` keeps the issue's address and body hash as a record. It records no decision: stage 3 still needs the human decision.\\n\\nWithout a confirmed seed, review the checklist commands and offer a dry run before definition approval (seed-bound runs use the [seed definition comparison](#seed-definition-comparison)):\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py dry-run fix-example\\n```\\n\\nIt runs every checklist `check` in the repository root with its timeout, replacing `runs/<slug>/checklist-dry-run.log` with launch errors, timeouts and nonzero exits per item. No check process survives its check: each starts in a new session, and on normal exit or timeout the rest of its process group is killed and reaped. Tell an expected reproduction failure from a broken command or environment (such as an unsuitable `TMPDIR`), fix the latter and rerun. The dry run records no decision, mark or receipt, does not enforce a new approval prerequisite, and does not replace `verify`, which still requires current approval and passing checks.\\n\\nAfter the human accepts the brief, record the actual review reference:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py decide fix-example 3 approved \\\\\\n  --reviewer 'Reviewer name' --source 'Actual review URL or decision reference' \\\\\\n  --reason 'Accepted scope, approach, and acceptance criteria'\\n```\\n\\nLight runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3: `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. Full runs record stages 1, 2 and 3 in order. `changes-requested` records rejection with the same command. Decisions bind their artifacts and contract inputs; edits stale them.\\n\\n`decide` and seed-judge `mark` are human-only: they refuse a reviewer starting with `Agent`, containing `not human`, or an agent actor (`--actor`, or run metadata). Older agent records stay readable, labelled `agent-recorded`, and never count as human acceptance.\\n\\n## Checklist\\n\\n`runs/<slug>/checklist.json` holds item definitions only: `{\\\"schema\\\":1,\\\"items\\\":[{\\\"id\\\",\\\"description\\\",\\\"verify\\\",\\\"check\\\"?}]}`. They are approved scope: stage 3 cannot be approved with zero or invalid items, and changing them stales that approval. Never add status fields. If the file is deleted, status reads MISSING and verification refuses until it is restored from Git.\\n\\n- **Checked items** carry `check`: `{\\\"argv\\\":[...],\\\"timeout_seconds\\\":N}`. `verify` runs them in the repository root after the configured checks, logging to `test-log.md`; a failure fails verification (`Checklist check failed: <id>`). A pass proves the item, so `mark` is refused for it.\\n- **Attested items** have no `check`: `sdlc.py mark <run> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>` appends a receipt under `marks/` (needs current stage 3 approval; latest wins; `verify` requires `passed`). Verification binds each latest mark, so mark first, then verify. Attestations are self-reported claims for review to challenge. Status flags a mark made on an older candidate; re-check it if the change affects it.\\n\\nWhen every command passes but attestations are missing, `verify` exits nonzero with `Checklist incomplete`, the mark commands to run, and `result: awaiting-attestation` with `pending_attestations`. That is machine proof only, not a pass. It stays current while its proof inputs and log match (evidence-only commits keep it); a mark or changed input retires it. An item a person marked `failed` makes the result `failed`. Record the actual observations, then rerun `verify`.\\n\\n### Locked seed acceptance checks\\n\\nAt stage-1 intake, use the explicitly supplied session and a trusted Tinkery executable:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 1 \\\\\\n  --seed-contract <session>/seed-contract.md \\\\\\n  --tinkery <path>/tinkery\\n```\\n\\nTinkery is the single seed verifier. Intake runs exactly `PATH seed status --json DIR` and `PATH seed checks DIR` (DIR is the seed's session directory) and reads status again to detect changes. Tinkery verifies human v2 and agent v3 confirmations against the seed; intake accepts v3 only with `operator: agent` and an agent `actor`, which records agent confirmation, never human confirmation. Missing executables, failed commands, malformed output, simulated sessions, missing confirmation and changed bindings refuse before any write. Supply trusted toolchain code, never a seed-provided script. Verification proves content binding, not operator identity or implementation approval.\\n\\nIntake copies the confirmed seed and its records into locked `runs/<slug>/seed-checks.json` items with reserved `seed-N` IDs; run metadata keeps the confirmation receipt and the original definitions' digest. Seed Markdown is never read as checks. Imported items are **LOCKED, seed-confirmed** definitions, distinct from the worker's `checklist.json`. Never silently weaken or remove acceptance: such edits are departures. Deleting the file or changing the run-local seed refuses verification. Later edits to the original session do not reach the run-local snapshot; to rebind, run verified intake again; rebinding replaces activations, and prior evidence stays in Git.\\n\\n#### Seed definition comparison\\n\\nStage 3 compares effective checks with the intake baseline. With no added, removed or changed checks, and no activation other than the confirmed proposal, it passes without another human question and records `matches confirmed seed (sha256: …)` as evidence, not a human decision. This is structural, not a claim about the brief or permission to implement. An explicit human `changes-requested` stays blocking until a later human decision supersedes it.\\n\\nAny departure blocks automatic passage. Status lists each changed field, removed or added check, added worker item and non-proposal activation, and asks only `Accept these departures from the confirmed seed?`. `decide … 3` records the human answer bound to those exact inputs. Full-profile stages 1 and 2 keep their own reviews. Legacy bindings without a stored baseline compare against the intake digest; if their definitions changed, rebind rather than invent a baseline.\\n\\nSeed record kinds:\\n\\n- **`cmd`** runs verbatim through `bash -c` with a 120-second timeout, or `seed_timeout_seconds` from `_system/verification.json`; `cwd` is relative to the checkout (absolute paths, traversal, backslashes, drive letters, NUL and CR/LF refused). Failures and timeouts fail `verify` even when all worker checks pass. **Passing output proves a `cmd` or activated proposal entirely; no `seed-N-expect` mark is owed.** `expect` is descriptive context, not a matcher.\\n- **`judge`** (prose or taste) needs a manual human attestation, judged by `how_to_judge` or else the statement, never an automated pass. Record only the actual operator's observation:\\n\\n  ```sh\\n  python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py mark <slug> seed-1 passed \\\\\\n    --reviewer 'Actual operator' --source 'Actual observation reference' \\\\\\n    --evidence 'Observed output and why it satisfies the seed expectation'\\n  ```\\n\\n  Evidence already used for another item is refused unless `--covers-all` says the operator meant it for each. Mark `unknown` when the observation does not address the expectation; `unknown` blocks verification. Marks need current definition approval and bind the exact item.\\n- **`provisional`** names its prerequisite in `missing`; `cmd` is an optional proposal. It stays pending and nonblocking, never proven, while the prerequisite is absent. At implementation time, inspect the prerequisite. **Once it exists**, add only an `activation` object to that item in `seed-checks.json`, keeping the proposal intact:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\\"activation\\\": {\\\"cmd\\\":…2027 tokens truncated…ython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py verify`.\\n- The reviewer works in a separate checkout, writes `runs/<slug>/05-deploy/output/REVIEW-findings.md`,\\n  and changes no code or evidence. The candidate's owner brings the findings to the PR branch.\\n\\n## Pinned build steps\\n\\n`stage` prepares a checkout and prints a prompt; it never starts an agent. For an explicitly\\nauthorized build agent, open stage 3, then, from that checkout, take these deliberate steps one at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build prepare \\\\\\n  --report /absolute/run-notes/build-report.md \\\\\\n  --model provider/exact-model-id --launcher /absolute/trusted-adapter [--max-responses N]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build show\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build launch\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build resume\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py step <run> build complete --stopped-by operator  # or cap\\n```\\n\\n- `prepare` writes an immutable `runs/<run>/steps/<name>.json` binding the checkout, the exact model,\\n  the launcher's path and SHA-256, and a fresh absolute report path whose parent exists. It starts nothing.\\n  It refuses existing names or reports, relative paths, model aliases, and a run branch checked out\\n  elsewhere. Contracts are not portable: prepare a new step in a new checkout.\\n- `--max-responses N` caps responses; an adapter that cannot enforce it refuses, so a cap is never ignored.\\n- `launch` and `resume` recheck every definition gate and resolve the exact model offline first. The\\n  prompt names the approved inputs, `stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md` and the report path; limits writes to\\n  the checkout and report; tells the worker to commit before ending each turn; and forbids `sdlc.py step`, `stage` and `decide`. Inside the session\\n  `TINK_SDLC_STEP_SESSION=1` makes `step` refuse everything but `show`. Resume keeps the same session.\\n- `complete` needs a nonempty report at the exact path and the adapter's offline confirmation that the\\n  agent's latest turn ended on the pin. A paused, failed or running agent leaves the step `active`.\\n  `--stopped-by operator|cap` ends the step's process group and records it `stopped`, never as an ended turn.\\n- `show` adds `observed_turn` (`ended`, `not ended` or `unknown`) from an offline probe.\\n- A crash or failed admission leaves the step `uncertain`, never retried. Inspect the real session and\\n  reconcile it before preparing another step; never hand-edit the contract.\\n\\nA report is not proof of passing checks, authorship or approval; `verify` and forge gates stay independent.\\n\\nBundled adapters (their module comments state what each promises and refuses):\\n\\n| Adapter | Pin | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `_system/scripts/pi-launcher` | `provider/model-id:thinking`, e.g. `openai-codex/gpt-5.6-luna:low` | The model must be in Pi's local catalog (run `pi update --models` yourself) with a stored credential. Enforces `--max-responses`. |\\n\\nResume pinned steps only through `step … resume`: pi-herdr's resume drops the pin. Another launcher\\nmust implement the [launcher protocol](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/launcher-protocol.md)\\nand pass offline-resolution tests before it gets a live-call budget.\\n\\n## Stage skills\\n\\nEach stage has a skillset pin committed at `.tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json`: 01-plan `planning-skillset`, 02-design `design-skillset`, 03-build `build-skillset`, 04-test `testing-skillset`, 05-deploy `deployment-skillset`, 06-maintain `maintenance-skillset`. A pin selects exact upstream code, so review a pin change like a lockfile change.\\n\\n```sh\\ntink library fetch .tink/skillsets/<name>-skillset.json    # once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin\\ntink use <name>-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/<stage-dir>  # at stage open, then start a NEW session\\ntink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`required` lists the disciplines `tink use` compiles into `AGENTS.md` at stage open. Capability skills come only through `tink-route` for genuine gaps. It searches the whole library (`--anywhere`, the default; `--skillset NAME` restricts it, and exit 1 may then add `Hint: <skill> fits but is on another shelf`). Exit 1 or 2 means continue without a skill. Keep its receipt with the run and read the delivered skill before relying on it. `tink-route` mounts into the git-ignored `.tink/.active` and changes no project skills.\\n\\nCommit `.tink/skills.toml` and `.tink/skills.lock` when Tink creates them through an authorized operation; restore them into each worktree through Tink, never by symlink. Run `tink` mutations through the wrapper, which serializes them on this host and rejects symlinked skill state:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py skills tink -- skill check\\n```\\n\\nTinkery's verified confirmed seed contract is the input to 01-plan (bind it as in [Locked seed acceptance checks](#locked-seed-acceptance-checks)). `tink use` and `tink-route` are optional; runs work without them.\\n\\n## Recovery and release\\n\\nRuns have one writer at a time; a busy or interrupted operation fails with its lock path. After a crash, confirm no process owns the operation, remove the lock directory and rerun.\\n\\nOpen the PR once the candidate is committed and verification is current (end of stage 4; `stage <slug> 5` enforces it). Delivery and merge authority belong to `stages/05-deploy/CONTEXT.md`. Keep deployed revision, result and rollback references in the deployment system.\\n\\nRecord the run's ending:\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py pr <run> <PR URL>   # needs current verification; one PR per run\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py label <run> --kind correction|none|accepted|rejected --words TEXT \\\\\\n  --reviewer NAME --source REF [--about FILE|outside] [--consent local|shareable]\\npython3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py close <run> --outcome merged --merge <full commit>   # or --outcome abandoned\\n```\\n\\nPut `Tink-Run: <run_id>` in the PR description. Labels are human-only (refused inside a build step session), keep the words verbatim, and chain to each other by digest; `none` records no corrections and `outside` a correction outside the run's records. `close` needs a label (and, when merged, the recorded PR); a run closes once. While the run builds in another checkout, these refuse and name it. Closing in a linked worktree copies the run's records into the main checkout's `runs/<run>/` (refusing if that folder has uncommitted changes or another run); commit them there.\\n\\nMaintenance is optional intake. Enable it only with metric thresholds, deduplication, cooldowns and run limits; alerts create drafts for triage, never approvals.\\n\\n## Machine interface (API 1)\\n\\nClients read `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py capabilities --json` and `status [<run>] --json` from the selected checkout's runtime, check `protocol`, `api_version` and capabilities first, and fail closed on unknown states, never falling back to the human CLI output. The field contract is in [references/api-1.md](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tink-sdlc/blob/main/references/api-1.md).\\n\\n## Maintaining the scaffold\\n\\n`python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py walk [--json]` is a read-only structural check that a memoryless agent can orient from the files: router size, resolving pointers, stage contracts naming Inputs, Output and a human Gate, per-stage context size, skillset pins, `status` for every run, and current `tink:rules` blocks. A real walk by a fresh agent stays the test.\\n\\nPreview, then apply an upgrade (`_system/scaffold.json` is the trust baseline):\\n\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade --check\\n    python3 <package>/scripts/init.py <target> --upgrade\\n\\nUnmodified managed files are updated, restored or removed; project-owned files (`_system/verification.json`, `.tink/skillsets/*.json`) are never overwritten. A customized managed file blocks the upgrade and is listed with a `diff`: merge by hand, or commit and rerun with `--overwrite-customized`. Only the `AGENTS.md` router block is replaced; `runs/` is never modified, but changed stage 1-3 contracts stale existing approvals. Downgrades are refused, and an interrupted upgrade is safe to rerun. A killed install can leave a partial one: inspect it and its lock, confirm no writer remains, and recover through a reviewed migration, never a forced overwrite. Never update factory files during an active feature run. Afterward run `walk`.\\n\\nTemplates live in `_shared/` (`brief-template.md` is the light-profile edit surface); run outputs go under `runs/`. Read the active contract and its immediate inputs, not the whole factory.\\n\"}\n==> text 3/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\",\"content\":\"# Stage 03: Implement\\n\\nInputs: current approved spec for full runs, or combined brief for light runs. A seed-bound run's\\nconfirmed `seed-contract.md` and locked checks define it; its `brief.md` may stay the template.\\nOutput: `runs/<slug>/03-build/output/plan.md` for full runs, the approved checklist definitions\\nin `runs/<slug>/checklist.json` for both profiles, and code in an isolated worktree/clone.\\nItems should carry a `check` whenever an automated proof exists; `verify` runs it and no mark is needed.\\nWhen a pending provisional seed check's prerequisite exists, follow the guide's [provisional activation step](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison) and report the activation; a command other than the confirmed proposal needs operator stage-3 approval.\\n\\nGate: inspect code and follow the stage 3 gate before implementation; for seed-bound\\nruns use the guide's [seed definition comparison](../../_system/SDLC.md#seed-definition-comparison).\\nFor full runs, write the plan first. Light runs have ONE definition gate, recorded as stage 3 (`sdlc.py decide <run> 3 ...`): the approved `brief.md` + `checklist.json` are the intent, design and plan. An explicit user instruction to execute a reviewed proposal is\\nimplementation authority; never fabricate separate role approvals.\\nEach concurrent writer needs its own checkout. Use the serialized skill wrapper\\nin `_system/SDLC.md`; no shared writable skill symlinks or automatic lockfile rewrites.\\n\\nFor bug fixes, reproduce the expected failure first, obtain independent acceptance,\\nand record protected test inputs with `sdlc.py lock-tests`. Implement and verify in\\na loop with stage 04. Update the plan when scope changes and renew stale decisions.\\nMark items only with `sdlc.py mark <slug> <item-id> passed|failed --evidence <text>`; never hand-edit receipts.\\nFor launcher-managed build agents, use the guide's [pinned build steps](../../_system/SDLC.md#pinned-build-steps).\\nRead the supplied step contract, write the handoff at its exact absolute report path,\\nand retain that contract and model pin on resume. A missing report refuses step completion.\\n\\n## Skills\\n\\nSkillset: `build-skillset` (pin: `.tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`).\\n- Once per machine/library, after reviewing the pin (it selects exact upstream code):\\n  `tink library fetch .tink/skillsets/build-skillset.json`\\n- At stage open, compile the required disciplines, then start a NEW session so\\n  `AGENTS.md` is re-read: `tink use build-skillset --snapshot runs/<slug>/03-build`\\n  The launcher does this for you: `python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py stage <slug> 3`.\\n- For a capability gap: `tink-route --receipt runs/<slug>/skills.jsonl \\\"<what you need>\\\"`\\n  (searches the whole library; prints the skill on stdout. Exit 1 means nothing fits; exit 1 or 2 means\\n  continue without a skill).\\n\\nStage skills (always for this stage; `tink use` compiles the same set):\\n- `principle-build-the-lever`: Non-trivial work: build the script or tool that does or proves it, so a reviewer can rerun it, instead of doing it by hand.\\n- `unslop`: Any prose you write (docs, commit messages, replies): cut AI tells and filler before you send it.\\nWhen one of these applies, read that skill in full before acting on it. In the handoff note, name each skill that changed a decision and the decision it changed.\\n\"}\n==> text 4/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\",\"content\":\"# Change brief\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/864\\nIssue body sha256: 29f447833355967617805c1fc76789f53c35598279aa8b5a4f7ba921cf6aa3b1\\nTitle: Tend: Keep a raw project thought before any goal is confirmed\\n\\nThe approved issue below is this run's brief, unchanged. Each Done-when line is one item in `checklist.json`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Primary area\\n\\ntend\\n\\n### Kind\\n\\nenhancement\\n\\n### Goal\\n\\nThe operator needs one place per project to keep ideas, goals and thoughts, including thoughts that are not yet goals.\\nSaving a thought must not claim that the operator confirmed a goal.\\n\\n### Evidence\\n\\nOperator words, 2026-11-15, exact:\\n\\n> i just want a place that i can saturate my ideas/goals and thoughts in that are project specific and I can allow LLM to find surfaced connections, refactoring and determination if a goal(s) are ready for shaping. thats my main goal. I think idempotency is an idea that is relevant\\n\\n> think of an entry to tend be a neuron, and with enough neurons a action potential activates over those neuron connections, and then its refined and insulated better through shape like myelination and then then the action is tink-sdlc which is like the behavior..\\n\\nAsked whether Tend must keep a raw thought before any goal is confirmed from it, the operator answered: \\\"Yes, raw thoughts\\\".\\n\\nObserved in code at `04c2bc4`:\\n\\n- Tend lists saved sessions only. Think exits without confirmation leave no record (`docs/design/think.md`, Think record).\\n- `tinkery seed new` saves any text without Think or a model call, but records it as a confirmed goal (`src/shape/command.rs`, `new_goal`).\\n- That command writes no repository attribution. One active goal with unknown attribution makes Find connections refuse\\n  for every repository (`src/tend/connection.rs`, `snapshot_with`).\\n\\nProposal, not observation: a thought is a separate kind of entry, not a goal with a weaker label.\\nNot established: that more entries make connection discovery useful. The one real trial gave a false alarm.\\n\\n### Scope and boundaries\\n\\nOne outcome: the operator saves exact words as a thought in the current repository's garden, sees it there after restart,\\nand later turns it into a goal only through the existing goal confirmation.\\n\\nChanges:\\n\\n- A key in Tend's repository garden opens one exact-word editor. No Think conversation is needed.\\n- One explicit save of exact words, with the repository shown before saving. No model call.\\n- Thoughts appear in the repository garden, visibly distinct from goals with and without colour.\\n- A thought records where it was saved. It never has unknown attribution.\\n\\nStays unchanged:\\n\\n- Goal confirmation, seed confirmation and implementation permission. A thought is none of them.\\n- Shape accepts confirmed goals only. A thought cannot open Shape.\\n- Find connections input, its 20-goal and 32 KiB bounds, the saved correction and all saved-goal formats.\\n  Thoughts do not enter a provider request in this slice.\\n- No automatic interpretation, grouping, merging, readiness judgment, wiki or background work.\\n- No editing or deletion of a saved thought in this slice.\\n\\nSubtract first: reuse the exact-word editor, the repository boundary, the private atomic write and the garden view.\\nIf the build needs a new storage service, a session-format migration or a change to the shared goal saver, stop and report.\\n\\n### Done when\\n\\n- [ ] From the repository garden, the operator saves a thought with one disclosed action, and the saved words equal the typed words byte for byte.\\n- [ ] After restart, the thought is listed in that repository's garden, labelled as a thought and not as a goal.\\n- [ ] A saved thought has no goal confirmation record, cannot open Shape, and is absent from the Find connections request.\\n- [ ] Find connections still runs in a repository that holds thoughts. A thought never causes incomplete discovery.\\n- [ ] Saving the same exact words twice in one repository results in one entry, and the screen says so.\\n- [ ] Turning a thought into a goal uses the existing goal confirmation, keeps the thought's words as the original, and leaves the thought on record.\\n- [ ] Terminal journeys at 80×24 and 100×30 with colour off cover save, restart, duplicate save, cancel and an unwritable store, with temporary TEST roots and no provider.\\n- [ ] Reversion probes fail when a thought is recorded as a confirmed goal, when saved words differ from typed words, and when a thought reaches a provider request.\\n\\n### Context and dependencies\\n\\n- [Yohaku](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/yohaku.md), Articles 1 and 2.\\n- [Tend and handoff](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/shape-and-handoff.md#tend-garden-and-detail) and [Think](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/blob/main/docs/design/think.md).\\n- #860 and #862 are merged. This slice does not change them.\\n- #852 covers pruning and restoring goals. It is related and not a blocker.\\n- Later, separate slices: thoughts as Find connections input, volume above 20 entries, refactoring proposals,\\n  readiness judgments and dismissal that persists. None is part of this issue.\\n\\n### Open decisions and permissions\\n\\nDecided by the operator on 2026-11-15: a thought enters through a key in Tend's garden. Operator words: \\\"Key in Tend garden\\\".\\nThe key name, its label and its place in the key bar remain design details for review.\\n\\nDefaults proposed by the agent and accepted by the operator for this slice on 2026-11-15:\\n\\n1. A saved thought cannot be edited or removed in this slice. Pruning is separate work (#852).\\n2. \\\"Project\\\" means the launch repository, as goals use today.\\n3. A duplicate means the same exact bytes in the same repository. Whitespace and case differences make a different thought.\\n\\nEach default can change in a later slice. No other decision is open for this slice.\\n\\nThis issue records a design request. Publication, implementation, provider budget and real-data trials each need separate operator approval.\\n\\n\"}\n==> text 5/7 <==\n{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"d50e7a87-1232-43e5-a29a-0cbf2512c1ab\\\",\\n  \\\"checkout\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought\\\",\\n  \\\"report\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/output/handoff.md\\\",\\n  \\\"model\\\": \\\"openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol:medium\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher\\\": \\\"/Users/jondev/dev/worktrees/tinkery/issue-864-tend-thought/_system/scripts/pi-launcher\\\",\\n  \\\"launcher_sha256\\\": \\\"d0c05547c00cf7882c0fef3d8e89d786fdd2a488b76413aa9159cd61183a6763\\\",\\n  \\\"state\\\": \\\"active\\\",\\n  \\\"session\\\": \\\"tsdlc-4e1822e7fd164f8b825f6555621346b6\\\",\\n  \\\"prepared_ns\\\": 1794731465469500000,\\n  \\\"max_responses\\\": 250,\\n  \\\"process_group\\\": 32154\\n}\\n\"}\n==> text 6/7 <==\n{\"output\":\" M AGENTS.md\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/03-build/\\n?? runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n7c134de stage 3 open: issue-864-tend-thought (approved artifacts and receipts)\\n04c2bc4 Merge pull request #863 from jon-devlapaz/codex/issue-862-tend-correction\\nba6606f Record independent review of Linux test repair\\ndade851 docs: record verified Linux correction test fix\\nc1728c6 test(tend): observe clear frame before large correction paste\\nissue-860-tend-connection\\nissue-862-tend-correction\\nissue-864-tend-thought\\n\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit_code\":0,\"wall_time_seconds\":0.1}\n==> text 7/7 <==\nCreated #1: Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation (pending)\nCreated #2: Implement the approved slice and regression checks (pending)\nCreated #3: Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes (pending)\n\n[Full output: /var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt (read with offset/limit)]"}],"details":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"AGENTS.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.3395000000000437},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"_system/SDLC.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":1.0922499999996944},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.6723750000001019},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.624958999998853},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","args":"{\"path\":\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json\"}","status":"ok","durationMs":0.575790999999299},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","args":"{\"command\":\"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status\",\"timeout\":30}","status":"ok","durationMs":117.500082999999},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/7","name":"todo","args":"{\"action\":\"create\",\"tasks\":[{\"subject\":\"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation\"},{\"subject\":\"Implement the approved slice and regression checks\"},{\"subject\":\"Run checks, write han...","status":"ok","durationMs":1.6686250000002474}],"fullOutputPath":"/var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-d9a144a11d99f58d.txt"},"isError":false,"durationMs":190,"timestamp":1794731482775,"nestedCalls":{"calls":[{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/1","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"AGENTS.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/2","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"_system/SDLC.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/3","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"stages/03-build/CONTEXT.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/4","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/brief.md"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/5","name":"read","status":"ok","arguments":{"path":"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/steps/build.json"},"durationMs":1},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/6","name":"bash","status":"ok","arguments":{"command":"git status --short; git branch --show-current; git log -5 --oneline; python3 _system/scripts/sdlc.py status","timeout":30},"durationMs":117},{"id":"call_4e76b0ceab1d4095aac1f92efadf45bb|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96dd7543087d188578f6cca089367/7","name":"todo","status":"ok","arguments":{"action":"create","tasks":[{"subject":"Read approved inputs and inspect existing implementation"},{"subject":"Implement the approved slice and regression checks"},{"subject":"Run checks, write handoff, and commit changes"}]},"durationMs":2}],"complete":true}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0f17a582a0ac48cda672eb3669bde610|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96ddd2e4087d1b2345b041ae76772","name":"codemode","arguments":{"code":"text(await tools.todo({action:\"update\",id:1,status:\"in_progress\",activeForm:\"reading approved inputs\"}));\ntext(await tools.bash({command:\"tink mount principle-build-the-lever --json --payload; tink mount unslop --json --payload\",timeout:30}));\ntext(await tools.read({path:\"runs/issue-864-tend-thought/checklist.json\"}));\ntext(await tools.ls({path:\"src/tend\"}));\ntext(await tools.read({path:\"docs/design/shape-and-handoff.md\"}));\ntext(await tools.read({path:\"docs/design/think.md\"}));\ntext(await tools.read({path:\"docs/design/architecture.md\"}));\n"}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","usage":{"input":21648,"output":149,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":21797,"cost":{"input":0.043296,"output":0.0014900000000000002,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.044786,"totalFromProvider":false}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794731482778,"responseId":"resp_0df587760909a640016af96ddb121087d1ac2517193419fd97","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":5104,"thinkingLevel":"medium"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0f17a582a0ac48cda672eb3669bde610|ctc_0df587760909a640016af96ddd2e4087d1b2345b041ae76772","toolName":"codemode","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 27043)\nTotal output lines: 1371\n\n==> text 1/7 <==\nUpdated #1 (pending → in_progress)\n==> text 2/7 <==\n{\"output\":\"{\\\"contract_version\\\":1,\\\"skill\\\":\\\"principle-build-the-lever\\\",\\\"target\\\":null,\\\"entrypoint\\\":\\\"/Users/jondev/.tink-library/skills/principle-build-the-lever/SKILL.md\\\",\\\"has_scripts\\\":false,\\\"has_references\\\":false,\\\"references\\\":[],\\\"scripts\\\":[],\\\"tree_digest\\\":\\\"sha256:e3bba643a08d279036ca2272ab7dbc7b809ac49e11986fcf186e9fa1576be145\\\",\\\"mounted\\\":false,\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"content\\\":\\\"---\\\\nname: principle-build-the-lever\\\\ndescription: \\\\\\\"Apply to any non-trivial work, not just bulk work: edits, migrations, analyses, checks. Build the tool that does it or proves it (codemod, script, generator, or a skill your subagents follow) instead of working by hand. The tool is the artifact a reviewer can rerun.\\\\\\\"\\\\ndisable-model-invocation: true\\\\n---\\\\n# Build the Lever\\\\n\\\\nWhen the work isn't trivial, build the tool that does it instead of doing it by hand.\\\\n\\\\n**Why:** Two payoffs. Throughput: a codemod, generator, or script does the work the same way every time and reruns for free. Confidence: the tool is one artifact a reviewer can read and rerun to check the work. Hand-done changes can only be re-verified by redoing them. A deterministic script turns \\\\\\\"trust me\\\\\\\" into \\\\\\\"run this\\\\\\\".\\\\n\\\\n**Pattern:** Default to building the lever. Skip it only when the task is trivial, a couple of obvious edits you can see at a glance.\\\\n\\\\n- Do the first unit by hand to learn the recipe, then build the tool. Prove it by rerunning it on that unit and diffing against your hand-done version. Make the lever safe to rerun.\\\\n- Codemod or script for edits, generator for repetitive files, a dump-to-sqlite query for analysis, a rerunnable check for verification.\\\\n- A deterministic lever beats fan-out. If the tool can process every unit in one pass, run it yourself. Don't fan out delegates to hand-apply what a script can do.\\\\n- When you fan work out to subagents, write the lever as a skill they all read: the recipe, the verification contract, and the do-not-touch fences in one artifact. Keep it outside the delegates' write scope so they can't quietly edit the contract.\\\\n- Applying this principle produces a file. If you cited it and there is no codemod, script, generator, or delegate skill in the diff, you didn't apply it.\\\\n- Commit the lever when the work outlives the session.\\\\n\\\\n**Balance:** The bar is triviality, not repetition. A one-off still earns a lever when the lever is what makes the work checkable. Per the [Laziness Protocol](../principle-laziness-protocol/SKILL.md), build the smallest script that does or proves the job, never a framework.\\\\n\\\\nDistinct from [Encode Lessons in Structure](../principle-encode-lessons-in-structure/SKILL.md), which makes a recurring instruction a durable guardrail. This is throughput and reviewability on the work in front of you. For scripting the verification itself, see [Prove It Works](../principle-prove-it-works/SKILL.md).\\\\n\\\",\\\"chars\\\":2458}}\\n{\\\"contract_version\\\":1,\\\"skill\\\":\\\"unslop\\\",\\\"target\\\":null,\\\"entrypoint\\\":\\\"/Users/jondev/.tink-library/skills/unslop/SKILL.md\\\",\\\"has_scripts\\\":false,\\\"has_references\\\":false,\\\"references\\\":[],\\\"scripts\\\":[],\\\"tree_digest\\\":\\\"sha256:57f29101707e4dca1128ee859d3fd8bdc0c3d31fa80a50e98258e55fe083233c\\\",\\\"mounted\\\":false,\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"content\\\":\\\"---\\\\nname: unslop\\\\ndescription: Cut AI tells from any writing. Must always apply.\\\\ndisable-model-invocation: true\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# Unslop\\\\n\\\\nEdit text to remove AI patterns.\\\\n\\\\n## Process\\\\n\\\\n1. Scan for the patterns below.\\\\n2. Rewrite. Preserve meaning, match intended tone.\\\\n3. Self-audit: \\\\\\\"What makes this obviously AI generated?\\\\\\\" Fix remaining tells.\\\\n\\\\n## Patterns to detect and fix\\\\n\\\\nRule numbers are stable ids that other skills cite. A removed rule leaves a gap.\\\\n\\\\n### Content\\\\n\\\\n3. **Superficial -ing phrases.** \\\\\\\"highlighting...\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"ensuring...\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"reflecting...\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"showcasing...\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"fostering...\\\\\\\". Delete or expand with real sources.\\\\n5. **Vague attributions.** \\\\\\\"Experts believe\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Industry reports suggest\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Some critics argue\\\\\\\". Name the source or delete.\\\\n\\\\n### Language\\\\n\\\\n7. **AI vocabulary.** Additionally, crucial, delve, enduring, enhance, fostering, garner, interplay, intricate, landscape (abstract), pivotal, showcase, tapestry (abstract), testament, underscore, vibrant. Replace with plain words.\\\\n8. **Fancy ways to say \\\\\\\"is\\\\\\\".** \\\\\\\"serves as\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"stands as\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"boasts\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"features\\\\\\\". Just say \\\\\\\"is\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"has\\\\\\\".\\\\n9. **\\\\\\\"Not just X, but Y.\\\\\\\"** State the point directly instead.\\\\n10. **Rule of three.** Forcing ideas into groups of three. Use the natural number.\\\\n11. **Synonym cycling.** Protagonist, main character, central figure, hero all in one paragraph. Pick one, repeat it.\\\\n12. **False ranges.** \\\\\\\"from X to Y\\\\\\\" where X and Y aren't on a meaningful scale. List topics directly.\\\\n\\\\n### Style\\\\n\\\\n13. **Em dash overuse.** Avoid em dashes entirely. Use periods or commas only (no parentheses, no en dashes, no hyphen-as-dash substitutes). If a thought needs separation, end the sentence or use a comma.\\\\n14. **Colon overuse.** Colons are fine before a list or example. Not as mid-sentence connectors. \\\\\\\"If you're coming from traditional automation: instead of registering event handlers, you describe conditions\\\\\\\" adds nothing with the colon. Rewrite to let the point stand on its own without comparison framing. \\\\\\\"Describing when the scheduler should fire works best as plain English.\\\\\\\" Same meaning, no crutch punctuation.\\\\n15. **Boldface overuse.** Don't bold every proper noun or acronym.\\\\n16. **Inline-header lists.** The tell is a bold label and colon that restates the line: \\\\\\\"**Performance:** Performance improved...\\\\\\\". Convert those to prose. A bold lead-in that ends in a period, names the item, and is followed by genuinely new detail (\\\\\\\"**Schema in TypeScript.** Tables live in one file.\\\\\\\") is fine, not a tell.\\\\n17. **Title case headings.** Use sentence case.\\\\n18. **Decorative emojis.** Remove from headings and bullets.\\\\n19. **Curly quotes.** Replace with straight quotes.\\\\n\\\\n### Communication artifacts\\\\n\\\\n20. **Chatbot phrases.** \\\\\\\"I hope this helps!\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Let me know if...\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Of course!\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Certainly!\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Found the smoking gun!\\\\\\\" Remove.\\\\n22. **Sycophantic tone.** \\\\\\\"Great question! You're absolutely right!\\\\\\\" Respond directly.\\\\n\\\\n### Filler\\\\n\\\\n23. **Filler phrases.** \\\\\\\"In order to\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"To\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"Due to the fact that\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"Because\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"It is important to note that\\\\\\\" gets deleted.\\\\n24. **Excessive hedging.** \\\\\\\"could potentially possibly be argued that it might\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"may\\\\\\\".\\\\n25. **Generic conclusions.** \\\\\\\"The future looks bright.\\\\\\\" State specific plans or facts.\\\\n\\\\n### Jargon\\\\n\\\\n26. **Abstract metaphor nouns.** Substrate, wedge, vector, locus, vantage, nexus, primitive (as noun), harness (as metaphor), surface (as in \\\\\\\"API surface\\\\\\\"), bedrock, scaffolding (as metaphor), modality, paradigm, gold-plating, ratchet (as metaphor), evacuate (for moving code), endgame, north star, flywheel. These read as technical but usually have a plainer concrete word. \\\\\\\"Substrate\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"base\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"Wedge in\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"add\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"Vector\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"way\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"Gold-plating\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"more than the job needs\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"Ratchet\\\\\\\" becomes the mechanism's real name or \\\\\\\"a limit that only tightens\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"Evacuate\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"move out\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"Endgame\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"the last phase\\\\\\\". Pick the concrete word.\\\\n\\\\n### Plain speech\\\\n\\\\n27. **Say what it does, not how it feels.** \\\\\\\"the database stays close at hand\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"SQL you can read\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"types that follow your schema\\\\\\\" name a feeling. The fix names the mechanism or a number: \\\\\\\"`.toSQL()` returns the exact string sent to the database\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"a column rename fails the build\\\\\\\". Ask what the sentence tells the reader to do or know, then write that. If you can't restate it as a concrete instruction, fact, or number, cut it. One more check: if the sentence could appear unchanged in another project's docs, it says nothing about this one. Cut it.\\\\n28. **Shorten or split dense sentences.** If the reader has to backtrack to parse a sentence, break it in two or drop clauses. One idea per sentence.\\\\n29. **Active voice.** Prefer it. Catch \\\\\\\"is/are/was/were + past participle\\\\\\\" and name the actor: \\\\\\\"queries are validated\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"the compiler validates queries\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"the file is parsed by the loader\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"the loader parses the file\\\\\\\". Passive is fine only when the actor is unknown or genuinely doesn't matter.\\\\n30. **Cut adverbs, or use a stronger verb.** \\\\\\\"runs quickly\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"is fast\\\\\\\" or the number. \\\\\\\"significantly improves\\\\\\\" becomes the measured delta. An adverb propping up a weak verb means the verb is wrong.\\\\n31. **Prefer the plain word.** \\\\\\\"utilize\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"use\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"leverage\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"use\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"facilitate\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"help\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"numerous\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"many\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"in the event that\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"if\\\\\\\". The fancier synonym is rarely clearer.\\\\n32. **Mannered prose.** Metaphor or flourish where a literal phrase exists: aphorisms (\\\\\\\"wire it or delete it\\\\\\\"), rhetorical fragments for effect, personified code (\\\\\\\"the plan holds it\\\\\\\"), figurative verbs (\\\\\\\"rides along\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"stands on\\\\\\\"), stock framing phrases. \\\\\\\"A dial worth turning\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"a parameter worth varying\\\\\\\". Say what you mean. Rule 26 covers the metaphor nouns.\\\\n33. **Over-compression.** Dropped articles, verbless fragments, symbol-speak, and abbreviations that make the reader decode instead of read. \\\\\\\"Parser rejects bad date → exit 2, no write\\\\\\\" becomes \\\\\\\"The parser rejects a bad date, exits with code 2, and writes nothing.\\\\\\\" Write whole sentences with their articles and verbs, and spell out arrows and abbreviations.\\\\n\\\",\\\"chars\\\":6091}}\\n\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit_code\":0,\"wall_time_seconds\":0}\n==> text 3/7 <==\n{\n  \"schema\": 1,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done-1\",\n      \"description\": \"From the repository garden, the operator saves a thought with one disclosed action, and the saved words equal the typed words byte for byte.\",\n      \"verify\": \"Show evidence on the pull request that this Done-when line holds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done-2\",\n      \"description\": \"After restart, the thought is listed in that repository's garden, labelled as a thought and not as a goal.\",\n      \"verify\": \"Show evidence on the pull request that this Done-when line holds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done-3\",\n      \"description\": \"A saved thought has no goal confirmation record, cannot open Shape, and is absent from the Find connections request.\",\n      \"verify\": \"Show evidence on the pull request that this Done-when line holds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done-4\",\n      \"description\": \"Find connections still runs in a repository that holds thoughts. A thought never causes incomplete discovery.\",\n      \"verify\": \"Show evidence on the pull request that this Done-when line holds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done-5\",\n      \"description\": \"Saving the same exact words twice in one repository results in one entry, and the screen says so.\",\n      \"verify\": \"Show evidence on the pull request that this Done-when line holds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done-6\",\n      \"description\": \"Turning a thought into a goal uses the existing goal confirmation, keeps the thought's words as the original, and leaves the thought on record.\",\n      \"verify\": \"Show evidence on the pull request that this Done-when line holds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done-7\",\n      \"description\": \"Terminal journeys at 80\\u00d724 and 100\\u00d730 with colour off cover save, restart, duplicate save, cancel and an unwritable store, with temporary TEST roots and no provider.\",\n      \"verify\": \"Show evidence on the pull request that this Done-when line holds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done-8\",\n      \"description\": \"Reversion probes fail when a thought is recorded as a confirmed goal, when saved words differ from typed words, and when a thought reaches a provider request.\",\n      \"verify\": \"Show evidence on the pull request that this Done-when line holds.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n==> text 4/7 <==\nconnection/\nconnection-prompt.txt\nconnection.rs\ndiscovery/\ndiscovery.rs\ngarden/\ngarden.rs\ngraph/\ngraph.rs\nhistory/\nhistory.rs\nlinking/\nlinking.rs\nmod.rs\nneeds.rs\nscope_tests.rs\n==> text 5/7 <==\n# Tend and handoff\n\nTend holds saved goals; [Shape](#shape) asks its questions with an explicit\nmodel and budget.\n\n## Receipt\n\nExplicit successful saving shows `Saved.`, the exact frozen goal block and\n`Next: shape it into a seed in Shape.`\n\n- `s open in tend` opens this session's detail, not the garden.\n- Esc returns to the menu, including from standalone Think.\n\nNothing launches, opens a browser, pastes into a harness or starts implementation.\nPaths belong in F1 then `h`, with the [think record](think.md#think-record) below\nthe session path. If recording fails, the goal stays saved; help and a muted receipt\nnotice disclose the gap. If only reading context was rejected, the successful receipt says\n`Reading sequence wasn't saved; F1 then h shows why.` The path and full reason remain in\ntechnical details and the plain-text exit receipt. Notices wrap without clipping in receipt and detail.\nThe plain-text exit receipt may include recovery locations. Its next step is read\nfrom the session at exit, through the shared verifier: a verified confirmed seed\npoints to Approve, a draft to confirming in Shape, and a goal without a seed to\nshaping. An unverifiable or unreadable session points to checking in Shape, never\nclaims readiness; other ended sessions are named as ended.\n\n## Tend: garden and detail\n\nInside a repository, Tend starts with only active goals whose verified creation\norigin names that exact repository root. A muted `this repo · a all` line offers\n`a` to show every active goal; `all · a this repo` toggles back. Outside a repo,\nall goals show without a toggle. Unknown, missing, older or conflicting records\nnever guess a repo; those goals remain under all. An empty repo view still offers\n`a`. If any active goal lacks attributable repo information, say `N goals have no\nreadable repo record. Press a for all goals.`, not that this repo has no goals.\nExplicit no-repo records are known non-matches; older records without repo\ninformation are unknown. Only with no unknown active goals or unreadable sessions\nmay an empty repo view say `No active goals are recorded for this repo.` With\nunreadable sessions, say `N saved records couldn't be read; this repo may have\nactive goals. Press a for all goals.` Ended counts remain visible. Reopening Tend\nstarts with this repo again; explicit receipt or Shape returns still open the\nexact saved goal, switching to all if necessary.\n\nFiltering reads existing records on discovery's worker, never during painting,\nand writes nothing. Legacy discovery enumerates the Think record root once and\nreads each JSON record at most once, rebuilding an in-memory attribution index;\nrefreshes never reuse an earlier index. It shares history's strict validation and\nrechecks session identity and goal across the scan. Malformed or unsupported files\nmake every origin unknown; duplicate paths and matching schema-1 records refuse\nattribution, never choose a winner. The index keeps paths, identities, exact goals\nand origins, not turns or checks; the 16 MiB per-file limit remains, with no total\nindex-size cap. Links, identity checks and readiness still use every readable\nsession, including other repos and ended goals. Use all to link across repos;\n`a` does nothing during linking, detail, history, help or pending/failed reads.\n\nTend displays the visible goals in one static graph, without paging or node cap.\nEqual hollow Braille rings mean size is unknown; no sizes or merges are known.\nNeeds lines join only active, uniquely identified endpoints. Missing, ambiguous or\nended endpoints draw no line; the selected-line summary still names them. Straight\nlines leave gaps around unrelated rings, never implied junctions or routed paths.\nExisting sessions stay read-only; linking writes only Tend's store.\n\nEnded sessions are omitted. `N ended not shown` and\n`Links couldn't be read. F1 then h for details.` share the muted incomplete-read\nrow, joined by ` · `, even with no active goals, and survive navigation. An\nunreadable-links refusal replaces, rather than repeats, the note. When an empty\nview's body reports unreadable sessions, the footer keeps ended and link notes\nwithout repeating that count. This applies to repo and all-goals views, including\noutside a repository. An incomplete empty all-goals view says `N saved records\ncouldn't be read, so nothing can be listed. Esc goes back.`, never `No active goals`.\nOnly when every session was readable and ended, say `No active goals. N ended not\nshown. Confirm a goal in think to start one.`, not that no sessions exist.\n\nLabels use the first saved-goal line, including unstructured goals. They sit above\nor below rings, clipped with `…` or omitted rather than overlapping or interleaving.\nThe selected label gets space first and always appears, prefixed `› `; its label\nand ring are bold, jade with colour. Other labels are muted and rings use ink;\nnone is italic.\n\nThe selected-line footer shows the first goal line in ink, status and relative time\nmuted, then needs and readiness, e.g. `needs Local records · ready`.\nValid offsets determine age and order. Timestamp syntax stays unchanged; invalid\ndates and pre-epoch values remain `time unknown`.\nUnresolved ids say `1 need not found` or `N needs not found`; no needs adds nothing.\nShort summaries retain status and time. If these crowd the headline, show the\nheadline, readiness and `F1 needs`; scrollable Help retains full titles. Ambiguous source\nidentity says `readiness unknown` and attributes no needs. Refusals use the blank\nrow before the key bar.\n\n| Input | Action |\n| --- | --- |\n| Right, Down or `j`; Left, Up or `k` | Select next or previous in discovery's newest-first order, clamped; not graph neighbours. |\n| Enter | Open detail. |\n| `+` or `=`; `-` | Zoom in; zoom out. |\n| `H J K L` | Pan left, down, up or right. |\n| Left press on a ring or label | Select its exact session path; a second press within 500 ms opens it. |\n| Drag a ring or label; drag empty space; scroll | Move the goal; pan; zoom. |\n\nThe key bar is `↑↓←→ move   enter open   l link   esc back` (fits 80 columns).\nSelection pans offscreen goals into view without resetting zoom. Help, detail,\nhistory, reading, empty and unavailable states ignore graph input.\n\nGraf uses deterministic identity-ordered slots adapted to the canvas, initially\nat least 12 columns × 6 rows apart, without a physics thread. Crowded gardens\nrequire navigation, panning or zooming, not simultaneous labels. Moving, panning\nand zooming write nothing. Refresh preserves positions and viewport for an unchanged\ngraph. Changed nodes, labels or links rebuild slots and reset manual positions but\nkeep zoom and pan. Resize reflows slots with relative zoom and pan retained.\nFollowing selection may pan again. Session reads, scope changes and needs-link reads or\nedits prepare the path-bound nodes and links; unchanged repaints reuse those inputs.\nThe adapter rebuilds Graf’s cached node colors and labels only for new graph data,\nchanged ink/jade colors or a changed marked source. Geometry still updates each frame.\n\nDetail uses the receipt's shared renderer for the exact stored goal, without\n`Saved.`. ↑↓, `j k`, PgUp or PgDn scroll (a page moves the view less one row); `s` opens Shape on permitted stages, and `r` opens history. Esc goes detail → garden →\nmenu; F10 goes to the menu. F1 opens help; `h` opens technical details. Returning from Shape\n…17043 tokens truncated… including CRLF, CR, tabs and controls.\nCopy uses base64 OSC 52, not executable source. The host owns clipboard support and\npermission. LF starts rows; CR displays as ␍, tab as ⇥, ASCII controls as control\npictures, and C1 as �. CRLF is one grapheme. Display-only marks map cells to raw\ngrapheme offsets for motion, selection, highlighting and voice preview, never\nsubstring reconstruction. Rows use machine-sized integers; only the visible viewport\nnarrows to terminal coordinates.\n\nLiteral ESC `[201~` ends bracketed paste and cannot occur inside its envelope.\nCrossterm replaces invalid UTF-8 before Paste delivery. Retain received strings,\nnot unavailable predecode bytes. Valid-UTF-8 complete-envelope tests include OSC\nsentinels: bytes survive sending and copying, never execute on display.\n\n### Sending and leaving\n\n- The encoded request limit for Think and Shape is 128 KiB (`pi::REQUEST_LIMIT`), including\n  metadata, history and the waiting reply's refreshed reading. It was 32 KiB, about 8K\n  tokens, until a long goal's Shape step was refused at 59,795 bytes (#593). 128 KiB is about\n  32K tokens, well inside current context windows and at the low end of where long-context\n  quality is reported to fall off. Replies stay capped at 32 KiB (`pi::LIMIT`). One shared preview measures the final payload\n  as it grows or shrinks. Idle frames reuse it until input, extraction, voice or\n  a reply arrives. Unapplicable replies make size unknown; F2 preserves the old\n  reading and sends one retry. Transactional preflight refusal starts no host and\n  commits neither words nor the waiting reply. Copy a fitting replacement with\n  Ctrl-A then Ctrl-C, deliberately replace it and send once. If retained context\n  alone exceeds the limit, new words cannot fix it. F1 explains copying local words\n  and originals using Tab, Ctrl-PgUp or Ctrl-PgDn, then Ctrl-A and Ctrl-C.\n  Copying never prunes history or restarts.\n- Distinguish provider input or context rejection, rate limits, timeout and overflow.\n  Shaping failure preserves committed originals and the prior reading. Failed,\n  unreadable or oversized review is best-effort: keep valid shaping and explain the\n  skipped review in Why. Never automatically retry, chunk, summarize or send.\n- With nothing to send or retry, F2 says “Write something, then F2 sends it.” on the\n  initial or refining screen, without requesting or saving. The next input clears\n  it; there is no permanent warning.\n- One `refuse` route explains ineffective keys and recovery under writing or in the\n  review's confirm slot. It takes at most two narrow-pane rows and overrides\n  Thinking and receipts. The next key or paste clears display; Last result retains\n  it. Review names unsent words, a waiting reply or an in-progress reading.\n  Enter before the reading's end says PgDn. Confirmation saves only from the end.\n- An unfinished save says “Handoff not complete. F1 then h shows why; Enter retries.”\n  Last result retains the reason. Review messages grow, leaving Back its own row.\n- Idle Esc guards unsaved work. `--home` returns to the menu; standalone\n  exits before saving, opens the menu after saving. Esc first cancels voice or the\n  reading, closes overlays, or leaves review. In the original, Esc clears a\n  selection first; with no selection it returns to writing, preserving the draft.\n  The next Esc asks before discarding; Esc at that prompt declines.\n- Originals and Why are F1 rows. [Local voice](voice.md) inserts editable\n  words, never sends. Answering keeps the original dump visible.\n\n## Published proposals\n\nPublished proposal scope and current permission live in the linked GitHub issues:\n[multiple-choice answer research](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/426)\nand [evidence on demand](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/428).\nNeither issue authorizes implementation.\n\n## Decision-review evidence (#34)\n\nSee [historical evidence](../../findings/README.md#decision-review-evidence-34).\n\n## Evidence-note regression check (#58)\n\nSee [historical evidence](../../findings/README.md#evidence-note-regression-check-58).\n\n## Goal-history and review regression check (#64)\n\nSee [historical evidence](../../findings/README.md#goal-history-and-review-regression-check-64).\n==> text 7/7 <==\n# Architecture\n\nRead [the constitution](../yohaku.md) first. This page owns module responsibilities,\ndependency rules and the design score. [Architecture tests](../../tests/architecture.rs)\nenforce the limits below; a limit may only get stricter.\n\n## Phases and the dependency rule\n\nEach built [place](menu.md) is a **phase**, with its own compartment:\n\n- A phase uses only shared code, never another phase.\n- Only the menu knows every phase.\n- Shared code names no phase.\n\nEach top-level module belongs to one area, listed in `MODULES` in the check. A new module must be listed\nthere, so a new phase can't count as shared code by default.\n\nPublished task scope and status live in GitHub Issues. Measure's former\n[failure presentation](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/119) and\n[owned reader](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/issues/120) are retired.\n`MODULES` owns area assignments; the map below describes responsibilities.\n\n[Shape](shape-and-handoff.md#shape), [Approve](approve.md), [Watch](watch.md) and\n[Taste](taste.md) have separate modules. There are no dependency exceptions: Think hands\nsaved goals to Tend only through `seed_me`. The menu alone connects Shape and Tend.\n\n## Factory boundaries\n\n[Factory principles](https://x.com/zachlloydtweets/status/2110505938604134737) are\ncontext, not requirements or permission. Tinkery versions prompts, model boundaries,\ntests and CI; external SDLC owns implementation orchestration. Tinkery neither\nconfigures nor verifies whole-factory permissions, automations or sandboxes.\n\nPi uses an explicitly selected provider and model. [Manual continuation](shape-and-handoff.md#continuation-contract)\nin Claude, Codex or Pi is not interchangeable execution inside Tinkery. Model\nselection establishes neither open-weight compatibility nor comparative quality.\n\n[Think records](think.md#think-record) retain confirmed-session evidence, not\nunconfirmed sessions or complete factory telemetry. The read-only\n[seed-to-run join](shape-and-handoff.md#sdlc-handoff) uses SDLC's source-owned record.\n\n## Modules today\n\n| Module | Owns |\n| --- | --- |\n| `main.rs`, `main/input.rs` | Command-line options, the terminal loop and bounded ordered input. Mouse input after resize waits for a source-level drain acknowledgement for that resize, never an empty transfer queue. Input and read completions wake the UI without shortening its heartbeat. A Unix hangup-only watchdog reports terminal loss and detaches a trapped reader; normal shutdown joins input workers. Wires the menu or think to a real or practice host. |\n| `lib.rs` | The palette (one job per colour) in light or dark, the header's light/dark switch and the model label's room, the minimum terminal size, paging, grapheme-safe list titles and the clipped-view `more` cue. |\n| `calendar.rs` | Converts epoch days to the calendar date used by Think records and ledger writers. Each writer owns its timestamp format. |\n| `model_setup.rs`, `model_setup/panel.rs` | The saved model setup's one parser (file and flags), atomic save, header label and Model panel; the terminal loop owns when it shows (shared). |\n| `pi.rs` | The one place a model host starts, Pi or Claude Code: which models and levels each accepts, a bounded, cancellable request with or without read-only tools held inside one folder ([`pi/folder.mjs`](../../pi/folder.mjs) for Pi, `dontAsk` for Claude Code), the call budget and prompt-file assembly (shared). |\n| `repo.rs` | Finding the Git repository Tinkery was started in, by looking for `.git` only (shared). |\n| `menu.rs`, `menu/worker.rs`, `menu/retry.rs` | Navigation through the seven place variants, plus the explicit worker-action screen and its retained finite request, and the hand-off of one reviewed Taste rejection to a saved Shape attempt. |\n| `menu/place.rs` | The seven-place enum, display names and purposes, and ordered navigation list; no routing or actions. |\n| `measure.rs` | Recent PR list, details and evidence limits; explicit reads only, no writes or providers. |\n| `measure/read.rs` | Bounded, cancellable `gh` reads scoped to the launch repo; no shell or retries. |\n| `measure/runs.rs`, `measure/runs/records.rs` | Closed-run postmortems: one explicit, cancellable read of tink-sdlc status, the run's labels, its steps' Pi launch logs and Git, then list and details. Unknown is never zero; no writes or providers. |\n| `tend/mod.rs` | The saved-goal garden's discovery states, selection and detail, with navigation to Shape and saved Think history. |\n| `tend/connection.rs` | One bounded explicit request, latest exploration, source freshness and receipt coordination. |\n| `tend/connection/panel.rs` | Source and guidance review, explicit discovery, goal save and correction edits. |\n| `tend/connection/correction.rs` | One repository correction, bounded reads and stale-view-safe explicit replacement. |\n| `seed_me/connection.rs` | Strict v1/v2 Tend creation evidence and exact correction words shared by history, attribution and saving. |\n| `seed_me/connection_save.rs` | Frozen confirmation, owned staging and exclusive publication to one destination. |\n| `tend/history.rs` | One owned history read at a time and a read-only conversation view with separate technical help. |\n| `tend/linking.rs` | The source and target selection state, link refusals and derived needs wording, independent of garden drawing. |\n| `tend/garden.rs` | The graph's framing: selected-line needs, read notes and explicit refusals. |\n| `tend/graph.rs` | Graf adapter: path-keyed topology, deterministic layout, palette, labels, viewport and pointer geometry; no physics worker or writes. |\n| `tend/discovery.rs` | Scanning for sessions off the paint path, with unknown, complete, incomplete and failed kept apart. |\n| `tend::needs` | Tinkery-owned needs links, strict atomic persistence and readiness derived from readable session identities; never writes session files. |\n| `seed_me/` | Session formats, goal saving and the one-time root migration; `answering.rs` adapts Shape actions, typed facts and operator review reasons to the shared ledger without duplicating its fact rules. Goal initialization uses the Rust ledger; exit receipts retain data until printing, when the shared verifier supplies the current next step. Contract guidance is bundled, and copied intake commands name this Tinkery executable (shared). |\n| `seed_me/ledger` | Validation, transitions, explicit answer actions and cascading reopen, confirmation risks and completion-review coverage, byte-bound teach-back records, seed decisions and checks, confirmation receipts, verification and ending; shared writer lock and atomic saves. No phase policy, viewer or network connection. |\n| `seed_me/shaping.rs` | Classifying ledger-owned status and review flags for display, with optional Tinkery-executable intake text; no subprocess or mutation. |\n| `approve.rs` | One verified seed at a time, read-only commands, a retained verifier and navigation to worker-start review; no execution on navigation or copying. |\n| `seed_me/retry.rs` | A new attempt's starting checkout and commit, pinned in the goal's evidence; carries the prior goal unchanged and its decisions as assumptions. Only the operator's save creates the session. |\n| `seed_me/ready.rs` | Consistent verified goal/acceptance snapshots and typed intake shared by copying and explicit execution; no mutation. |\n| `worker.rs`, `worker/orientation.rs` | Bounded intake, stage and worker actions through SDLC, including whether a failed start permits retry or requires inspection. Recent Pi launch logs inform the operator’s cap choice. Only the explicitly configured allowance can reach the worker. |\n| `shape.rs` | Goals awaiting seeds; each session's confirmed goal, stage, folder, words, draft reading and next step. Reads and steps stay off the paint path. |\n| `shape/forms.rs` | Unsaved deferral and replacement-answer fields, navigation and cancellation; no validation or publication policy. |\n| `shape/words.rs` | Shape's in-memory textarea adapter: editing, selection, cursor-following and original line-ending provenance; no save or agent policy. |\n| `shape/command.rs` | Shape without a screen: saving a goal's exact words, showing where a seed stands as JSON and taking one explicit step; it builds the same work the screen does and adds no ledger rule. |\n| `shape/step.rs`, `shape/prompt.txt` | The step's budget check, folder, model input, prompt, reply decoding and recording through the shared session boundary. Replies contain findings with a question or named seed prose fields; `seed_me` alone owns their layout and validation. |\n| `goal/mod.rs` | Goal parts, constraint normalisation and composing the goal block (shared). |\n| `goal/confirmed.rs` | Rendering a confirmed goal the same way in review, receipt and tend detail (shared). |\n| `think/brain_dump.rs` | Think's `BrainDump`, which routes keys, mouse and ticks to its four parts, paints them, and reports one `Exit` to the menu. |\n| `think/brain_dump/conversation.rs` | Words, extracts, readings, question rules, skips, scope, unresolved review questions and notes, requests and ordered record turns. Fields are private; methods hide these rules. It knows nothing about screens or jobs. |\n| `think/brain_dump/reading.rs` | The model request, prompt, wire reply and lenient decoder, including open choices linked to questions. |\n| `think/brain_dump/suggestion.rs` | Optional proposal decoding and copying into an empty draft; never submission or model context. |\n| `think/brain_dump/review.rs` | The decision review: its prompt, what it sees, and how its decisions join the reading's. |\n| `think/brain_dump/prompts/` | The two prompts as text, one rule per line, with a note naming the finding behind each tuned rule. Notes never reach the model. |\n| `think/brain_dump/host.rs` | Pi shaping then review, or practice simulation. Stateful hosts start separate histories for each Think visit. |\n| `think/brain_dump/board.rs` | A verified reading; `Board` exists only after verification. |\n| `think/brain_dump/checks.rs` | The per-send record shown in \"Why\", from F1. |\n| `think/brain_dump/goal_view.rs`, `intact_view.rs`, `voice_input.rs`, `layout.rs` | The review screen, original pane, dictation and screen layout, including help, key bar and notices. |\n| `think/brain_dump/help_view.rs` | Think's bounded help overlay: visual hierarchy, scrolling body, fixed navigation and the boundary against hidden actions. |\n| `think/drafting.rs` | Think's model host: its harness and model settings and bundled working-draft instructions, over the shared `pi.rs` boundary. |\n| `think_record.rs`, `think_record/confirmation.rs`, `think_record/read.rs` | Confirmed-session format, caller-declared context, the shared authorization-to-actor boundary, exact-session attribution, one record scan shared by history and origin lookup, formatted history and typed submissions for Shape; no phase behavior. |\n| `think/canvas.rs`, `note.rs` | Extracted cards, and the raw text box with safe display, grapheme-offset mapping and machine-sized wrapped rows. Editor storage does not own send policy. |\n| `voice/` | Local dictation: the host boundary, helper process, protocol and owned setup. |\n| `watch.rs`, `watch/state.rs`, `watch/steps.rs` | The runs in one named tink-sdlc checkout, rendered from the shared typed report: waiting runs first, one run's detail and worker attention. No API-word recognition. |\n| `sdlc.rs`, `sdlc/run.rs`, `sdlc/source.rs` | Shared read-only API 1 status with one `Run::parse` word boundary and typed verification, receipt results, validated pending judgment IDs, gates, checklist methods/results and worker steps. Unknown, missing and null values remain distinct for operation-specific refusals. Checklist attestation obligations (missing ownership fails closed), the canonical lowercase UUIDv4 run-ID rule, exact original-seed matching, the check that a checkout's files match its checked commit, cancellable transactions and bounded command ownership; no store, checkout discovery or phase policy. |\n| `taste.rs`, `taste/read.rs` | One result's unchanged goal, revision-bound observations, the one question and external copy handoff; explicitly mapped PRs, read-only status/GitHub reads and deliberate browser navigation, never an approval, merge or agent launch. |\n| `taste/retry.rs` | The latest rejection's words and checked commit, a read-only review, and on F2 a fresh session with a detached checkout; never a worker or commit. |\n| `taste/preview.rs` | Exact-head repo preview declarations, local HTTP readiness and explicitly launched temporary checkout/process ownership; no inferred commands, public URLs, agent or verdict. |\n| `taste/next.rs` | Deterministic post-acceptance pause and next-dump proposal from snapshot evidence; no model, verdict, structural diagnosis or permission. The menu alone passes its unsent draft to Think. |\n| `taste/label.rs` | Binding and reading the operator's pass/fail verdict for one exact head, including stale legacy records and latest rejection words; its one write through the checkout's `sdlc.py label`, only on F2, words unchanged, signed with the name shown; never an approval, merge or agent. |\n| `taste/view.rs` | Title and goal excerpts with result counts, a one-question review, palette-bound headings and statuses, and details on request with verbatim hanging check wraps and evidence; no source reads, gate derivation or copy formatting. |\n| `taste/links.rs`, `taste/evidence.rs` | The run's own recorded PR (API 1 `pr`), unknown when absent or malformed; source-owned log pointers and check methods as evidence references, never prose parsing, a mapping store, log reads or check execution. |\n\nVendored libraries are path dependencies, not phases: `vendor/pinstar/` owns\nrendering and input primitives and `vendor/graf/` owns graph mechanics. Tend reaches graf\nonly through `tend/graph.rs`; linking and persistence do not depend on it. Graf knows\nno Tinkery phase or Seed Me format. Each library's `UPSTREAM` records its source and\nlocal edits; [Pinstar API](../../vendor/pinstar/API.md) and\n[Graf API](../../vendor/graf/API.md) describe their boundaries.\n\nDesign reasons remain with [theme ownership](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/pull/430),\n[presentation receipts](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/pull/451),\n[history sequence](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/pull/458) and\n[typed history](https://github.com/jon-devlapaz/tinkery/pull/462).\nFor Shape's typed context, read [Think history](shape-and-handoff.md#think-history).\n\n## Design score\n\nJohn Ousterhout's *A Philosophy of Software Design* supplies these eight questions.\nStructural PRs name the rows they move.\n\n| # | Question |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Can each module be described in one sentence? |\n| 2 | Are interfaces simpler than implementations? |\n| 3 | Can an implementation change without touching callers? |\n| 4 | Do interface comments describe the abstraction? |\n| 5 | Is design discussion part of review? |\n| 6 | Does each module hide an important decision? |\n| 7 | Can a newcomer see the boundaries without reading code? |\n| 8 | Is design improvement part of ordinary work? |\n\n## Limits enforced by `tests/architecture.rs`\n\n| Limit | Today |\n| --- | --- |\n| Phase reaches across phases | none allowed |\n| `BrainDump` fields / `pub` fields | 13 / 0 |\n| Long non-test source lines | See `LONG_LINE` and `LONG_LINES` in [the owning check](../../tests/architecture.rs) |\n| Test calls to `Conversation`'s test-only accessors | 77 |\n| Library files without a `//!` comment, `pub` items without a doc comment | none allowed |\n\nChecks fail whenever code differs from a limit, even by improving it. Lower improved\nlimits in the same PR.\n\n\n[Full output: /var/folders/sk/r2ns7lvn2ygcsj7bhd0mvnyw0000gn/T/pi-codemode-c4d0fbe89953f0f2.txt (read with offset/limit)]"}],"details":{"calls"